hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"8401","8401 - And with the same chain: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), spoke to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning women's hems when he forbade dragging the garment; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let them lower a handspan. She said: It will be uncovered. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then a forearm's length, no more than that. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Ajlan except Ibn Lahi'ah and Abd Allah ibn Abi Musa; Muhammad ibn Rumh is alone in narrating it from Ibn Lahi'ah, and Abd al-Hamid ibn Bakkar is alone in narrating it from Abd Allah ibn Abi Musa."
"8402","8402 - And with the same chain: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Iyad ibn Abd Allah, that he heard Ibrahim ibn Ubayd ibn Rifa'ah reporting from his mother, from Asma bint Umays, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Aishah while her sister Asma was with her wearing Syrian garments with wide sleeves; when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at her, he stood and went out. Aishah said to her: Move aside, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has seen something he disliked. So she moved aside, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered; Aishah asked him: Why did you stand? He said: Have you not seen her appearance? It is not for a Muslim woman that anything appear of her except thus - and he took his two sleeves and covered his hands with them until nothing of his hands appeared except his fingers, and raised his hands to his temples until nothing appeared except his face. This hadith is not narrated from Asma bint Umays except with this chain; Ibn Lahi'ah is alone in narrating it."
"8403","8403 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Kathir ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Rudayh ibn Madarah ibn Ali al-Sulami, of the people of Quba, from his father, from his grandfather who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until we stopped at al-Qahah where there was no water; he stopped at the head of the valley, dug with his hand in the sand, and it became moist; he sat and scooped, and water gushed forth; he drank and gave drink to those with him. He said: This is a watering with which Allah has watered you - so it was named al-Suqya. This hadith is not narrated from Ali al-Sulami except with this chain; Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari is alone in narrating it."
"8404","8404 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad al-San'ani narrated to us, Rashid ibn Dawud narrated to us: Abu Asma al-Rahabi narrated to us, Thawban narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man in Baqi' al-Gharqad on the eighteenth of Ramadan while he was being cupped, and said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken fast. No one narrated this hadith from Rashid ibn Dawud except Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad and Ismail ibn Ayyash."
"8405","8405 - Musa narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb ibn Shabur narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Qilabah al-Jarmi Abd Allah ibn Zayd, from Abu Asma al-Rahabi, from Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earth was folded for me until I saw its easts and wests, and I was given the two treasures, the yellow and the white - meaning gold and silver; and it was said to me: Your ummah's dominion shall reach where it was folded for you. I asked Allah, mighty and majestic, three things: not to set hunger upon my ummah so as to destroy them thereby altogether, not to set an enemy upon them so as to destroy them, and not to clothe them in factions causing them to taste one another's violence. It was said to me: When I decree a decree, there is no repelling it; I shall not set hunger upon your ummah to destroy them, nor set an enemy upon them to destroy them even if all from its horizons gathered against them, until some of them destroy others and kill others. Among what I fear for my ummah are misleading leaders; when the sword is placed among them it shall not be lifted until the Day of Resurrection; tribes of my ummah shall worship idols, and tribes of my ummah shall join the polytheists; before the Hour there shall be lying impostors near thirty in number, each claiming to be a prophet, and there is no prophet after me; and a party of my ummah shall ever remain upon the truth victorious, unharmed by those who oppose them until the command of Allah comes. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Sa'id ibn Bashir; Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb is alone in narrating it."
"8406","8406 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, from Ibn Abbas who said: Abu Jahl said: If Muhammad returns to pray toward the qiblah, I shall surely kill him. So he returned, and Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: Read in the name of your Lord who created, up to His words: So let him call his council; We shall call the guards. When it was said to Abu Jahl that he had returned, he said: Something intervened between me and him. Ibn Abbas said: By Allah, had he moved, the angels would have seized him while people watched. No one narrated this hadith from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth except Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq."
"8407","8407 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Hajwah ibn Mudrik narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulaym, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The neighbor has most right to his neighbor's pre-emption; it is awaited for him even if he is absent, when their road is one. No one narrated this hadith from Hajwah except Hisham ibn Ammar."
"8408","8408 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, Humayd ibn Abi Suwayd narrated to us, saying: I heard a man asking Ata ibn Abi Rabah about the Yemeni corner while he was circumambulating the House; Ata said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seventy are entrusted with it: whoever says: O Allah, I ask You for pardon, well-being, and complete well-being in this world and the Hereafter; Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield us from the punishment of the Fire - they say: Amen. When he reached the Black Corner he said: O Abu Muhammad, what reached you concerning this Black Corner? Ata said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me that he heard the Beloved (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever circumambulates the House seven times speaking only Subhan Allah, La ilaha illa Allah, Allahu Akbar, al-hamdu lillah, and La hawla wa la quwwata illa billah - ten sins are erased from him, ten good deeds written for him, and ten ranks raised for him thereby; and whoever circumambulates speaking while in that state wades through mercy with his feet as he wades through water with his feet. No one narrated this hadith from Ata except Humayd ibn Abi Suwayd; Ismail ibn Ayyash is alone in narrating it."
"8409","8409 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Rabi'ah al-Kilabi narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Waqid, from Muslim ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah shall not look on the Day of Resurrection at the old man who commits zina, nor at the old woman who commits zina. No one narrated this hadith from Muslim ibn Yasar except Uthman ibn Waqid; Muhammad ibn Rabi'ah is alone in narrating it."
"8410","8410 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, Qaza'ah ibn Suwayd narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: From the excellence of a man's Islam is his leaving what does not concern him. No one narrated this hadith from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar except Qaza'ah ibn Suwayd."
"8411","8403 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Qarqasani narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he was junub and wanted to eat or sleep would perform wudu. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Shubah, nor from Shubah except Hajjaj; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Qarqasani alone transmitted it."
"8412","8404 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Suhayl ibn Ibrahim al-Jarudi narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn Isa al-Raqashi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah who said: They said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what is ill omen? He said: Evil character. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir except al-Fadl ibn Isa, and it is not narrated from Jabir except with this chain."
"8413","8405 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The accepted Hajj has no reward except Paradise. He said: And what is its righteousness? He said: Feeding food, and good speech. No one narrated this hadith from Amr ibn Dinar except Muhammad ibn Muslim, nor from Muhammad except Bishr ibn al-Mundhir; Ibrahim ibn Said alone transmitted it."
"8414","8406 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, Dawud ibn Mansur narrated to us, Qays narrated to us, from Ghaylan ibn Jami and Ibn Abi Layla and Jabir, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed at Jam the Maghrib and Isha, three and two with one iqamah. No one narrated this hadith from Ghaylan except Qays; Dawud ibn Mansur alone transmitted it. And Dawud ibn Mansur opposed the people in the chain of this hadith, for Sufyan al-Thawri narrated it from Jabir, and more than one narrated it from Ibn Abi Layla, and Malik ibn Anas and a group narrated it from Yahya ibn Said al-Ansari, all of them: from Adi ibn Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari."
"8415","8407 - Musa ibn Sahl narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Yahya Abu al-Khattab narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, Simak ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas who said: While Abu Bakr al-Siddiq was eating with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), there was revealed to him: So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it. So Abu Bakr raised his hand and said: O Messenger of Allah, will I indeed see what I did of an atom's weight of evil? He said: O Abu Bakr, have you seen what you see in this world of what you dislike, then it is in place of the atoms of evil, and the atoms of good are stored for you until you are given them in full on the Day of Resurrection. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Simak ibn Atiyyah, nor from Simak except al-Haytham; Ziyad ibn Yahya alone transmitted it."
"8416","8408 - Musa ibn Isa al-Jazari al-Basri narrated to us, Suhayb ibn Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Suhayb narrated to us, Abbad ibn Suhayb narrated to us, Said ibn al-Marzuban Abu Sad al-Baqqal narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father who said: We passed by Abu Dharr at al-Rabadhah, and I asked him about mutah in Hajj. He said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while we were declaring ihram for Hajj, and when we arrived at Makkah he ordered us and we dissolved ihram and we had relations with women, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not dissolve because he had driven the sacrificial animals, then he said: It shall not be for anyone after you. No one narrated this hadith in its fullness from Abu Sad except Abbad ibn Suhayb; Suhayb alone transmitted it."
"8417","8409 - And with the same chain: Abbad ibn Suhayb narrated to us, Kharijah ibn Musab informed me, Ibn Rufay narrated to me, Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to me, he said: Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me one day and said: O Aishah, today I did something I wish I had not done; I entered the House and I wish I had not entered it, for there will come one from my ummah who will see that it is a duty upon him to enter the House, and I fear I may have made it hard upon my ummah. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay except Kharijah ibn Musab; Abbad ibn Suhayb alone transmitted it."
"8418","8410 - And with the same chain: Abbad narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Dhakwan, from Sulayman ibn al-Rabi, from Ata, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Women have no reward in following funerals. No one narrated this hadith from Ata except Sulayman ibn al-Rabi; al-Hasan ibn Dhakwan alone transmitted it."
"8419","8411 - And with the same chain: Abbad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Nawwar the freedman of Quraysh narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah narrated to us, from his father, Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: I entered with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon Abu Salamah while he was dying, and when his sight became fixed staring upward, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaned toward him and closed his eyes, and his family raised their voices and he calmed them, then he said: When the soul comes out the sight follows it, and the angels attend the dead person saying amin to what the family of the dead says. Then he said: O Allah, raise the rank of Abu Salamah among the guided, and leave a successor for him among his descendants among those who remain, and forgive us and him, Lord of the worlds. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Bakrah except from the hadith of Muhammad ibn Abi al-Nawwar, and no one narrated it from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Nawwar except Abbad ibn Suhayb and Awn ibn Kahmas, and Awn did not narrate it in this fullness nor with a connected chain, and he narrated it from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Nawwar, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from Abu Bakrah, without saying from his father."
"8420","8412 - And with the same chain: Abbad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali narrated to us, Yasar Abu Azzah narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah wants to take a servant in a land, He creates a need for him there, and it does not end until he comes to it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited the end of Surah Luqman: Indeed Allah, with Him is knowledge of the Hour, until he completed it, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These are the keys of the unseen, none knows them except Allah. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Azzah except Abu al-Malih, and no one narrated it from Abu al-Malih except Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani and Ubaydullah ibn Abi Humayd, and Ayyub did not narrate it with this chain."
"8421","8413 - Musa narrated to us, Suhayb narrated to us, Abbad narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Miqsam, from Qatadah, from Sulayman al-Yashkuri, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade plastering graves, or building over them, or sitting upon them. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Uthman ibn Miqsam; Abbad ibn Suhayb alone transmitted it."
"8422","8414 - And with the same chain Abbad narrated to us, Harun ibn Ibrahim al-Ansari narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Maghrib prayer is the witr of the daytime, so make the night prayer odd. No one narrated this hadith from Harun except Abbad."
"8423","8415 - Musa ibn Isa al-Jazari narrated to us, he said: Suhayb ibn Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Suhayb narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Suhayb narrated to us, Ismail ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to order us, when we were two, to stand together in one row. No one narrated this hadith from al-Hasan except Ismail ibn Muslim; Abbad alone transmitted it."
"8424","8416 - And with the same chain, Abbad narrated to us, he said: al-Sari ibn Ismail al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Shabi narrating from Wabisah ibn Mabad who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led the people in prayer, then when he turned he looked at a man standing alone praying behind the people, and said: O you who prays alone, why did you not join the row, or take the hand of a man from the people and stand with him; for there is no prayer for you alone, so repeat your prayer. No one narrated this hadith in its fullness from al-Shabi except al-Sari ibn Ismail; Abbad alone transmitted it."
"8425","8417 - And with the same chain, Abbad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muharrar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to raise his hands at the takbir in every prayer, and at funerals. No one narrated this hadith from Nafi, with and at funerals, except Abdullah ibn Muharrar; Abbad ibn Suhayb alone transmitted it."
"8426","8418 - And with the same chain, Abbad narrated to us, from Kharijah ibn Musab, from Amr ibn Yahya, from his father, from Abu Said who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sadaqah in less than five dhawd, nor in less than five awaq is there sadaqah, nor in less than five awsuq is there sadaqah. No one narrated this hadith from Kharijah ibn Musab except Abbad."
"8427","8419 - And with the same chain, Abbad narrated to us, he said: Awf and Hisham and al-Rabi ibn Subayh and Harun and Said ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin who said: I was informed from Hakim ibn Hizam who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me to sell what is not with me. No one narrated this hadith from Harun al-Ahwazi and Said ibn Abd al-Rahman except Abbad."
"8428","8420 - And with the same chain: Abbad narrated to us, from al-Sari ibn Ismail, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best quality of the fasting person is the siwak. No one narrated this hadith from al-Shabi except Mujalid and al-Sari ibn Ismail; Abu Ismail al-Muaddib alone transmitted it from Mujalid, and Abbad from al-Sari."
"8429","8421 - And with the same chain: Abbad narrated to us, from Uthman al-Barri, from Said al-Maqburi, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a young man asked him about kissing would forbid him, and when an old man asked him he would permit him, and he said: The young man is not like the old man. No one narrated this hadith from Ata except Said al-Maqburi, nor from al-Maqburi except Uthman al-Barri; Abbad alone transmitted it."
"8430","8422 - Musa ibn Isa al-Zubaydi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abu Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj mentioned, from Mamar, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik who said: Fatimah said when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away: O my father, how near to his Lord, O my father, the Garden of Firdaws is his abode, O my father, to Jibril do we announce it. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj except Abu Qurrah."
"8431","8423 - And with the same chain: Abu Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj said: I heard my father saying: Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: Qasamah was from the practice of Jahiliyyah, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmed it so that people would restrain most from bloodshed."
"8432","8424 - And with the same chain: Abu Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj mentioned, Ibn Judan informed me, that al-Hasan narrated to him from Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I advise you with two things: Do not ask for office, for if you are given it after asking you will be left to it, and if you are given it without asking you will be helped with it; and if you swear an oath and see another better than it, then do what is better and expiate for your oath. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj except Abu Qurrah."
"8433","8425 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Shatha Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Bashshar ibn Kidam the brother of Misar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Swearing is only sin or regret."
"8434","8426 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Kathir ibn Murrah al-Hadrami, from Abdullah ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever hoards food for forty days waiting for a price rise has become free of Allah and Allah is free of him, and whichever people of a courtyard in whose club a hungry man remains overnight, the covenant of Allah is free of them. This hadith is not narrated from Ibn Umar except with this chain; Abu al-Zahiriyyah alone transmitted it."
"8435","8427 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Madan, Rabiah al-Jurashi narrated to me, he said: I asked Aishah: What would the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say when he stood to pray, and with what would he begin? She said: He would say takbir ten times, and praise ten times, and glorify ten times, and declare tawhid ten times, and seek forgiveness ten times, and say: O Allah forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, and provide for me, ten times, and say: O Allah I seek refuge in You from distress on the Day of Reckoning, ten times. No one narrated this hadith from Thawr except al-Asbagh; Yazid ibn Harun alone transmitted it, and it is not narrated from Aishah except with this chain."
"8436","8428 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Yahya ibn al-Azhar narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yazid ibn Mardanah narrated to us, he said: Raqabah ibn Masqalah al-Abdi narrated to me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Shaddad ibn al-Hakam who said: Were it not for a word I heard from Amr ibn al-Hamiq, I would have walked between the corpse of al-Mukhtar and his head. I heard Amr ibn al-Hamiq saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who gives a man security for his blood then kills him will be burdened with carrying a banner of treachery on the Day of Resurrection. No one narrated this hadith from Raqabah except Ibrahim ibn Yazid; Said ibn Yahya ibn al-Azhar alone transmitted it."
"8437","8429 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Yahya ibn al-Azhar narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, he said: My father and Ali ibn Salih narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from Muawiyah ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, from al-Bara ibn Azib who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to follow funerals, and ordered us to accept the inviter, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us from drinking in vessels of gold. No one narrated this hadith from Ali ibn Salih except Waki."
"8438","8430 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Shatha Ali ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Hayyan Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Muslim al-Mulai, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wash from janabah with a sa, and Aishah similarly. No one narrated this hadith from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, except Muslim al-Awar, and no one narrated it from Muslim except Israil and Abu Khalid al-Ahmar."
"8439","8431 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, he said: Tamim ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, he said: al-Azraq narrated to us, from Sharik, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them on the day of al-Hudaybiyyah that seven of them share in the badanah. No one narrated this hadith from Sharik except Ishaq al-Azraq."
"8440","8432 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Marwan al-Simmari narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Rashid al-Himyari narrated to us, from Israil, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas who said: Ali ibn Abi Talib had eighteen virtues; if he had but one of them it would have saved him, and indeed he had thirteen virtues that no one of this ummah had. No one narrated this hadith from Hakim ibn Jubayr except Israil, nor from Israil except Hafs ibn Rashid; Jafar ibn Marwan al-Simmari alone transmitted it."
"8441","8433 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Marwan al-Simmari narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from his father, from Abu Sadiq, from Rabiah ibn Najid who said: I heard Ali saying: I was commanded to fight the nakithin, the qasitin, and the mariqin. No one narrated this hadith from Rabiah ibn Najid except Salamah; his son alone transmitted it."
"8442","8434 - And with the same chain: Hafs ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Atiyyah, from Abu Said who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whosever master I am, Ali is his master. No one narrated this hadith from Fudayl except Hafs ibn Rashid."
"8443","8435 - Musa ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Shatha Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Qatadah, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every people has a substance, and the substance of Quraysh is their freedmen. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except al-Hajjaj ibn Artah."
"8444","8436 - Musa ibn Khazim al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bukayr al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Thabit ibn al-Walid ibn Abdillah ibn Jumay narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: Hudhayfah said: Nothing prevented us from attending Badr, I and my father Hisl who is al-Yaman, except that the disbelievers of Quraysh intercepted us while we were heading to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: You intend Muhammad. We said: We do not intend him. They said: Give us a covenant and pledge that you will not fight with him, so we gave it to them, and they let us go, then we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of the news, and he said: Fulfill their covenant for them, and seek Allah's help against them; go back to Madinah. So we went back."
"8445","8437 - And with the same chain: from Abu al-Tufayl, from Hudhayfah ibn Asid, that Abu Dharr al-Ghifari stood before Banu Ghifar and said: O Banu Ghifar, the truthful one believed (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me: The people will be gathered in three groups: a group fed, clothed, riding; a group walking and running; and a group whom the angels urge on and the Fire gathers them from behind. They said: We have known these and these, so what about those who walk and run? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He will cast affliction upon the mounts so no mount remains, until one of you will give one of you a walled garden of his for an old camel with a saddle and not find it. No one narrated these two hadiths from Thabit ibn al-Walid except Muhammad ibn Bukayr."
"8446","8438 - Musa ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bukayr al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Isa ibn Ibrahim, from al-Hakam ibn Abdillah al-Ayli, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever gives iftar to a fasting person will have a like reward, without diminishing anything from his reward. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except al-Hakam, nor from al-Hakam except Isa; Kathir ibn Hisham alone transmitted it."
"8447","8439 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Faraj ibn Fudalah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: I saw myself, and we would perfume the beard of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with ghaliyah perfume, then he would enter ihram. No one narrated this hadith from Hisham except Faraj ibn Fudalah; Muhammad ibn Bukayr alone transmitted it."
"8448","8440 - Musa narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Thabit al-Misri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None shall enter your house except a God-fearing one, and do not bestow your favor except upon a believer. No one narrated this hadith from al-Qasim except Abu al-Aswad, nor from Abu al-Aswad except Ibn Luhayah; Muhammad ibn Bukayr alone transmitted it."
"8449","8441 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Murrah, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: I am jealous, and Allah is more jealous than me, and Allah loves from His servants the jealous one. This hadith is not narrated from Ali except with this chain; Muhammad ibn Bukayr alone transmitted it."
"8450","8442 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Farwah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: A servant supplicates to Allah while He loves him, so Allah Mighty and Majestic says: O Jibril, fulfill the need of this servant of Mine, but delay it, for I love to keep hearing his voice; and a servant supplicates to Allah while He hates him, so Allah Mighty and Majestic says: O Jibril, fulfill the need of this servant of Mine, and hasten it, for I dislike hearing his voice. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir except Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Farwah; Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz alone transmitted it."
"8451","8443 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Yasin al-Zayyat, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No man is punished for a sin except that Allah makes it an expiation for what he committed of that sin."
"8452","8444 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Suwayd narrated to us, from Yasin, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever finds his property in the fay before it is divided, he is most entitled to it, and if he finds it after it is divided, he has nothing. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Yasin; Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz alone transmitted it."
"8453","8445 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Sawdah, from Maymunah the freedwoman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, give us a fatwa about Bayt al-Maqdis. He said: Go to it and pray in it. I said: And how, when between us and it are the Romans? He said: Then send oil to it to be lit in its lamps. No one narrated this hadith from Said ibn Abd al-Aziz except al-Walid."
"8454","8446 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every intoxicant is unlawful, and Allah has pledged that whoever drinks an intoxicant He will give him to drink from the clay of al-Khabal on the Day of Resurrection, the pus of the people of the Fire. No one narrated this hadith from Umarah except al-Darawardi."
"8455","8447 - Musa narrated to us, Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Nuh ibn Qays narrated to us, he said: Ashath ibn Jabir al-Huddani narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Your Lord said: Whose two precious ones I take away, then he is patient and expects reward, his reward will be Paradise. No one narrated this hadith from Ashath except Nuh ibn Qays."
"8456","8448 - Musa ibn Khazim narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ubaydillah al-Numayri narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to us, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I would make it hard upon my ummah, I would have ordered them with the siwak at every prayer. No one narrated this hadith from Ata except Said ibn Rashid."
"8457","8449 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Ata narrated to us, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Line up as the angels line up before their Lord. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how do the angels line up before their Lord? He said: They establish the rows and join their shoulders together. No one narrated this hadith from Ata except Said ibn Rashid."
"8458","8450 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Ata narrated to us, he said: I asked Aishah, did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) use oil and comb his hair? She said: Had we found oil we would have used it as food; and I saw the household of Muhammad while they were at that time nine houses, having nothing but a sa of barley; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left this world without ever being satiated with red wheat bread. No one narrated this hadith from Ata except Said ibn Rashid."
"8459","8451 - Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Fudalah ibn Ubayd and Tamim al-Dari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever recites ten verses in a night, two qintars of reward will be written for him, and the qintar is better than the world and what is in it, then when the Day of Resurrection comes your Lord will say: Recite and ascend, a degree for each verse, until he ends at the last verse with him; your Lord says to the servant: Take hold, and the servant says with his hand, saying: O Lord, You know best; He says: With this is eternity and with this is bliss. This hadith is not narrated from Fudalah ibn Ubayd and Tamim al-Dari except with this chain; Ismail ibn Ayyash alone transmitted it."
"8460","8452 - Musa ibn al-Hasan al-Kisai al-Ubulli narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, he said: Abu Umayyah ibn Yala al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muayqib, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know for whom the Fire is forbidden? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: For the gentle, the soft, the easy, the close. This hadith is not narrated from Muayqib except with this chain; Abu Umayyah ibn Yala alone transmitted it."
"8461","8453 - Musa ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas who said: We were with Umar ibn al-Khattab between Makkah and Madinah, and I was sharp-sighted, so we sighted the crescent, and no one saw it but I, so I kept saying: Yes, do you not see it, and Umar would say: I shall see it while I am lying on my bed. Then he began narrating to us about the people of Badr, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would show us the places where the people of Badr would fall the day before, saying: This is the place of such-and-such tomorrow, and this is the place of such-and-such tomorrow, Allah willing. Umar said: By the One Who sent him with the truth, they did not miss the limits set by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: They were put into a well, some upon others. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went until he stopped at them, and said: O so-and-so son of so-and-so, and O so-and-so son of so-and-so, have you found what Allah and His Messenger promised you true? For I have found what Allah promised me true. Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, how do you speak to bodies in which there are no souls? He said: You are not more hearing of what I say than them, except that they cannot return anything. This hadith is not narrated from Umar except with this chain; Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah alone transmitted it."
"8462","8454 - Musa ibn Muhammad Abu Harun al-Sirrayni narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Ibrahim al-Juddi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas who said: Bilal was ordered to make the adhan even and the iqamah odd. No one narrated this hadith from Shubah except Abd al-Malik al-Juddi."
"8463","8455 - Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Sirrayni narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Ibrahim al-Juddi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah and Amr ibn Murrah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Amir ibn Abi Umayyah, from Umm Salamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would wake junub not from a wet dream, then he would wash and fast. No one narrated this hadith from Shubah, from Amr ibn Murrah, except Abd al-Malik al-Juddi."
"8464","8456 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna ibn Muadh al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, he said: Kulayb ibn Isa ibn Abi Hudayr narrated to us, he said: I heard Zujlah, she said: I heard Salim or Nafi narrating from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah Mighty and Majestic tomorrow as a Muslim, let him guard the five prayers where they are called for. No one narrated this hadith from Zujlah the freedwoman of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan except Kulayb ibn Isa; al-Haytham ibn Kharijah alone transmitted it."
"8465","8457 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd the muadhdhin of the mosque of Sharadan narrated to us, from Udaysah bint Uhbab, she said: When Ali came to Basrah he came to the house and said: Is Abu Muslim here? We said: Yes. So he came out to him, and Ali said to him: Will you not help us in this matter? He said: Yes; O slave girl, bring me that sword. So she brought his sword, and it was a sword of wood. Abu Muslim said to him: Your cousin, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), entrusted to me that when there is fitnah among the Muslims I should take a sword of wood. So Ali turned away angry and said: We have no need of you nor of your sword. Yazid ibn Zuray said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated this hadith to me from this shaykh before I met him. No one narrated this hadith from Yunus ibn Ubayd except Yazid ibn Zuray; Muhammad ibn al-Minhal alone transmitted it."
"8466","8458 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Mukharram Abu Qatadah al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us, from Amr ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from his mother, from Umm Salamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: Indeed We have granted you al-Kawthar. This hadith is not narrated from Umm Salamah except with this chain; Amr ibn Mukharram alone transmitted it."
"8467","8459 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Mahmud ibn Amr, from Asma bint Yazid, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir except Aban; Musa ibn Ismail alone transmitted it, and it is not narrated from Asma bint Yazid except with this chain."
"8468","8460 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Khuzai narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas and Qatadah, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Hour will not come until people boast over mosques. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Hammad; Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Khuzai alone transmitted it. And the people narrated it from Hammad, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas only."
"8469","8461 - Muadh narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Beware of suspicion, for suspicion is the falsest speech; and do not spy, do not hate one another, do not envy one another, and be servants of Allah as brothers. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Tawus except Wuhayb."
"8470","8462 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Today a part of the barrier of Yajuj and Majuj has been opened like this, and he formed ninety with his fingers. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Tawus except Wuhayb."
"8471","8463 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard him while he was in a riding party, saying: By my father. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah forbids you to swear by your fathers; whoever swears, let him swear by Allah, or keep silent. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Wuhayb; Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak alone transmitted it."
"8472","8464 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Suwayd al-Dhari narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, he said: Ayyub and Yunus and Habib and Hisham and Yahya ibn Atiq informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered us to bring out the secluded young women to the two Eids. It was said: And the menstruating women? He said: They attend the good and the supplication of the Muslims. Then a woman said: O Messenger of Allah, what if one of us has no garment? He said: Her companion lends her part of her garment. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Atiq except Hammad ibn Salamah; Ibrahim ibn Abi Suwayd alone transmitted it."
"8473","8465 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak al-Ayshi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The fortunate is one who is fortunate in his mother's womb. No one narrated this hadith from Hammad ibn Zayd except Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak."
"8474","8466 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Numayr Abu Mihsan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People of two religions do not inherit from one another. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Abi Layla except Abu Mihsan."
"8475","8467 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would apply perfume before entering ihram. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj except Abd al-Aziz; Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak alone transmitted it."
"8476","8468 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque on Friday and there was Abu Israil standing in the sun, so he asked about him, and they said: He vowed to stand and not sit, nor speak, nor seek shade, nor break fast. He said: Let him sit, speak, seek shade, and not break fast. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Tawus connected except Wuhayb; Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak alone transmitted it."
"8477","8469 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Khalid narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: When Ali was killed, al-Hasan ibn Ali stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: To proceed, by Allah, tonight you have killed a man on a night in which the Quran was revealed, and in which Yusha ibn Nun the youth of Musa was killed, and in which Isa son of Maryam was raised; none before him preceded him, nor did any after him catch him; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send him on expeditions, with Jibril on his right and Mikail on his left; and by Allah he left neither gold nor silver, except seven hundred dirhams or eight hundred dirhams which he had set aside to buy a servant. No one narrated this hadith from Hafs ibn Khalid except Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz; Abd al-Rahman alone transmitted it."
"8478","8470 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Suwayd Abu Hatim narrated to us, he said: Qatadah said: from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said who said: We were discussing the Quran at the door of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one pulling with a verse and another with a verse, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out as if pomegranate seeds had been burst open upon his face, and said: O you people, for this were you sent? Or with this were you commanded? Do not return after me as disbelievers striking the necks of one another."
"8479","8471 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Suwayd Abu Hatim narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, the like of it. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Suwayd; Abd al-Rahman alone transmitted it."
"8480","8472 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us. He said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died when he was sixty-five years old. No one narrated this hadith from Humayd except Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal; al-Muthanna ibn Muadh alone transmitted it."
"8481","8473 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would give a single taslimah. None raised this hadith from Humayd except Abd al-Wahhab; al-Hajabi alone transmitted it."
"8482","8474 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aishah al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abdillah Abu Yahya narrated to us, from Amr ibn Malik, from Abu al-Jawza, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took hold of the two doorposts while we were in the house, and said: O Banu Abd al-Muttalib, is there among you anyone other than you? They said: A sister's son of ours. He said: The sister's son of a people is from them. Then he said: O Banu Abd al-Muttalib, when distress or hardship or poverty befalls you, say: Allah, Allah, our Lord, He has no partner. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Jawza except Amr ibn Malik, nor from Amr except Salih ibn Abdillah; Ibn Aishah alone transmitted it."
"8483","8475 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Uthman al-Lahiqi narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Ubaydillah the freedman of Ubayd al-Sayd narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hakam ibn Aban narrating from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says: Glory be to Allah, and praise be to Allah, and there is no god but Allah, and Allah is greatest, Allah will plant for him for each one of them a tree in Paradise."
"8484","8476 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ali narrated to us, he said: Imran narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hakam narrating from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever builds a mosque which Allah sees, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise, and if he dies that day he will be forgiven; and whoever digs a grave which Allah sees, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise, and if he dies that day he will be forgiven. No one narrated these two hadiths from al-Hakam except Imran; Ali ibn Uthman alone transmitted them."
"8485","8477 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ali narrated to us, he said: Imran narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hakam narrating from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shelters one orphan or two orphans, then is patient and expects reward, I and he will be in Paradise like these two, and he moved his two fingers the index and middle. No one narrated this hadith from al-Hakam except Imran; Ali ibn Uthman alone transmitted it."
"8486","8478 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi informed me, from Amir ibn Sad ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered placing the palms and raising the feet in prayer. None perfected the chain of this hadith from Muhammad ibn Ajlan except Wuhayb and al-Darawardi."
"8487","8479 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Shaqiq, from Azrah ibn Qays, from Khalid ibn al-Walid who said: The Commander of the Believers Umar wrote to me when Sham had laid down its burdens with butter and honey, that I march to Hind, while I disliked that. He said: And Hind in our view that day was Basrah. Then a man said: Fear Allah O Abu Sulayman, for tribulations have appeared. He said: And is Ibn al-Khattab alive? They will only come after him, and the people will be at Dhu Billiyan, at such-and-such place and such-and-such place, so a man will look and consider whether he finds a place not afflicted with the like of the tribulation and evil that afflicted the place he is in, and he will not find it. Those are the days which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned before the Hour, the days of harj, so seek refuge with Allah lest they reach me and you. No one narrated this hadith from Asim except Abu Awanah."
"8488","8480 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Muhammad al-Nashiti narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mudrik al-Nakhai, from Ibrahim, from al-Rabi ibn Khuthaym, from Abdullah ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Is one of you unable to recite a third of the Quran every night? They said: And who can bear that or is able to do that? He said: He recites: Say He is Allah the One. No one narrated this hadith from Shubah except Uthman ibn Muhammad and Muadh ibn Muadh and Yahya ibn Abdillah the freedman of Banu Hashim."
"8489","8481 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Sawwar al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb said: from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had cupping done, gave the cupper his wage, and used snuff. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Tawus except Wuhayb."
"8490","8482 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abbad ibn al-Awwam, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas who said: Verses were abrogated from this surah, meaning Surah al-Maidah: the verse of the garlands, and His saying: So judge between them or turn away from them. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given a choice; if he wished he judged between them, and if he wished he turned away from them and returned them to their laws. Then was revealed: And judge between them by what Allah has revealed and do not follow their desires. He was ordered to judge between them by our Book. No one narrated this hadith from al-Hakam except Sufyan; Abbad ibn al-Awwam alone transmitted it."
"8491","8483 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Taifi, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no sadaqah in less than five awsuq, nor in less than five awaq is there sadaqah, nor in less than five dhawd is there sadaqah. No one narrated this hadith from Amr ibn Dinar except Muhammad ibn Muslim."
"8492","8484 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Shuayth ibn Muhriz narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Khalid al-Khuzai narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a ring of silver in his hand, and its stone was of it, and its engraving was: Muhammad, Messenger of Allah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would place its stone toward the inside of his palm. He entered the house of Hafsah and threw it away out of dislike or from dislike. No one narrated this hadith from Uthman ibn Khalid except Shuayth ibn Muhriz."
"8493","8485 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur and Dawud ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has hair, let him honor it. No one narrated this hadith from Suhayl except Ibn Abi al-Zinad."
"8494","8486 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj alone. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Zinad except his son."
"8495","8487 - Muadh narrated to us, Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym al-Taifi narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays over a funeral has a qirat, and whoever stays until it is buried has two qirats. They said: Like these qirats of ours? He said: No, but like Uhud. No one narrated this hadith from Nafi except Ismail ibn Umayyah; Yahya ibn Sulaym alone transmitted it."
"8496","8488 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that Dawud peace be upon him was brought two women, a wolf having eaten the son of one of them, disputing over the remaining one, so he judged for the elder. When they came out before Sulayman he said: How did he judge between you? So they informed him, and he said: Bring me a knife. Abu Hurayrah said: And the first time I heard him mention the knife was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); we used to call it al-mudyah. The younger said: Why? He said: We shall cut him between you. She said: Give him to her. And the elder said: Cut him between us. So Sulayman judged with him for the younger. They said: Had he been your son you would not have agreed to cut him. No one narrated this hadith from Rawh except Yazid ibn Zuray; Umayyah alone transmitted it."
"8497","8489 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Awn al-Dubai narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Awf, from Abu Raja, from Samurah ibn Jundub who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woman is created from a rib; if you straighten her you will break her, so deal with her gently and you will live with her. No one narrated this hadith from Awf except Jafar ibn Sulayman."
"8498","8490 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Umar al-Juddi narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn al-Rabi al-Yuhmadi narrated to us, from Hisham al-Dastuwai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you takes, let him take with his right, and when he gives let him give with his right, and when he eats let him eat with his right, and when he drinks let him drink with his right, for Satan takes with his left, gives with his left, eats with his left, and drinks with his left. No one narrated this hadith from Hisham except Ziyad ibn al-Rabi; Hafs ibn Umar al-Juddi alone transmitted it."
"8499","8491 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Aslam al-Adawi narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There will be rulers who lie and oppress; whoever believes them in their lies and helps them in their oppression is not from me, and whoever does not believe them in their lies nor help them in their oppression is from me and I am from him, and he will come to me at the Hawd. No one narrated this hadith from Yunus except Sahl ibn Aslam."
"8500","8492 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Umar ibn Sulayt narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Dirar ibn Murrah narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah and Abu Said, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Mighty and Majestic says: Fasting is for Me, and I reward for it. And the fasting person has two joys: when he breaks fast he rejoices, and when he meets Allah and He rewards him he rejoices. And the breath of the fasting person's mouth is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Sinan Dirar ibn Murrah except Abd al-Aziz and Muhammad ibn Fudayl."
"8501","8493 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Dirar ibn Murrah narrated to us, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people of Paradise are one hundred and twenty rows; this ummah is eighty rows of them. No one narrated this hadith from Dirar except Abd al-Aziz."
"8502","8494 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Kulayb ibn Wail narrated to us, he said: Hani ibn Qays narrated to me, from Habib ibn Abi Mulaykah who said: I was sitting beside Ibn Umar, then a man came to him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, inform me about Uthman, did he attend Badr? He said: No. He said: Did he attend the pledge of Ridwan? He said: No. He said: Was he among those who turned away on the day the two hosts met? He said: Yes. He said: So the man turned away. He said: Then the man said to Abdullah ibn Umar: This one will now go and inform the people that you spoke ill of Uthman. He said: Did I do that? He said: So he claims. He said: Bring the man to me, so they returned him. He said: Do you know what I said to you? The man said: I asked you: Did Uthman attend Badr? And you said: No. And I asked you: Did he attend the pledge of Ridwan? And you said: No. And I asked you: Was he among those who turned away on the day the two hosts met? And you said: Yes. So Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Badr: Uthman was detained for the need of Allah and the need of His Messenger, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck a share for him, and he did not strike a share for anyone absent other than him. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Uthman on the day of the pledge of Ridwan to Makkah, to seek their permission regarding the sacrificial animals and entering Makkah, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledged the pledge of Ridwan while he intended to enter Makkah, and said: Uthman is in the need of Allah and His Messenger, so I pledge allegiance to Allah on his behalf, and he clapped one of his hands upon the other. He said: And Allah said: Those of you who turned away on the day the two hosts met, Satan only caused them to slip for some of what they earned, and Allah has pardoned them. So go, for Allah has pardoned; go now and exert your utmost effort against me. None of those who narrated this hadith inserted in this chain between Kulayb ibn Wail and Habib ibn Abi Mulaykah: Hani ibn Qays, except Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad; and Zaidah and a group narrated it from Kulayb ibn Wail, from Habib ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Umar; and Habib ibn Abi Mulaykah is kunya'd Abu Thawr al-Huddani, a clan of Murad."
"8503","8495 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Jundub ibn Abdillah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever among you is able not to let there come between him and Paradise the fill of a palm of blood which he sheds as if slaughtering a chicken, every time a door of the doors of Paradise presents itself it comes between him and it; and whoever among you is able not to put in his belly except what is wholesome, for the first thing that rots of man is his belly. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Abu Awanah and al-Hajjaj ibn al-Hajjaj."
"8504","8496 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade tabattul. And Qatadah recited: And We sent messengers before you and made for them wives and offspring. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Hisham; Muadh alone transmitted it."
"8505","8497 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Musab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Muharrar ibn Harun al-Qurashi narrated to us, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah cursed seven of His creation from above His seven heavens, and repeated the curse upon one of them three times, and cursed each one of them with a curse sufficient for him. He said: Cursed is whoever does the deed of the people of Lut, cursed is whoever does the deed of the people of Lut, cursed is whoever does the deed of the people of Lut, cursed is whoever slaughters for other than Allah, cursed is whoever comes upon any of the beasts, cursed is whoever disobeys his parents, cursed is whoever joins a woman and her daughter, cursed is whoever changes the boundaries of land, cursed is whoever claims to other than his patrons."
"8506","8498 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Musab narrated to us, he said: Muharrar ibn Harun narrated to us, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hasten to deeds before six: you await nothing but enriching wealth that transgresses, or corrupting illness, or dotting old age, or sudden death, or the Dajjal, and evil awaited, or the Hour, and the Hour is more calamitous and bitter. No one narrated these two hadiths from al-Araj except Muharrar ibn Harun."
"8507","8499 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Musab narrated to us, he said: Attaf ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi narrated to us, from Talhah the freedman of the family of Suraqah who said: I saw Muawiyah ibn Abdillah ibn Jafar performing wudu; he rinsed his mouth, sniffed water, washed his face three times, washed his hands three times, wiped his head, and washed his feet three times. Then he said: Thus I saw Abdullah ibn Jafar performing wudu, and Abdullah ibn Jafar said: Thus I saw Uthman ibn Affan performing wudu, and Uthman said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performing wudu. No one narrated this hadith from Muawiyah ibn Abdillah ibn Jafar except Talhah the freedman of the family of Suraqah; Attaf ibn Khalid alone transmitted it."
"8508","8500 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Shadh ibn al-Fayyad narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is more joyful at the repentance of His servant than one of you who finds his camel upon which he was dropped after having lost it in a desert land. No one narrated this hadith from Umar ibn Ibrahim except Shadh."
"8509","8501 - Muadh narrated to us: Shadh narrated to us, he said: Umar narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan, from Najiyah ibn Kab, from Abdullah ibn Masud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The servant is born a believer, lives a believer, and dies a believer; and the servant is born a disbeliever, lives a disbeliever, and dies a disbeliever; and the servant works for a period of his life with felicity, then what was written for him overtakes him and he dies a disbeliever; and the servant works for a period of his life with wretchedness, then what was written for him overtakes him and he dies fortunate. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Umar ibn Ibrahim; Shadh alone transmitted it."
"8510","8502 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Shadh narrated to us, he said: Hashim ibn Said al-Kufi narrated to us, from Kinanah, from Safiyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) freed me and made my freedom my dowry."
"8511","8503 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Shadh narrated to us, he said: Hashim ibn Said narrated to us, from Kinanah, from Safiyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me while I was weeping, and said: O daughter of Huyayy, what makes you weep? She said: It reached me that Aishah and Hafsah speak against me and say: We are better than her; we are the cousins of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his wives. He said: Why did you not say: How can you be better than me when my father is Harun, my uncle is Musa, and my husband is Muhammad?"
"8512","8504 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Shadh narrated to us, he said: Hashim narrated to us, from Kinanah, from Safiyyah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me while before me were four thousand date-pits with which I was glorifying Allah, and said: O daughter of Huyayy, what is this? I said: I glorify with them. He said: I have glorified since I stood over your head more than this. I said: Teach me O Messenger of Allah. He said: Say: Glory be to Allah to the number of what He created of things. No one narrated these hadiths from Kinanah, from Safiyyah, except Hashim ibn Said al-Kufi; Shadh alone transmitted them."
"8513","8505 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to me, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in three white Sahuli garments. No one narrated this hadith from Rawh except Yazid ibn Zuray; Muhammad ibn al-Minhal alone transmitted it."
"8514","8506 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah, from his father, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade three hours in which we pray or bury our dead: when the sun rises shining until it ascends, and when the stander of noon stands, and when it inclines to its setting. I said to Uqbah: Is burial at night allowed? He said: Yes; Abu Bakr was buried at night. No one narrated this hadith from Rawh ibn al-Qasim except Yazid ibn Zuray and Makhlad ibn Yazid al-Basri; al-Fadl ibn Yaqub al-Jazari alone transmitted it from Makhlad."
"8515","8507 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give the obligatory shares to their entitled ones, and what the obligatory shares leave is for the nearest male relative. No one narrated this hadith from Rawh except Yazid ibn Zuray; Muhammad ibn al-Minhal alone transmitted it."
"8516","8508 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day urged charity, then a man said: I have a dinar. He said: Spend it upon yourself. He said: I have another. He said: Spend it upon your servant. He said: I have another. He said: You know best. No one narrated this hadith from Rawh ibn al-Qasim except Yazid ibn Zuray; Muhammad ibn al-Minhal alone transmitted it."
"8517","8509 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Musa al-Shami Kudaym narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman al-Numayri narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wudu and wiped over the two khuffs. No one narrated this hadith from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abu al-Nadr, from Amir, except Fudayl ibn Sulayman; Yunus ibn Musa alone transmitted it."
"8518","8510 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Abd al-Wahid Abu Ghassan al-Mismai narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn al-Sabbah al-Mismai narrated to us, from Shubah, from Waqid ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was ordered to fight the people until they testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, establish prayer, and give zakah; when they do so they protect from me their blood and wealth, and their reckoning is upon Allah. No one narrated this hadith in its fullness from Shubah except Abd al-Malik ibn al-Sabbah; Abu Ghassan alone transmitted it."
"8519","8511 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, he said: Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrating from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: When Allah revealed to His Prophet: And warn your nearest kin, the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O company of Quraysh, save yourselves from the Fire; O company of Banu Abd Manaf, save yourselves from the Fire; O company of Banu Kab ibn Luayy, save yourselves from the Fire; O company of Banu Hashim, save yourselves from the Fire; O Fatimah daughter of Muhammad, save yourself from the Fire, I own nothing for you from Allah, except that you have kinship and I shall keep it moist with its moisture. No one narrated this hadith from al-Hajjaj except Mutamir."
"8520","8512 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, he said: Mutamir narrated to us, I heard Ibn Artah narrating from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Kurdus ibn Abbas al-Thalabi, from Abu Musa al-Ashari who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The perishing of my ummah is by stabbing and plague. It was said: And what is the plague O Messenger of Allah? He said: The piercing of your enemies from the jinn, and in each is martyrdom. No one narrated this hadith from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Kurdus, except al-Hajjaj; Mutamir alone transmitted it. And Abu Bakr al-Nahshali narrated it from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Usamah ibn Sharik, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith. And al-Thawri and Misar and Israil narrated it from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Abu Musa."
"8521","8513 - Muadh narrated to us, Kathir ibn Yahya Abu Malik narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Amash, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from al-Qasim ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abdillah, from Ibn Masud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has placed this affair among you and you are its rulers, and it will remain among you so long as you do not do deeds by which it is taken from you; when you do that, Allah will set over you the worst of His creation, and they will shave you as the twig is shaved. No one narrated this hadith from al-Amash except Abu Awanah and Abu Yahya al-Himmani; Abu Malik Kathir ibn Yahya alone transmitted it from Abu Awanah, and Abu Kurayb alone transmitted it from Abu Yahya al-Himmani."
"8522","8514 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Azrah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite in the Fajr prayer on Friday Alif Lam Mim the Sajdah, and: Has there come upon man. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Hammam ibn Yahya."
"8523","8515 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Hudbah narrated to us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Hazm al-Qutai narrated to us, he said: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, regarding the saying of Allah Mighty and Majestic: He is worthy of fear and worthy of forgiveness. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah said: I am worthy to be feared so none is associated with Me, and I am worthy, for whoever fears Me not to associate with Me, to forgive him."
"8524","8516 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Hudbah narrated to us, he said: Suhayl narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever Allah promises a reward for a deed, He will fulfill it for him; and whomever He promises punishment for a deed, He has the choice. No one narrated these two hadiths except Suhayl ibn Abi Hazm; Hudbah alone transmitted them."
"8525","8517 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, from his father, from Amir al-Shabi, that he heard al-Numan ibn Bashir saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The likeness of the sinner among the people is like the likeness of people who rode in a ship in the sea, and divided it, so each man of them got a place; then a man of them set to his place and bored a hole in it, so they said: What is with him, does he want to drown us? He said: And what are you to me regarding my place? He said: If they leave him they will drown and he will drown with them, and if they seize his hand they will be saved and he will be saved with them; such is the likeness of the sinner. No one narrated this hadith from Salamah except his son Muhammad, nor narrated it from his son except Hassan; al-Azraq alone transmitted it."
"8526","8518 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My intercession is for the people of major sins of my ummah. None narrated it from Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah al-Umari except al-Muqaddami."
"8527","8519 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from Abu Bishr, from Abbad ibn Sharahil who said: I went out, I and my uncle, to Madinah, and hunger struck me, so I entered a garden, and there was a crop ripe, so I began rubbing and eating. Then the owner of the garden came, beat me and took my garment. So I complained of him to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: You neither fed him when he was hungry, nor disciplined him when he was ignorant; return his garment to him. No one narrated this hadith from Sufyan ibn Husayn except Umar ibn Ali. And Sufyan ibn Husayn said: from Abu Bishr, from Abbad ibn Sharahil; and Shubah narrated it from Abu Bishr, from Abbad ibn Sharahil."
"8528","8520 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Said al-Zimmani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Hasir narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Dharr said: That I swear ten oaths that Ibn Said is the Dajjal is dearer to me than that I swear once that he is not he; and that is because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to his mother and said: Ask her, how long did she carry? So I asked her, and she said: Twelve months. He said: Ask her, how was his cry when he fell? She said: The cry of a one-month-old boy. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I have hidden for you something hidden, so what is it? He said: A bone of a dusty white sheep. He began wanting to say smoke, and began saying: Dukh, Dukh. So he said: Be off, for you will not precede the decree. No one narrated this hadith from al-Harith except Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad."
"8529","8521 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The muhrim, when he does not find an izar, wears trousers; and if he does not find sandals, he wears khuffs. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Yazid ibn Zuray."
"8530","8522 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I read to al-Fudayl ibn Maysarah Abu Muadh, he said: Abu Hariz narrated to us, that Qays ibn Abi Hazim narrated to him, from Adi ibn Amiray al-Hadrami who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits would be seen; then when he gave salam he would turn with his face to his right until the whiteness of his cheek would be seen, and to his left. This hadith is not narrated from Adi ibn Amiray except with this chain; Mutamir alone transmitted it."
"8531","8523 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Mutamir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Abu Mijlaz, from Anas ibn Malik who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Zaynab bint Jahsh he invited the people, and they ate; then he took as if preparing to stand, so whoever stood stood, and some sat; when he saw that he stood, then they stood after that; so I informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he came; then I went to enter, and he cast the curtain between me and him. He said: And the verse of hijab was revealed; he recited: When you have eaten, then disperse. No one narrated this hadith from Sulayman al-Taymi except Mutamir."
"8532","8524 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Yazid al-Awdi narrated to us, from Amir al-Shabi, from Urwah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, that he heard his father saying: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he went for his need; then he gestured to me, so I went; then I brought him water, and upon him was a Syrian jubbah without sleeves, so he cast it upon his shoulder. He said: Pour upon me. So I poured upon him, and he performed wudu and wiped over the khuffs. So it became a sunnah: for the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night. None narrates this hadith from Dawud al-Awdi except Makki, and none of those who narrated this hadith from al-Shabi said: So it became a sunnah, for the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night, except Dawud."
"8533","8525 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, that he came to Abdullah and said: Between me and anyone of the Arabs there is no hatred, and I passed by the mosque of Banu Hanifah, and they were sitting believing in Musaylimah; so Abdullah sent for them, and they were brought; then he asked them to repent except Ibn al-Nawwahah. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Were you not a messenger I would have struck your neck; for today you are not a messenger. Stand O Qarazah and strike his neck. So Qarazah ibn Kab stood and struck his neck in the market, and said: Whoever wants to look at Ibn al-Nawwahah, let him look in the market. No one narrated this hadith from al-Thawri except Muhammad ibn Kathir."
"8534","8526 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Abu Ismail al-Muaddib narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best quality of the fasting person is the siwak. No one narrated this hadith from Mujalid except Abu Ismail al-Muaddib."
"8535","8527 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the prayer is established do not stand until you see me. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Abd al-Warith."
"8536","8528 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zubayd, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab who said: The Adha prayer is two rakahs, the Fitr prayer is two rakahs, the traveler's prayer is two rakahs, and the Friday prayer is two rakahs, complete not shortened, upon the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). None of those who narrated this hadith from Sufyan, from Zubayd, from Ibn Abi Layla, from his father, said it except Muadh ibn Muadh."
"8537","8529 - Muadh narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Mutarrif who said: Imran ibn Husayn said: I narrate to you a hadith, perhaps Allah will benefit you with it. Know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Hajj and Umrah, and no Quran was revealed abrogating it, nor did he forbid it until he died. A man said with his opinion what he wished. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Abd al-Warith."
"8538","8530 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu al-Muhallab the uncle of Abu Qilabah, from Imran ibn Husayn who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Your brother al-Najashi has died, so stand and pray over him. So we stood and lined up over him as one lines up over the dead, and prayed over him as one prays over the dead. No one narrated this hadith from Yunus, from Ibn Sirin, except Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal."
"8539","8531 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah ibn Masud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sleep in his prostration, and we would not know his sleep except by his breathing; then he would stand in his prayer. No one narrated this hadith from al-Amash except Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad."
"8540","8532 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn Amr al-Jafari narrated to us, he said: I heard Amr ibn Dinar, from Urwah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever sells wine, let him cut up pigs. This hadith is not narrated from al-Mughirah except with this chain; Talhah ibn Amr alone transmitted it."
"8541","8533 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Bujayr ibn Abi Bujayr, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes a dog, not a hunting dog nor a livestock dog, a qirat is deducted from his reward every day. Then he mentioned that they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and they passed by the grave of Abu Righal, and he said: This is the grave of Abu Righal, and he is the father of Thaqif, and he is a man of Thamud; his dwelling was in the Haram, then when Allah destroyed his people with what He destroyed them with, he was protected by his place from the Haram; and he went out until when he reached here he died and was buried, and with him was buried a branch of gold. So we hastened to it and extracted it. No one narrated this hadith from Rawh ibn al-Qasim except Yazid ibn Zuray."
"8542","8534 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Iyas ibn Khalifah, from Rafi ibn Khadij, that Ali ordered Ammar to ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about madhi, and he said: He washes his private parts and performs wudu. No one narrated this hadith from Rawh except Yazid; Umayyah alone transmitted it."
"8543","8535 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me, that Kab ibn Malik, when Allah accepted his repentance, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, only truthfulness saved me from Allah, and among my repentance is that I shall not speak a lie, and that I divest myself of my wealth for Allah and His Messenger. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep some of your wealth, it is better for you. And Kab ibn Malik said: Then I keep my share from Khaybar. No one narrated this hadith from Ismail ibn Umayyah except al-Fadl."
"8544","8536 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Ziyad Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Nafi the freedman of Hamnah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sala the Ansari, that his brothers complained of him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: He squanders his wealth and is lavish with it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Qays, what is the matter with your brothers complaining of you? They claim you squander your wealth and are lavish with it. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I take my share of the fruit and spend it in the path of Allah and upon those who accompany me. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck his chest and said: Spend, Allah will spend upon you, three times. Then after that I went out in the path of Allah with a mount. He said: I am today the richest of my household and the most at ease. This hadith is not narrated from Qays ibn Sala except with this chain; Sad Abu Asim alone transmitted it."
"8545","8537 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Sufyan ibn Abdillah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: One who boasts of what he was not given is like the wearer of two garments of falsehood. It is not narrated from Sufyan ibn Abdillah except with this chain."
"8546","8538 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"8547","8539 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Ulathah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdillah and Anas, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he sought rain would say: O Allah, give us a drink, wide, gentle, beneficial, by which You fill wealth and souls, rain pleasant, wholesome, covering, enveloping, by which You bestow upon our desert and our settled folk, by which You send down of the blessings of the heavens, and bring forth for us of the blessings of the earth, and make us at it of the thankful; indeed You are the Hearer of supplication. And when he supplicated against locusts he would say: O Allah, kill its elders, destroy its young, spoil its eggs, and cut its offspring. No hadith is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding supplication against locusts other than this; Ibn Ulathah alone transmitted it."
"8548","8540 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man shall not sell over the sale of his brother, nor propose over the proposal of his brother, nor bid up prices against one another; and a townsman shall not sell for a bedouin; and a woman shall not ask for the divorce of her sister to overturn what is in her vessel, for she has only what was written for her; and do not leave camels and sheep unmilked, and whoever buys a musarrah, he has the choice between the two views: if he returns it, he returns with it a sa of dates. No one narrated this hadith on al-musarrah from al-Zuhri except Sulayman ibn Kathir; Muhammad ibn Kathir alone transmitted it."
"8549","8541 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Razin, from his brother, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah has forbidden the singing girl, her sale, her price, teaching her, and listening to her. This hadith is not narrated from Ibn Sabit, from Aishah, except with this chain; Jafar ibn Sulayman alone transmitted it."
"8550","8542 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from his maternal uncle, from Aishah, that they said: O Messenger of Allah, one of us speaks to himself about the matter of the Lord, and falling from the sky to the earth is dearer to him than to speak of it. He said: And have you found it? They said: Yes. He said: That is pure faith. No one narrated this hadith from Shahr ibn Hawshab except Thabit; Hammad alone transmitted it."
"8551","8543 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Muyassar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Amir al-Khazzaz, from al-Hasan, from Sad the freedman of Abu Bakr who said: I presented dates before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they began pairing them, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not pair. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Amir except Abu Dawud."
"8552","8544 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the house of Umm Salamah upon a green mat, and she sprinkled it, then he prayed upon it, and they prayed behind him. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Said except Sulayman ibn Kathir, and Yahya ibn Said."
"8553","8545 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Qayzi narrated to us, from Sulayman and Zurah and Muhammad ibn Husayn ibn Sawa, from their grandmother Umm Sunbulah, that she brought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a gift, but his wives refused to accept it, and said: We do not take. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them, and they took it. Then he granted her a valley as iqta, then Abdullah ibn Jahsh bought it from Hasan ibn Ali. This hadith is not narrated from Umm Sunbulah except with this chain; Zayd ibn al-Hubab alone transmitted it."
"8554","8546 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yunus Abu Muslim al-Mustamli narrated to us, he said: Man ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Muttalib narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever is killed defending his wealth is a martyr. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar except Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Muttalib, nor from Abd al-Aziz except Man; Abu Muslim alone transmitted it."
"8555","8547 - Muadh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abbad ibn al-Awwam, from Hajjaj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: Abu Israil vowed to stand in the sun a day until night and not speak, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to sit and speak. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Zubayr except Hajjaj; Abbad ibn al-Awwam alone transmitted it."
"8556","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Mukays, the freedman of al-Abbas, narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays the dawn prayer is under the protection of Allah. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Yunus, nor from Yunus except Ayyub; Mukays is alone in transmitting it."
"8557","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The imam is a guarantor, and the mu'adhdhin is entrusted. O Allah, guide the imams and forgive the mu'adhdhins. No one narrated this hadith from Rawh except Yazid ibn Zuray'."
"8558","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn Isa al-Qazzaz narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from al-Zuhri, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle Abdullah ibn Zayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats from this tree should not come near our mosques, meaning garlic. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri, from Abbad ibn Tamim, except Ibrahim ibn Sa'd; Ma'n al-Qazzaz is alone in transmitting it."
"8559","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Murajja ibn Raja' narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: We used to carry bricks for building the mosque, one brick at a time, while Ammar used to carry two bricks at a time, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brushed the dust off his shoulders and said: Woe to you, O son of Sumayyah, the rebellious party will kill you. No one narrated this hadith from Murajja ibn Raja' except Abu Umar al-Hawdi."
"8560","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Khuza'i narrated to us, he said: Raja' Abu Yahya, the seller of second-hand goods, narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lets his intercession block one of the prescribed punishments of Allah has opposed Allah in His dominion, and whoever helps in a dispute without knowing whether it is true or false remains under the wrath of Allah until he withdraws, and whoever walks with a people thinking he is a witness while he is not a witness is like a false witness, and whoever pretends to have dreamed falsely will be burdened with tying together the two ends of a barley grain, and reviling a Muslim is wickedness, and fighting him is disbelief. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, except Raja' Abu Yahya."
"8561","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Suwayd narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Aqil ibn Abi Talib, he said: Quraysh came to Abu Talib and said: O Abu Talib, your nephew comes to us in our Ka'bah and our gathering places and makes us hear what offends us; if you see fit to restrain him from us, do so. He said to me: O Aqil, seek out your cousin for me, so I brought him out from a shelter of the shelters in the ravine of Abu Talib, walking with me, seeking the shade with his garment but unable to find it, until he reached Abu Talib. Abu Talib said to him: O son of my brother, by Allah, as far as I know you have always obeyed me, and your people have come claiming that you come to them in their Ka'bah and their gatherings and make them hear what offends them, and I think you should refrain from them. He raised his gaze to the sky and said: By Allah, I am no more able to abandon what I was sent with than for one of you to kindle a flame of fire from this sun. Abu Talib said: By Allah, he has never lied; go back rightly guided. No one narrated this hadith from Talhah ibn Yahya except Abdul-Wahid ibn Ziyad and Yunus ibn Bukayr; Ibrahim ibn Abi Suwayd is alone in transmitting it from Abdul-Wahid, and Abu Kurayb from Yunus, and it is not narrated from Aqil except with this chain."
"8562","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray while traveling on his mount, wherever it turned with him, gesturing, and making the prostration lower than the bowing. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except al-Harith ibn Umayr; al-Shafi'i is alone in transmitting it."
"8563","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: It was forbidden to drink from the mouth of the waterskin. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except al-Harith ibn Umayr; Ibrahim al-Shafi'i is alone in transmitting it."
"8564","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from his father, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked about the Hour. The prayer came, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is the one who asked about the Hour? He said to him: What have you prepared for it? He said: I have not prepared much deed or prayer for it, except that I love Allah and His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A person will be with those he loves. Anas said: I never saw the Muslims rejoice over anything after Islam as they rejoiced over this. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Humayd al-Tawil except Abdullah ibn Yunus ibn Ubayd."
"8565","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Qaza'ah ibn Suwayd, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Ghannam, from one of his mothers, from Umm Farwah, that she heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The best of deeds is prayer at the beginning of its time. No one narrated this hadith from Ubaydullah ibn Umar except Qaza'ah ibn Suwayd."
"8566","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Hatim ibn Wardan narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, he said: Some cloaks came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he distributed them among his companions. My father Makhramah said: Take us to him, perhaps we will get something of them. My father came to the door and said: Is he here? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard his voice and came out with a cloak, and I can almost see him showing my father the beauties of the cloak, saying: I kept this for you, I kept this for you. I said to my father: Why did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do this with Makhramah? He said: He used to guard against his tongue. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Hatim ibn Wardan."
"8567","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Hatim narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, he said: Umarah ibn Qurs al-Laythi went out on one of his raids and stayed as long as Allah willed, then returned until, when he was near al-Ahwaz, he heard the sound of the call to prayer and said: By Allah, I have not prayed with a congregation of Muslims for a long time, and he headed toward the call intending to pray, but they turned out to be the Azariqah. They said to him: What brought you, O enemy of Allah? He said: Are you not my brothers? They said: You are the brother of Satan; we will surely kill you. He said: Are you not content with me with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was content with from me? They said: What did he accept from you? He said: I came to him while I was a disbeliever, and I testified that there is no god but Allah and that he is the Messenger of Allah, so he let me go. They seized him and killed him. No one narrated this hadith from Yunus ibn Ubayd except Hatim ibn Wardan; his son is alone in transmitting it from him."
"8568","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Frequently remember the destroyer of pleasures, for no one remembers it in hardship but Allah makes it spacious for him, nor remembers it in ease but it constrains it upon him. No one narrated this hadith from Abdul-Aziz ibn Muslim except Isa ibn Ibrahim."
"8569","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fear wrongdoing, for wrongdoing is darkness on the Day of Resurrection, and fear miserliness, for miserliness destroyed those before you; it drove them to shed their blood and to violate their sacred things. No one narrated this hadith from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam except Dawud ibn Qays, and it is not narrated from Jabir except with this chain."
"8570","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Muslim, the nephew of al-Zuhri, narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the virtue of Say: He is Allah, the One, and he said: It is a third of the Quran, or equals it. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except his nephew."
"8571","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tharwan, from Hudhayl ibn Shurahbil, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Before the Hour there will be trials like pieces of the dark night; a man will wake up therein a believer and enter the evening a disbeliever, and enter the evening a believer and wake up a disbeliever. The one sitting during them is better than the one standing, the standing is better than the walking, and the walking is better than the striving. So break your bows, cut your strings, and strike your swords with stones; if someone enters upon one of you in his house, let him be like the better of the two sons of Adam. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Juhadah except Abdul-Warith."
"8572","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Atiq and Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Imran ibn Husayn, that a man freed six slaves of his at the time of his death, and he had no wealth besides them. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he called them, divided them into portions, then cast lots among them, freed two and kept four in slavery. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Atiq except Hammad ibn Zayd."
"8573","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Warith narrated to us, from Abdul-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id, that Jibril came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Have you complained, O Muhammad? He said: Yes. He said: In the name of Allah I perform ruqyah upon you, from every disease that harms you, and from the evil of every soul and eye; Allah heals you, in the name of Allah I perform ruqyah upon you. No one narrated this hadith from Abdul-Aziz except Abdul-Warith."
"8574","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'n al-Raqashi narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is mindful of Allah will enter Paradise without misery, and will live without dying; his clothes will not wear out and his youth will not perish. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Hisham and al-Hajjaj ibn al-Hajjaj; his son Mu'adh is alone in transmitting it from Hisham, and Ibrahim ibn Tahman is alone in transmitting it from al-Hajjaj."
"8575","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hashim al-Simsar narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Atiyyah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seeking knowledge is an obligation upon every Muslim. No one narrated this hadith from Mis'ar except Yahya ibn Hashim and Isma'il ibn Ibrahim al-Kufi."
"8576","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Musaddad and Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami and Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, they said: Aththam ibn Ali narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counting the tasbih. No one narrated this hadith from al-A'mash except Aththam ibn Ali."
"8577","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Talut narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Nuh al-Muttawwi'i narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Extinguish fire with takbir. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Ajlan except Nuh al-Muttawwi'i; his son is alone in transmitting it from him."
"8578","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak al-Ayshi narrated to us, he said: Nahid ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Isma'il al-Makki, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited over the funeral four times: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Isma'il al-Makki, none narrated it from Isma'il except Nahid ibn al-Qasim; Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak is alone in transmitting it."
"8579","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hajabi narrated to us, he said: Abu Alqamah al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: I heard Hisham ibn Urwah say: My father informed me, from Busrah bint Safwan, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever touches his private part, wudu has become obligatory upon him. None of those who narrated this hadith from Hisham ibn Urwah said: wudu has become obligatory upon him, except Abu Alqamah al-Farwi."
"8580","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Haydam narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Ya'fur, he said: I asked Anas ibn Malik about wiping over the leather socks, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wipe over them. No one raised this hadith from Abu Ya'fur except Abu Awanah, and none narrated it as raised from Abu Awanah except Qutaybah ibn Sa'id and Nu'aym ibn Haydam."
"8581","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Khidash narrated to us, he said: al-Ala' ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No son can repay his father except that he finds him enslaved and buys him and frees him. No one narrated this hadith from al-Ala' except Khalid ibn Khidash."
"8582","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub and Yunus and Hisham and al-Mu'alla ibn Ziyad, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, he said: Abu Bakrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When two Muslims meet with their swords, the killer and the killed are in the Fire. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub, Yunus and al-Mu'alla ibn Ziyad except Hammad, and none narrated it from Hammad except Khalid ibn Khidash and Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il."
"8583","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Musa al-Hadi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-A'mash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Sakhbarah, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever claims a lineage that is not known has disbelieved in Allah, and disavowal of lineage, even if subtle, is disbelief in Allah. No one raised this hadith from al-A'mash except al-Hajjaj, and none raised it from al-Hajjaj except Hammad ibn Salamah; Umar ibn Musa al-Hadi is alone in transmitting it."
"8584","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Abu Uways narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade snatching, plundering, al-mujaththamah, and eating every fanged beast of prey. No one narrated the beginning of this hadith from al-Zuhri concerning snatching and al-mujaththamah except Abu Uways; al-Qa'nabi is alone in transmitting it, and the end of the hadith is with the companions of al-Zuhri."
"8585","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mawdud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman al-Aslami narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever enters the mosque and spits, let him dig for it and bury it; if he does not do so, let him spit in his garment then take it out with him. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Rahman except Abu Mawdud."
"8586","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Fa'id, the freedman of Ubaydullah ibn Ali ibn Abi Rafi', narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Ali, from his grandmother Salma, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was struck by a bruise I would put henna on it. This hadith is not narrated from Salma except with this chain; Fa'id is alone in transmitting it."
"8587","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, that he heard A'ishah say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw wind and clouds it would be known in his face, and he would go forward and back; when it rained he would be delighted by it and it would leave him. She said: I asked him, and he said: I feared that it might be a punishment inflicted upon my ummah, and when he saw rain he would say: Mercy. No one narrated this hadith from Ja'far ibn Muhammad except Sulayman ibn Bilal and Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Muhammad."
"8588","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Salih al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Musa ibn Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: My father Musa ibn Ja'far narrated to me, he said: My father Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, from his father, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Faith is knowledge in the heart, affirmation with the tongue, and action with the limbs. No one narrated this hadith from Musa ibn Ja'far except Abd al-Salam, and it is not narrated from Ali except with this chain."
"8589","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah al-Dawlabi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a raid, and he said: Take many sandals, for a man remains as if mounted as long as he wears sandals. No one narrated this hadith from Musa ibn Uqbah except Ibn Abi al-Zinad."
"8590","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The delay of the rich in paying is injustice, and whoever is transferred to a solvent person, let him follow."
"8591","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let none of you withhold surplus water so as to withhold grass thereby. No one narrated these two hadiths from Rawh except Yazid; Muhammad ibn al-Minhal is alone in transmitting them."
"8592","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Abu Muslim al-Mustamli narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to combine Maghrib and Isha when traveling urgently. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Karim except Sufyan ibn Uyaynah; Abu Muslim is alone in transmitting it."
"8593","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Uthman al-Lahiqi narrated to us, he said: Sakhrah bint Ka'b ibn Hittan ibn Dhurayh ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi narrated to us, from her grandmother Umm Awanah, she said: A'ishah said: It is from the Sunnah that one of you take something in her hands or feet or neck or ears, which is stripped off when she is placed on the washing bed. This hadith is not narrated from Umm Awanah except with this chain; al-Lahiqi is alone in transmitting it."
"8594","Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Uthman al-Lahiqi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Abi Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad al-Dayli narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi al-Malih al-Hudhali narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ya'la al-Laythi, the judge of Basrah, that Ma'qil ibn Yasar sold a house of his for one hundred thousand and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Any man who sells a property without need, Allah sends upon him a destroyer that destroys it. No one narrated this hadith from Hafs ibn Abi Harb except Ali ibn Uthman al-Lahiqi."
"8595","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Kathir ibn Yahya narrated to us, Sallam ibn Abi Muti' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The imam is a guarantor, and the mu'adhdhin is entrusted. O Allah, guide the imams and forgive the mu'adhdhins."
"8596","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Rashid narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated to us, from al-Husayn ibn Waqid, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Ata', from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling water. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except al-Husayn ibn Waqid; al-Fadl ibn Musa is alone in transmitting it."
"8597","Muntasir ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, Rayhan ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Abbad ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of the People of the Book greets you, say: And upon you. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Abbad ibn Mansur; Rayhan ibn Sa'id is alone in transmitting it."
"8598","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Abi Qurrah narrated to us, from Ibn Lahi'ah, from Musa ibn Ayyub, from Iyas ibn Amir, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Clientage belongs to the one who frees. No one narrated this hadith from Musa ibn Ayyub except Ibn Lahi'ah; Ubayd ibn Abi Qurrah is alone in transmitting it, and it is not narrated from Ali except with this chain."
"8599","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Amr ibn Thabit narrated to us, from his father, from Sumay' al-Zayyat, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, Fatimah would uncover her head when a boy entered, and when a man entered she would cover it. This hadith is not narrated from Sa'id ibn Zayd except with this chain; Ibrahim ibn Sa'id is alone in transmitting it."
"8600","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Nadr narrated to us, Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ammi narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No wine addict will enter the enclosure of sanctity, nor one disobedient to his parents, nor one who reminds others of his gifts. No one narrated this hadith from Ali ibn Zayd except Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ammi; Abu al-Nadr is alone in transmitting it."
"8601","Muntasir ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Zafir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Dawud ibn al-Wazi', from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Wahid, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: I rode to Abdullah ibn Unays in Egypt to ask him about the hadith of retaliation, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: People will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection on a single plain, barefoot, naked, uncircumcised; the voice will reach the farthest of them as it reaches the nearest of them, saying: I am the King; no one of the people of Paradise should enter Paradise while one of the people of Hell has a claim of injustice against him seeking it, even for a slap and what is less than it. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how, when we are barefoot, naked, uncircumcised? He said: Through your deeds. No one narrated this hadith from Zafir ibn Sulayman except Muhammad ibn Bakkar."
"8602","Muntasir ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, Ali ibn Shubrumah al-Harithi narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive the pilgrim and those for whom the pilgrim seeks forgiveness. No one narrated this hadith from Mansur except Sharik, and none narrated it from Sharik except Ali ibn Shubrumah."
"8603","Muntasir ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is questioned closely about the reckoning on the Day of Resurrection will perish. I said: O Messenger of Allah, then where is the saying of Allah: He will soon be reckoned with an easy reckoning? He said: That is the presentation, and whoever is questioned closely about the reckoning on the Day of Resurrection will perish. None of those who narrated this hadith from Ibn Abi Mulaykah included Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr between Ibn Abi Mulaykah and A'ishah in its chain except al-Hajjaj ibn Artah; Abu Khalid al-Ahmar is alone in transmitting it."
"8604","Muntasir ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Amr ibn Malik, from Abu al-Jawza', from A'ishah, that she cursed a camel of hers, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It shall not accompany us. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Jawza' except Amr ibn Malik, nor from Amr except Hisham; Mu'adh is alone in transmitting it."
"8605","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu Ayyub al-Afriqi, Isa ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Isa ibn Atiyyah, he said: Abu Bakr stood the morning after he was pledged allegiance and addressed the people, saying: O people, I have rescinded your opinion of me; I am not the best of you, so pledge allegiance to the best of you. They stood to him and said: O caliph of the Messenger of Allah, by Allah you are the best of us. He said: O people, people entered Islam willingly and unwillingly, so they are under the protection of Allah and the neighborhood of Allah; if you can manage that Allah does not seek you for anything of His protection, do so. I have a devil that attends me, so when you see me angry, avoid me, lest I harm your hair and skin. O people, check the wages imposed on your servants, for no flesh grown from ill-gotten gain should enter Paradise. Watch me with your eyes: if I am straight, follow me, and if I deviate, straighten me; if I obey Allah, obey me, and if I disobey Allah, disobey me. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Ayyub al-Afriqi except Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman; Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban is alone in transmitting it."
"8606","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Khaythamah, Abu Ishaq narrated to me, that Wahb ibn Jabir narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Among Gog and Magog, a man does not die until he leaves at least a thousand or more of his offspring, and beyond them are nations: Mansik, Tarik and Taris. No one narrated this hadith from Ziyad ibn Khaythamah except Shuja'; his son is alone in transmitting it from him."
"8607","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Jamil ibn Hammad al-Ta'i narrated to us, from Ismah ibn Zamil al-Ta'i, from his father, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said to those around him: Guarantee for me six traits and I will guarantee Paradise for you. I said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Prayer, zakat, trust, chastity, the belly, and the tongue. No one narrated this hadith from Ismah ibn Zamil except Jamil ibn Hammad; Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban is alone in transmitting it."
"8608","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Tha'labah ibn al-Hakam, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mut'ah on the day of Khaybar. No one narrated this hadith from Simak except Sharik, nor from Sharik except Abu Ahmad; Mahmud ibn Ghaylan is alone in transmitting it, and it is not narrated from Tha'labah except with this chain."
"8609","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from A'ishah, she said: Abu Bakr sought permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was sitting with his thighs uncovered, so he gave him permission; Umar sought permission and he gave him permission while in the same state; then Uthman ibn Affan sought permission, so he reached for his garment and covered his thighs. I said: O Messenger of Allah, as if you disliked Uthman seeing you? He said: Uthman is shy and modest; the angels are shy before him. No one narrated this hadith from Suhayl except Abdullah ibn Umar, nor from Abdullah ibn Umar except Ishaq ibn Sulayman."
"8610","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, Harun ibn Muhammad Abu al-Tayyib narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Muhammad ibn Tahla' narrated to us, from Abu al-Rijal, from Amrah, from A'ishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O A'ishah, this is the condiment for this, meaning dates and bread. This hadith is not narrated from A'ishah except with this chain; Dawud ibn Rushayd is alone in transmitting it."
"8611","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Hammad Sajjadah narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata', from Abu Hurayrah, I doubt whether he raised it, he said: Permission is not given to the one seeking permission until he begins with greeting. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman except Hafs ibn Ghiyath; Sajjadah is alone in transmitting it."
"8612","Muntasir ibn Muhammad narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Hammad Sajjadah narrated to us, Amr ibn Hashim al-Janbi narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata', from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The imam is appointed to be followed, so when he says takbir, say takbir, and when he bows, bow, and when he says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: Praise be to Allah, and when he prays sitting, pray sitting all together. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Malik except Amr ibn Hashim; Sajjadah is alone in transmitting it, and no one said: say: Praise be to Allah, except in this hadith."
"8613","Muntasir narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Hammad Sajjadah narrated to us, al-Muttalib ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Ya'qub al-Qummi, from Isa ibn Jariyah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: My mother prepared food and said: Go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and invite him. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and spoke to him privately, saying: My mother has prepared something. He said to his companions: Rise. Fifty men rose with him; he sat at the door, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Admit ten at a time. They ate until they were full, and there remained about as much as there was. No one narrated this hadith from Ya'qub al-Qummi except al-Muttalib ibn Ziyad; al-Hasan ibn Hammad is alone in transmitting it."
"8614","Musabbih ibn Hatim al-Ukli al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Jami' al-Attar narrated to us, al-Ala' ibn Maymun al-Anbari narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning His saying, mighty and majestic: Whoever kills a believer intentionally, his recompense is Hell, he said: If He recompenses him. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Sirin except al-Hajjaj ibn al-Aswad, and none narrated it from al-Hajjaj except al-Ala' ibn Maymun; Muhammad ibn Jami' is alone in transmitting it."
"8615","Musabbih ibn Hatim al-Ukli narrated to us: Abd al-Jabbar ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: al-Ma'mun gave a sermon and spoke at length about modesty, then said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah and Imran ibn Husayn, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Modesty is part of faith, and faith is in Paradise; obscenity is part of harshness, and harshness is in the Fire."
"8616","Musabbih ibn Hatim narrated to us, Abd al-Jabbar ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: A man came to Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash and said: I heard a man saying: Allah did not speak to Musa in speech. He said: None says this but a disbeliever; I read to al-A'mash, and al-A'mash read to Yahya ibn Waththab, and Yahya ibn Waththab read to Abu Abd al-Rahman, and Abu Abd al-Rahman read to Ali ibn Abi Talib, and Ali read to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And Allah spoke to Musa in speech. No one narrated this hadith from al-A'mash except Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash; Abd al-Jabbar ibn Abdullah is alone in transmitting it."
"8617","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli Abu al-Jarud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sarri al-Asqalani narrated to us, Rawwad ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Mis'ar ibn Kidam, from Sa'id ibn Abi Burdah, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah writes for the sick the best of what he used to do in health as long as he is confined, and for the traveler the best of what he used to do at home. No one narrated this hadith from Mis'ar, from Sa'id ibn Abi Burdah, except Rawwad; Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sarri is alone in transmitting it. And Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated it from Mis'ar, from Ibrahim al-Saksaki, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa; none narrated it from Hafs except Ahmad ibn Abi al-Hawari."
"8618","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sarri al-Asqalani narrated to us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Seeking the lawful is obligatory upon every Muslim. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit except Jarir ibn Hazim, nor from Jarir except Baqiyyah; Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sarri is alone in transmitting it."
"8619","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sarri al-Asqalani narrated to us, Rishdin ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seeking knowledge is obligatory upon every Muslim. No one narrated this hadith from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih except Rishdin; Muhammad ibn Abi al-Sarri is alone in transmitting it."
"8620","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abd al-Rahman, when you swear an oath and see something else better than it, expiate for it and do what is better. No one narrated this hadith from Amr ibn Dinar except Sufyan ibn Uyaynah; Muhammad ibn Amr is alone in transmitting it."
"8621","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us: My father narrated to us: al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a funeral, and he sat by one of the graves and said: No day passes over this grave but it calls out in a clear distinct voice: O son of Adam, how have you forgotten me? Did you not know that I am the house of solitude, the house of estrangement, the house of loneliness, the house of worms, and the house of narrowness, except for those for whom Allah makes me spacious. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The grave is a garden from the gardens of Paradise, or a pit from the pits of Hell. No one narrated this hadith from al-Awza'i except Ayyub ibn Suwayd; his son is alone in transmitting it from him."
"8622","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Hani' al-Nahwi narrated to us, Azhar ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Raja' ibn Haywah, from Mu'awiyah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever Allah wants good for, He gives him understanding in religion. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Awn except Azhar; Abdullah ibn Hani' is alone in transmitting it."
"8623","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us: Imran ibn Harun al-Sufi narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from al-A'raj, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah, she said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before he performed the final circumambulation."
"8624","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Imran ibn Harun al-Sufi narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from al-A'raj, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted from Safiyyah what a man wants from his wife, and he said: Is she detaining us? It was said: She already performed the final circumambulation before menstruating. He said: Then let her depart. No one narrated this hadith from al-A'raj except Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8625","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Imran ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abdullah ibn Rafi' informed him, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade shooting an animal then eating it, but it should be slaughtered. No one narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Rafi' except Abu al-Aswad; Ibn Lahi'ah is alone in transmitting it."
"8626","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Imran ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, Bakr ibn Sawadah narrated to us, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, he said: There was a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named Aswad, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Abyad. This hadith is not narrated from Sahl ibn Sa'd except with this chain; Ibn Lahi'ah is alone in transmitting it."
"8627","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Imran ibn Harun narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) washed from major impurity, he began by pouring over his hands, then put them into the vessel, then washed his private part and what touched intercourse, then performed wudu as for prayer, then poured over his head three handfuls, running fingers between the roots of his hair, then poured over his body, then washed his feet when finishing. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Aswad except Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8628","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Imran ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I stoned anyone without proof I would have stoned so-and-so woman, for what has appeared of her, of suspicion in her appearance, her speech, and who enters upon her. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman except Abu al-Aswad; Ibn Lahi'ah is alone in transmitting it."
"8629","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us: Imran ibn Harun narrated to us, Maslamah ibn Ali narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These characters are from Allah; whomever Allah wants good for, He grants him good character, and whomever He wants evil for, He grants him bad character. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Ajlan except Maslamah ibn Ali; Imran ibn Harun is alone in transmitting it."
"8630","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Imran ibn Harun narrated to us, Maslamah ibn Ali narrated to us: Abu Abdullah al-Himsi narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six of Shawwal emerges from his sins like the day his mother bore him. No one narrated this hadith from Nafi' except Abu Abdullah al-Himsi; Maslamah ibn Ali is alone in transmitting it."
"8631","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Imran ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever changes his religion, kill him. No one narrated this hadith from Bukayr except Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8632","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us: Imran ibn Harun narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj narrated to me, from Bayhas al-Thaqafi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah, from his father, that he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What of one who believes in you without seeing you, and affirms you without seeing you? He said: Glad tidings to them, then glad tidings to them; they are from us and they are with us. No one narrated this hadith from Bukayr except Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8633","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us: Imran ibn Harun narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Hubayrah narrated to me, from Sahlah bint Suhayl ibn Amr, that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a woman who does something to endear her husband to her, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enjoyment in this world, and no share in the Hereafter. She said: What of a woman who sees a wet dream in her sleep, should she wash? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If she sees water, let her wash. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Hubayrah except Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8634","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us, Imran ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, from Amrah, from A'ishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The thief is not cut except for what reaches the value of a shield. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Nadr except Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8635","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us: Imran ibn Harun narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Jarir, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a pledge, he took it upon testifying that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, establishing prayer, giving zakat, hearing and obeying Allah and the Messenger, and sincere advice to every Muslim. No one narrated this hadith from Salamah ibn Kuhayl except Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'id; Ibn Lahi'ah is alone in transmitting it."
"8636","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Imran ibn Harun narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad narrated to me, he heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad informing, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, that she informed him, that her mother Umm Salamah informed her that the daughter of Nu'aym ibn Abdullah al-Adawi came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: My daughter's husband died; she was under al-Mughirah al-Makhzumi, and she complains of her eye; may she apply kohl? He said: No. Then she was silent a while, then said to him again: She complains of her eye; may she apply kohl? He said: No. Then he said: It is not lawful for a woman to mourn more than three days except over a husband. No one narrated this hadith from al-Qasim except Abu al-Aswad; Ibn Lahi'ah is alone in transmitting it."
"8637","Mas'ud ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated to us: Imran ibn Harun narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad narrated to me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from A'ishah, that she informed him that Barirah was under a slave, and when she was freed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: You have more right; if you wish, stay with your husband, and if you wish, separate from him, as long as he has not touched you. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Aswad except Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8638","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Ishaq ibn Asid narrated to me, from Abd al-Karim, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One who strives for his parents to suffice them or free them from need of people is in the path of Allah, and whoever strives for a wife or child to suffice them and free them from need of people is in the path of Allah, and one who strives for himself to free himself and restrain himself from people is in the path of Allah, and one who strives to increase is in the path of Satan. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari except Ishaq ibn Usayd; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it, and it is not narrated from Anas except with this chain."
"8639","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that a man greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in prayer, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned it by gesture; when he finished he said: We used to return greetings in prayer, then we were forbidden from that. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Ajlan except al-Layth."
"8640","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Abdullah ibn Nafi' ibn al-Amya', from Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Prayer is two by two, and you recite tashahhud in every two rak'ahs, and humble yourself, and show awe, and show need, then raise your hands, meaning: you lift them to your Lord, facing your face with their palms, and you say: O Lord, O Lord; whoever does not do that, it is deficient. No one perfected the chain of this hadith among those who narrated it from Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'id except al-Layth. And Shu'bah narrated it from Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'id but differed in its chain."
"8641","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Preserve the affection of your father, do not cut it, lest Allah extinguish your light. No one narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Dinar except Khalid ibn Yazid."
"8642","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever supplicates with these five phrases will not ask Allah for anything but He gives it to him: There is no god but Allah, and Allah is greatest; there is no god but Allah alone without partner, to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He is over all things capable; there is no god but Allah, and there is no power nor strength except with Allah. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Ishaq, from Mu'awiyah, except al-Layth ibn Sa'd."
"8643","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Ziyadah ibn Muhammad al-Ansari narrated to me: Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi narrated to us, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, from Abu al-Darda', he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, blessed and exalted, descends in the last three hours remaining of the night; He looks in the first of them into the Book into which none but He looks, and He erases what He wills and confirms; then He looks in the second hour into the Garden of Eden, which is His dwelling in which none will be with Him but the prophets, the martyrs and the truthful, and in it is what no one has seen nor has occurred to a human heart; then He descends in the last hour of the night and says: Is there a seeker of forgiveness seeking My forgiveness that I may forgive him, is there an asker asking Me that I may give him, is there a supplicant supplicating Me that I may answer him, until dawn rises. That is His saying: And the recitation of dawn; indeed the recitation of dawn is witnessed. Allah and His angels witness it."
"8644","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Ziyadah ibn Muhammad al-Ansari narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, from Abu al-Darda', that a man came to him and mentioned to him that his urine was retained and he was afflicted with bladder stones, so he taught him a ruqyah he had heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Our Lord Allah who is in heaven, sanctified is Your name, Your command is in heaven and earth; as Your mercy is in heaven, place Your mercy on earth, forgive us our sins and our faults, You are the Lord of the good; send down healing from Your healing and mercy from Your mercy upon this pain, so he recovers. He ordered him to perform ruqyah with it, so he did and he recovered. These two hadiths are not narrated from Abu al-Darda' except with this chain; al-Layth ibn Sa'd is alone in transmitting them."
"8645","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Bashir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Baylamani, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever says in the morning: Glory be to Allah when you enter evening and when you enter morning, up to: Thus you will be brought out, attains what he missed in his day, and whoever says them in the evening attains what he missed in his night. This hadith is not narrated from Ibn Abbas except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8646","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me, from Abu al-Aswad, from Ibn Abbas, that some Muslims were with polytheists, swelling the numbers of the polytheists against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so an arrow shot would strike one of them and kill him, whereupon Allah, majestic in grandeur, revealed: Those whom the angels take while wronging themselves, they will say: In what were you, up to His saying: And evil is the destination. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Aswad except al-Layth ibn Sa'd and Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8647","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me, from Shurahbil ibn Sharik, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The world is enjoyment, and the best of its enjoyment is a righteous spouse. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli except Shurahbil ibn Sharik and Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um."
"8648","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me: Ayyub ibn Musa ibn Amr ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asi narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that A'ishah said: The wife of Rifa'ah al-Qurazi came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I married Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Zubayr, and by Allah he has nothing but like this fringe. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps you want to return to Rifa'ah? No, until he tastes your sweetness and you taste his sweetness. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub ibn Musa except al-Layth."
"8649","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me: Ayyub ibn Musa narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A woman is not married over her paternal aunt, nor over her maternal aunt. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub ibn Musa except al-Layth, and none inserted Abd al-Malik ibn Yasar between Sulayman ibn Yasar and Abu Hurayrah except Ayyub ibn Musa. A group narrated it from Bukayr, from Sulayman, from Abu Hurayrah."
"8650","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sulaym ibn Zayd, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he heard Isma'il ibn Bashir, the freedman of Banu Maghalah al-Ansari, say: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah and Abu Ayyub al-Ansari say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No person abandons a Muslim in a place where his honor is diminished and his sanctity violated but Allah will abandon him in a place where he loves His help, and no person helps a Muslim in a place where his honor is diminished and his sanctity violated but Allah will help him in a place where he loves His help. This hadith is not narrated from Jabir and Abu Ayyub except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8651","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sulaym ibn Zayd, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from al-Mus'ab ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Umayyah: Umm Salamah narrated to me that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will come upon people a time when the truthful is declared a liar, the liar is declared truthful, the trustworthy is declared treacherous and the treacherous is trusted, a person will testify though not asked to testify, and a person will swear though not asked to swear, and the happiest of people with the world will be Luka' ibn Luka', who does not believe in Allah and His Messenger. This hadith is not narrated from Umm Salamah except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8652","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn A'yan narrated to me, from Abu Amr al-Abdi, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever apologizes to his brother and he does not excuse him or accept his excuse, upon him will be like the sin of the collector of tolls. Abu al-Zubayr said: The toll collector is the tithe taker. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Zubayr except Abu Amr al-Abdi, nor from Abu Amr except Ibrahim ibn A'yan; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8653","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn A'yan narrated to me, from Bahr al-Saqqa', he said: I heard Abu al-Zubayr narrate from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever honors a Muslim person, he only honors Allah. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Zubayr except Bahr, nor from Bahr except Ibrahim; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8654","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn A'yan narrated to me, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a father looks at his child and it delights him, the child gets the freeing of a slave. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, even if he looks three hundred and sixty times? He said: Allah is greatest. No one narrated this hadith from al-Hakam ibn Aban except Ibrahim ibn A'yan; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it, and it is not narrated from the Messenger of Allah except with this chain."
"8655","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ibrahim narrated to me, from Kharijah ibn Mus'ab, from Suhayl ibn Dhakwan, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No son can repay his father except that he finds him enslaved and frees him. No one narrated this hadith from Kharijah ibn Mus'ab except Ibrahim; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8656","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn A'yan narrated to me, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Ibn al-Qa'qa' who is Umarah, from Abu Zur'ah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, which charity is best? He said: That you give it while you are eager, miserly, hoping for life and fearing poverty, and do not delay until when death comes to you you say: For so-and-so such and for so-and-so such, though it already belonged to him."
"8657","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn A'yan narrated to me, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from Abu Habibah al-Ta'i, from Abu al-Darda', that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of one who gives charity at death, or frees at death, is like one who gives a gift after becoming full."
"8658","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn A'yan narrated to me, from Shu'bah, from Ya'la, from Ata', from Jabir ibn Yazid ibn al-Aswad al-Suwa'i, from his father, he said: We prayed dawn with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina, and there were two men at the back of the mosque who had not prayed. He called them, their limbs trembling, and said: What prevented you from praying with us? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we already prayed in our dwellings. He said: Do not do so; when you have prayed in your dwellings, then come to the imam while he has not prayed, pray with him, for it will be voluntary for you."
"8659","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn A'yan narrated to me, from Abu al-Mu'alla, he said: I heard al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever enters into any of the prices of the Muslims to raise them against them, it is due upon Allah to cast him into the greatest part of the Fire. Al-Layth ibn Sa'd is alone in transmitting these hadiths from Ibrahim ibn A'yan."
"8660","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahim ibn Khalid ibn Zayd, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from al-Awza'i, from Umm Kulthum bint Asma', from A'ishah, she said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day while he was in the mosque standing in prayer, the door ajar on the side facing the qiblah, set apart from the mosque. I sought to open, and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard my voice he reached with his hand and opened the door, then continued his prayer."
"8661","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahim ibn Khalid ibn Zayd, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from al-Awza'i, from Umm Kulthum, from A'ishah, she said: Ali ibn Abi Talib entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying, and stood beside him and prayed with his prayer. A scorpion came until it reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then left him and went toward Ali, so he struck it with his sandal and killed it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw no harm in killing it. These two hadiths are not narrated from al-Awza'i except with this chain; al-Layth ibn Sa'd is alone in transmitting them."
"8662","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani, from his father Ayyash ibn Abbas, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the prayer is established, there is no prayer except the one that was established. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Salamah except Ayyash ibn Abbas, and none narrated it from him except his son Abdullah."
"8663","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah, that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The liar is not one who walks to reconcile between people, conveying good by his words. No one narrated this hadith from Malik except Yahya ibn Ayyub; al-Layth ibn Sa'd is alone in transmitting it."
"8664","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Yasin ibn Mu'adh al-Kufi, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When one of you catches the two rak'ahs on Friday, he has caught Friday; when he catches one rak'ah, let him add another to it, and if he does not catch a rak'ah, let him pray four rak'ahs. No one narrated this hadith with this wording from al-Zuhri except al-Zayyat."
"8665","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Umar ibn Isa al-Qurashi then al-Asadi, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: A slave girl came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: My master accused me, and made me sit on fire until my private part burned. Umar said to her: Did he see that upon you? She said: No. He said: Did you confess anything to him? She said: No. Umar said: Bring him to me. When Umar saw the man he said: Do you punish with the punishment of Allah? He said: O Commander of the Believers, I accused her regarding herself. He said: Did you see that upon her? The man said: No. He said: Did she confess it to you? He said: No. He said: By Him in whose hand is my soul, had I not heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No slave is retaliated against from his owner, nor a child from his father, I would have retaliated her against you. He brought him forward and struck him one hundred lashes, then said: Go, you are free for the sake of Allah, and you are the freed slave of Allah and His Messenger. I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever is burned with fire, or mutilated, is free, and he is the freed slave of Allah and His Messenger. Al-Layth said: This is a matter acted upon. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj except Umar ibn Isa; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8666","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from Muhammad ibn Thabit ibn Shurahbil al-Qurashi, the freedman of Abdullah, that Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi narrated to him, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honor his guest; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honor his neighbor; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not enter the bathhouse except with a waist wrapper; and whoever among your women believes in Allah and the Last Day, let them not enter the bathhouse. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Ayyub except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8667","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, from Qays ibn Nafi', from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that he passed by Mu'adh ibn Jabal while he was standing at his door gesturing with his hand as if speaking to himself. Abdullah ibn Amr said to him: What is the matter with you, O Abu Abd al-Rahman, speaking to yourself? He said: What is wrong with me? The enemy of Allah wants to divert me from what I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to me: You now struggle with your time in your house, will you not go out to the gathering? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever strives in the path of Allah is guaranteed by Allah, and whoever visits a sick person is guaranteed by Allah, mighty and majestic, and whoever goes to the mosque in the morning or evening is guaranteed by Allah, mighty and majestic, and whoever enters upon a ruler to honor him is guaranteed by Allah, and whoever sits in his house without backbiting anyone with evil is guaranteed by Allah. The enemy of Allah wants to take me out of my house to the gathering. This hadith is not narrated from Abdullah ibn Amr, from Mu'adh, except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8668","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr, from Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, she said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque and said: I am burnt. He said: What is that? He said: I had intercourse with my wife, and that was in Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Give charity. He said: I have nothing. He sat down, and a person came driving a donkey laden with food to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is the one who was burnt just now? He said: Here I am. He said: Take this and give it in charity. He said: To one more needy than me, O Messenger of Allah? My family has no food. He said: Then eat it."
"8669","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray twelve rak'ahs in the night; when dawn became clear he prayed two rak'ahs, then lay on his right side until he went out to prayer."
"8670","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I spent the night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he stood and prayed two by two until he reached ten rak'ahs, then prayed witr with three rak'ahs, then lay down and slept until he breathed audibly, and his sleep was audible breathing. Then the mu'adhdhin called him for dawn prayer, so he went out and did not perform wudu."
"8671","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Abu al-Najib, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: We mentioned onions and leeks before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). A man said: There is what is worse than them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is it? He said: Garlic; do you forbid it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No, and whoever eats it should not come near the mosque until its smell goes away."
"8672","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Abu al-Najib, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: A man came from Bahrain to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but he did not return his greeting; he had a gold ring on his hand and wore a silk cloak. The man left grieved and complained to his wife. She said to him: Perhaps the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked your cloak and your ring, so throw them away. He threw them away, then came in the morning to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he returned his greeting. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I came to you just now and you turned away from me? He said: There was in your hand a coal of fire. He said: Then I must have come with many coals. He said: What you came with is of no more benefit to you than the stones of the lava field, but it is the enjoyment of worldly life. He said: With what should I wear a ring? He said: A ring of silver, or iron, or brass."
"8673","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Tawbah ibn Namir, from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, he said: A man freed six slaves of his in his will, and he had no wealth besides them. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he became angry about it, then cast lots among them and freed a third of them."
"8674","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Shu'bah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, or Kurayb the freedman of Ibn Abbas, that Abdullah ibn Abbas passed by Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Rabi'ah while he was praying with his head braided, tied from behind. He stopped over him and did not leave, loosened the knot of his head, and Abdullah ibn al-Harith acknowledged him until he finished loosening it, then sat. When Ibn al-Harith finished his prayer he came to him and said: Why did you do what you did to my head just now? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The example of one who prays with his head tied from behind is like one who prays wrapped up. No one narrated this hadith from Amr ibn al-Harith except al-Layth ibn Sa'd."
"8675","And with the same chain he said: al-Layth narrated to me, Shurahbil ibn Sharik al-Ma'afiri narrated to me, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: An evening stroll or a morning stroll in the path of Allah is better than what the sun rises and sets upon. No one narrated this hadith from Shurahbil except al-Layth ibn Sa'd and Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub."
"8676","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah narrated to me, from his father, from al-Mustawrid, that he said to Amr ibn al-As: Will the Hour come while the Romans are the majority of people? Amr said: Watch what you say. He said: I tell you what I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Amr ibn al-As said: If you said that, there are four traits in them: they are the swiftest of people to attack after retreat, they are the best of people to the poor, destitute and weak, they are the most forbearing of people at tribulation, and the fourth is a fine good trait: they are the most protective of people against the injustice of kings. This hadith is not narrated from al-Mustawrid except with this chain; Musa ibn Ulayy is alone in transmitting it."
"8677","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-A'raj, from Kathir ibn al-Abbas, from al-Hajjaj ibn Amr al-Mazini, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform tahajjud after his sleep, and he used to clean his teeth before tahajjud."
"8678","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, and Ibn Lahi'ah, both, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz, from Kathir ibn al-Abbas, from al-Hajjaj ibn Amr al-Mazini, he said: Does one of you think that when he stands at night praying until morning he has performed tahajjud? Tahajjud is only prayer after sleeping, then prayer after sleeping, then prayer after sleeping; that was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). These two hadiths are not narrated from al-Hajjaj ibn Amr except with this chain; Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah is alone in transmitting them."
"8679","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Abu al-Nadr, from Musa ibn Abdullah ibn Qays, from Abu Rafi', the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while people were around him: Let me not know one of you to whom a matter of my affair comes while he is reclining on his couch, saying: Whatever we found in the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic, we acted upon it. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Nadr, from Musa ibn Abdullah ibn Qays, except al-Layth. And Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated it from Malik ibn Anas, from Abu al-Nadr, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from his father."
"8680","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Qays, the freedman of Amr ibn al-As, from Uthman ibn Affan, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Filth is seventy parts; one part is among the jinn and mankind, and sixty-nine are among the Berbers."
"8681","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib: Abu Hani' Humayd ibn Hani' narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ya'mur al-Kala'i, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Qays, from his father, from Uthman ibn Affan, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah divided filth into seventy parts; He placed sixty-nine parts in the Berbers, and one part for the two heavy creations. This hadith is not narrated from Uthman except with this chain; Yazid ibn Abi Habib is alone in transmitting it from Abu Hani' Humayd ibn Hani'."
"8682","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came one day from the mountain pass after relieving himself, and before us was food touched by fire on a shield or leather mat. We invited him to it, so he sat and ate with us, and did not touch water. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Yazid except al-Layth."
"8683","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Ibn Buhaynah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prostrated would move his hands away from his armpits, so that I could see the whiteness of his armpits. This hadith is not narrated from Ibn Buhaynah except from the hadith of Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, and none narrated it from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah except al-Layth and Bakr ibn Mudar."
"8684","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik called Abdullah ibn Yahya, from his father, from his grandfather, that his grandmother Khayrah, the wife of Ka'b ibn Malik, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with some jewelry of hers and said: I have given this in charity. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman's disposal of her wealth is not permissible except with her husband's permission; have you sought Ka'b's permission? She said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Ka'b and said: Have you permitted al-Khayrah to give this jewelry of hers in charity? He said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted it from her. This hadith is not narrated from Khayrah, the wife of Ka'b ibn Malik, except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8685","And with the same chain he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Rabi'ah: Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated to me, and he was among the righteous Muslims, and said: The sun set while we were with Abdullah ibn Umar, so we traveled; when we saw that evening had come we said: Prayer. He was silent and traveled until the twilight disappeared and the stars came out, then he descended and prayed both prayers together. Then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when traveling urgently pray this prayer of mine, meaning: he combined them after nightfall. No one narrated this hadith from Rabi'ah except al-Layth."
"8686","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani narrated to me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, that Busr ibn Sa'id narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Husayn al-Ashja'i, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, that he said when they killed Uthman ibn Affan: I bear witness that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be a trial; the one sitting back from it is better than the walking, and the walking is better than the striving. It was said to him: What if he enters upon me in my house or stretches his hand to me to kill me? He said: Be like the two sons of Adam. This hadith is not narrated from Sa'd except from the hadith of Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, and none narrated it from Bukayr except Ayyash and Ibn Lahi'ah."
"8687","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Ayyash ibn Abbas narrated to me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, that Busr ibn Sa'id narrated to him, that Abu Waqid al-Laythi said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while we were sitting on a mat: There will be a trial. They said: What shall we do, O Messenger of Allah? He put his hand back to the mat and held it, saying: Do like this. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned to them one day: There will be a trial, but many people did not hear him. Mu'adh ibn Jabal said: Do you not hear what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says? They said: What did he say? He said: There will be a trial. They said: What for us, O Messenger of Allah, or what shall we do? He said: Return to your original affair. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Waqid except from the hadith of Bukayr."
"8688","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Sharik ibn Abdullah, from Husayn ibn Abdullah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray in a single garment, guarding against the heat and cold of the ground with its excess. Al-Layth narrated nothing from Sharik with a chain except this hadith."
"8689","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, and Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prostrate on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except al-Layth and the nephew of al-Zuhri."
"8690","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Time will draw near, knowledge will decrease, trials will appear, and harj will increase. They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is harj? He said: Killing. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri, from Humayd, except al-Layth and the nephew of al-Zuhri."
"8691","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Ya'qub ibn Afif ibn al-Musayyib, that he asked Abu Ayyub, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about a man who prays in his house then comes to the mosque and catches that prayer, should he repeat with the people or not? Abu Ayyub said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: Yes, he repeats it, and that is a share of gathering. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Ayyub except with this chain; Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj is alone in transmitting it."
"8692","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that he said: Tamim al-Dari informed me, or I was informed that Tamim al-Dari, prayed two rak'ahs after Umar ibn al-Khattab's prohibition of prayer after Asr, so Umar came to him and struck him with the whip. Tamim gestured to him to sit while in his prayer, so Umar sat until Tamim finished. He said to Umar: Why did you strike me? He said: Because you prayed these two rak'ahs though I forbade them. He said: I prayed them with one better than you, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umar said: I am not concerned with you, O group, but I fear that after you will come people who pray between Asr and Maghrib until they pass through the hour in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer, as they pray between Zuhr and Asr, then they will say: We saw so-and-so praying after Asr. This hadith is not narrated from Tamim al-Dari except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8693","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Yazid, the freedman of al-Munba'ith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Messenger of Allah, inform me about lost property. He said: Know its container and its string, and announce it for a year; if its seeker comes, return it to him, otherwise spend it. He said: Inform me about stray sheep? He said: Take it, for it is for you, or for your brother, or for the wolf. He said: Inform me about stray camels? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry until his cheeks reddened, then said: What have you to do with it? It has its shoes and its water, it comes to water and eats trees; leave it until its owner comes to it. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub ibn Musa except Yahya ibn Ayyub; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8694","Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan al-Basri, from Zayd ibn Abdullah, he said: We presented to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a ruqyah for fever, and he permitted us with it. He said: It is only covenants. The ruqyah is: In the name of Allah, a piercing horned wound, sea salt, qafta. This hadith is not narrated from Zayd ibn Abdullah except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8695","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Zuhrah ibn Ma'bad, from his father, from A'ishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to pray wherever near the House; A'ishah said to him: O Messenger of Allah, sometimes you pray in a place through which a menstruating woman passes, so if only you took a prayer place to pray in. He said: How strange of you, O A'ishah, do you not know that a believer's prostration purifies its place down to seven earths. Ma'bad narrated no hadith from A'ishah besides this, and none narrated it from Ma'bad except his son Zuhrah; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8696","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have anyone take his hand and pull his hand away from his hand until the man was the one to release him, and his knee would not be seen protruding beyond his companion's knee, and no one would speak to him but he turned to him with his face, then did not turn it away until he finished his speech. No one narrated this hadith from Sa'id al-Maqburi except al-Layth."
"8697","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqbah ibn Amir, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, one of us sins? He said: It is written against him. He said: Then he seeks forgiveness from it and repents? He said: He is forgiven and his repentance accepted. He said: Then he returns and sins? He said: It is written against him. He said: Then he seeks forgiveness from it and repents? He said: He is forgiven and his repentance accepted, and Allah does not tire until you tire. This hadith is not narrated from Uqbah except with this chain; Yazid ibn Abi Habib is alone in transmitting it."
"8698","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The killer does not inherit. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Ishaq ibn Abdullah; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8699","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Aban ibn Salih, from Ali ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas, from his father, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, that he said: One day I was in the mosque, and Abu Jahl came forward and said: Allah has a claim upon me that if I see Muhammad prostrating I will step on his neck. I went out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until I entered upon him and informed him of Abu Jahl's words. He went out angry until he entered the mosque, hastened to enter from the door and leapt over the wall. I said: This is a day of evil, so I wrapped my waist wrapper then followed him. I entered while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was reciting: Read in the name of your Lord who created, created man from a clot. When he reached the matter of Abu Jahl: No, indeed man transgresses, because he sees himself self-sufficient, a person said to Abu Jahl: O Abu al-Hakam, this is Muhammad. Abu Jahl said: Do you not see what I see? By Allah, the horizon of the sky is blocked for me. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached the end of the surah, he prostrated. This hadith is not narrated from al-Abbas except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in transmitting it."
"8700","And with the same chain: al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Aban ibn Salih, from Mujahid Abu al-Hajjaj, from Tawus, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: It is due upon every Muslim every seven days to purify himself one day and wash his head. No one narrated this hadith from Amr ibn al-Harith except al-Layth and Ibn Wahb."
"8701","Al-Layth narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abi Farwah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Prophet of Allah, my daughter is a bride, measles has seized her and her hair has fallen out, so may I add hair extensions to it? He said: Allah has cursed the woman who attaches hair and the woman who has it attached. No one narrated this hadith from Amr except al-Layth."
"8702","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr; Bashir ibn Abi Masud al-Ansari informed me, from Abu Masud, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Jibril came down to me, and I prayed with him, then I prayed with him, then I prayed with him, then I prayed with him, then I prayed with him. Then I saw the Messenger of Allah afterwards praying Zuhr when the sun declined, sometimes delaying it in intense heat, and Asr while the sun was white and high, so that a man leaving it could walk to Dhu al-Hulayfah, six miles, before sunset, and Maghrib when the sun had set, and Isha when the horizon darkened, and Fajr early in the darkness, then one day he prayed it spreading the light, then he did not return to spreading the light until Allah took him. No one narrated this hadith from Usamah ibn Zayd except Yazid ibn Abi Habib; al-Layth is alone in it, and none of those who narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri specified the times except Usamah ibn Zayd."
"8703","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me, from al-Hakam ibn Abdullah, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from his father Abdullah: A man from the Ansar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: You commanded us to give charity, the charity of Fitr, and we pay it, so what of us if we live to see rulers who do not place it in its proper places? He said: Pay it to your rulers, for they will be held to account for it. No one narrated this hadith from Salim except al-Hakam ibn Abdullah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8704","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Kathir ibn Farqad, that Abdullah ibn Malik ibn Hudhafah narrated to him, from his mother al-Aliyah bint Subay, that Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to her: Some men of Quraysh passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dragging a sheep of theirs like a donkey, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: If only you took its hide? They said: It is dead. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Water and qaraz (tanning agent) purify it. This hadith is not narrated from al-Aliyah bint Subay, from Maymunah, except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8705","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid, from Sulayman ibn Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit who said: A group of men entered upon Zayd ibn Thabit and said: Narrate to us some of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: What shall I narrate to you? I was his neighbor, and when revelation came down he would send for me and I would write the revelation, and when we mentioned the Hereafter he would mention it with us, and when we mentioned this world he would mention it with us, and if we mentioned food he would mention it with us; so is all of this what I should narrate to you about him? No one narrated this hadith from Sulayman ibn Kharijah except al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8706","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me, from Ishaq Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Raja ibn Haywah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: A little understanding is better than much worship, and it suffices a man in understanding when he worships Allah, and it suffices a man in ignorance when he is conceited with his own opinion. People are but two men: a believer and an ignorant one, so do not harm the believer and do not keep company with the ignorant. No one narrated this hadith from Raja ibn Haywah except Ishaq Abu Abd al-Rahman; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8707","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Musab ibn Thabit, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa al-Ashari, from his father Abu Musa who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no believer but that he will come on the Day of Resurrection with a Jew or a Christian, and it will be said: This is your ransom from the Fire. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir except Musab ibn Thabit."
"8708","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Ibn Lahiah narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ikrimah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is killed defending his wealth is a martyr. No one narrated this hadith from Yazid ibn Abi Habib except Ibn Lahiah."
"8709","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Harmalah ibn Imran narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shammasah al-Mahri who said: I heard Abu Dharr say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will conquer a land in which qirats (a measure) are mentioned, so treat its people well, for they have a covenant of protection and kinship; and if you see two men fighting over the place of a brick, then leave it. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Dharr except with this chain; Harmalah ibn Imran is alone in it."
"8710","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Ibn Lahiah narrated to me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar who said: We used to give precedence among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Abu Bakr, and Umar, and Uthman, then the people were equal, and that would reach the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he would not disapprove of that from us. None of those who narrated this hadith from Nafi said: then that would reach the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he would not disapprove, except Bukayr."
"8711","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dowry of the adulteress is unlawful gain, and the price of a dog, except a hunting dog, is unlawful gain. This phrase, except a hunting dog, is not narrated except in this hadith; al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah is alone in it."
"8712","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: When the people fled from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, there remained with him eleven men from the Ansar, and Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, while he was climbing the mountain, and the polytheists caught up with them. He said: Is there no one for these? Talhah ibn Ubaydillah said: I, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Remain as you are, O Talhah. Then a man from the Ansar said: I, O Messenger of Allah, so he stood away from him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) climbed with those who remained with him, then the Ansari was killed and they caught up with him. He said: Is there no one for these? Talhah said the same as his first saying, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him the same as his saying, then a man from the Ansar said: I, O Messenger of Allah, so he permitted him, and he fought like the fighting of those before him, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) climbed and his Companions climbed, then he was killed, and they caught up. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying the same as his first saying, and Talhah would say: I, O Messenger of Allah, and he would hold him back, and a man from the Ansar would ask his permission to fight, and he would permit him, and he would fight like those before him, until none remained with him except Talhah. Then they overwhelmed them both, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is for these? Talhah said: I, so he fought like the fighting of all those before him, and one of his fingertips was injured, and he said: Hass (an exclamation of pain). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Talhah, had you said: In the name of Allah, or mentioned Allah, the angels would have raised you while the people watched, until they entered you into the expanse of the sky. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Talhah climbed to his Companions while they were gathered. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Zubayr except Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah; Yahya is alone in it."
"8713","Muttalib narrated to me: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ayyash ibn Abbas, from Wahib ibn Abdullah al-Maafiri, from Uqbah ibn Amir: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever finds a Muslim exposed in a fault and conceals it, it is as if he gave life to a buried girl from her grave. No one narrated this hadith from Wahib ibn Abdullah except Ayyash; Yahya ibn Ayyub is alone in it."
"8714","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Said ibn Abi Said, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: I say nothing but the truth. Those around him said: You joke with us, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I say nothing but the truth. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Ajlan except Yahya ibn Ayyub; Abdullah ibn Salih is alone in it."
"8715","With the same chain: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ubaydillah, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from al-Mustawrid, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of this world compared to the Hereafter is only as when one of you dips his finger into the sea, then takes it out; with what does it return? No one narrated this hadith from Abu Ishaq except Ubaydillah ibn Zahr, and none from Ubaydillah except Yahya ibn Ayyub."
"8716","With the same chain: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man standing in the row in the path of Allah is better than sixty years of worship. None narrated it from Hisham except Yahya; Abdullah is alone in it."
"8717","With the same chain: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ubaydillah ibn Zahr, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Hanash al-Sanani, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Islam is three hundred and thirteen paths (shariah); there is no path among them that its follower meets Allah with except that he enters Paradise by it. No one narrated this hadith from Hanash except Khalid ibn Abi Imran, and none from Khalid except Ubaydillah; Yahya ibn Ayyub is alone in it."
"8718","With the same chain: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from al-Aswad ibn al-Ala ibn Jariyah and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah and Kathir ibn Husayn, that they disputed about amputation, so they entered upon Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, and she narrated to them, from Aishah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no amputation except for a quarter of a dinar. No one narrated this hadith from al-Aswad ibn al-Ala, Abu Salamah, Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah, and Kathir ibn Husayn except Jafar ibn Rabiah; Yahya ibn Ayyub is alone in it."
"8719","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me: Jafar ibn Rabiah narrated to me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Abu Hamzah al-Khawlani, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah was placed in command over us in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and hunger struck us, so he slaughtered for us seven camels. Then we came down to the seashore, and there was an enormous whale, so we stayed at it three days, and carried from it whatever fat we wished in waterskins and sacks, and we traveled until we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of that. He said: Had we known we would reach it before it perished, we would have loved to have some of it with us. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Hamzah al-Khawlani except Bakr ibn Sawadah, and none from Bakr except Jafar ibn Rabiah; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8720","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abi Imran wrote to me: Abu Ayyash narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The heart of the son of Adam is only between two fingers of the fingers of the Most Merciful."
"8721","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me: Yahya ibn Said narrated to me, saying: Khalid ibn Abi Imran wrote to me: Abu Ayyash narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no shepherd entrusted with a flock except that he will be asked about it on the Day of Resurrection: did he uphold the command of Allah in it or neglect it."
"8722","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me, saying: Yahya ibn Said said: From Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Abdullah ibn Rafi, freedman of Umm Salamah, from Umm Salamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and said: O people. She said: I heard it while I was combing my hair, so I ordered my hairdresser to stop my head, then I advanced until I was in the nearest part of the chamber. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, I am your forerunner at the Hawd (Pool), and you will be brought to me in groups and then driven away from me, so I will say: Where? And it will be said: They changed after you, so I will say: Away, away."
"8723","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, saying: Yahya ibn Said said: Abu Hazim narrated to me, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Muadh ibn Jabal, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Beware of three things: the slip of a scholar, the argument of a hypocrite, and a world that cuts your necks. As for the slip of a scholar, if he is guided then do not blindly follow him in your religion, and if he slips then do not cut off your hopes from him. As for the argument of a hypocrite with the Quran, the Quran has landmarks like the landmarks of the road; what you recognize, take it, and what you do not recognize, refer it to its scholar. As for a world that cuts your necks, whoever Allah places richness in his heart is the rich one."
"8724","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me: Yahya ibn Said narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran: Abu Ayyash narrated to me, saying: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam began strange, and it will return strange, so glad tidings to the strangers. He said: And who are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Those who set right when the people corrupt."
"8725","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Said, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran: Ibn al-Baylamani narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Farrukh, that Abu Hurayrah informed him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be a deaf, dumb, blind tribulation; whoever exposes himself to it, it will expose itself to him; the tongue in it is like the striking of the sword. None narrated these hadiths from Yahya ibn Said except al-Layth ibn Sad."
"8726","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Khalid ibn Kathir al-Hamdani narrated to him, that al-Sari ibn Ismail narrated to him, that al-Shabi narrated to him, that he heard al-Numan ibn Bashir say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: From wheat comes wine, and from barley comes wine, and from raisins comes wine, and from dates comes wine, and from grapes comes wine, and from honey comes wine, and I forbid every intoxicant. No one narrated this hadith from Khalid ibn Kathir except Yazid ibn Abi Habib; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8727","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Nafi, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When two men trade, each of them has the choice as long as they have not separated and are together, or one of them gives the other the choice; if one of them gives the other the choice and they trade on that basis, the sale becomes binding, and if they separate after trading, even if neither of them renounces the sale, the sale is binding. No one narrated this hadith in its entirety from Nafi except al-Layth."
"8728","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari who said: I heard the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No prophet was sent but that after him was a successor, and he has two inner circles: an inner circle commanding him to good and forbidding him evil, and an inner circle that does not spare him corruption; so whoever is protected from the evil inner circle has been protected. No one narrated this hadith from Safwan ibn Sulaym except Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar; al-Layth is alone in it, and none narrated it from Abu Salamah, from Abu Ayyub, except Safwan ibn Sulaym. Al-Zuhri narrated it from Abu Salamah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, and Yazid ibn Sinan narrated it from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah."
"8729","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Fudayl who said: I heard Muawiyah, with a lock of womens hair with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever adds to his hair something that is not from it, he adds falsehood to it. No one narrated this hadith from Safwan except Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8730","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me: Safwan ibn Sulaym informed me, from Abu Said, meaning al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, blessed and exalted, said: My servants did not visit Me when I was sick, and My servants did not give Me drink when I was thirsty. He said: You, my Lord? He said: Yes, My believing servant falls sick, and had he been visited, I would have been visited; and My servant thirsts in the desert, and had he been given drink, I would have been given drink. No one narrated this hadith from Safwan ibn Sulaym except Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8731","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to us: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, from Abu Murawih, from Abu Dharr, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Which deeds are best? He said: Faith in Allah, and jihad in the path of Allah. He said: Then which necks (slaves to free) are best? He said: The most expensive in price, and the most precious to its owners. He said: What if I cannot do some of the work? He said: Then you help a craftsman, or make something for one lacking skill. He said: What if I am weak? He said: Then you spare the people from evil, for it is a charity you give for yourself."
"8732","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Qatadah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The righteous vision is from Allah, and the bad dream is from Satan; so whoever sees something he dislikes, let him spit to his left three times and seek refuge from Satan, for it will not harm him, and Satan does not appear in my likeness."
"8733","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, saying: I heard Hamzah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar say: I heard Abdullah ibn Umar say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man keeps begging the people until he comes on the Day of Resurrection with not a shred of flesh on his face. And he said: The sun will draw near on the Day of Resurrection until sweat reaches half the ear; while they are like that they will seek help from Adam, and he will say: I am not fit for that, then Musa, and he will say likewise, then Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he will intercede, and judgment will be passed among creation, and he will walk until he takes hold of the ring of Paradise; on that day Allah will raise him to a praised station, for which all the gathered people will praise him."
"8734","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, saying: Bukayr ibn Abdullah al-Ashajj said: Hanash al-Sanani narrated to me, from Fudalah ibn Ubayd who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Khaybar, selling the Jews a patch of gold for two or three dinars, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold except weight for weight."
"8735","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, from Zaynab al-Thaqafiyyah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whichever of you women goes out to the mosque, let her not come near perfume."
"8736","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to sleep while in a state of major impurity, he would wash his private part, then perform ablution as for prayer."
"8737","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, Ubaydillah narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Abdullah, freedman of the Jundaiyyin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: No prize (for racing) is lawful except on a hoof (horse) or a camel foot."
"8738","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah narrated to me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever frees a slave, his wealth belongs to him, unless the master stipulates his wealth, in which case it belongs to him."
"8739","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah narrated to me, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, from Urwah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The best charity is from surplus wealth; begin with those you support."
"8740","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Ubaydillah narrated to me, from Abu al-Aswad, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: If a man says to another: O disbeliever, disbelief must apply to one of them. None narrated these hadiths from Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar except al-Layth."
"8741","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever calls the adhan for twelve years, Paradise becomes obligatory for him, and sixty good deeds are written for him for each day of his adhan, and thirty good deeds for each iqamah. No one narrated this hadith from Nafi except Ibn Jurayj, and none from Ibn Jurayj except Yahya ibn Ayyub; Abdullah ibn Salih is alone in it."
"8742","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar and Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: We sacrificed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a camel for seven. We said to Jabir: And the cow? He said: It is like it. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, except Yahya ibn Ayyub."
"8743","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Abbad ibn Kathir, that Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani informed him, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led the people in prayer and took off his sandals; when the people sensed it they took off their sandals. When he finished the prayer he turned to the people and said: An angel came to me and informed me that there was filth in my sandals; so when one of you comes to the door of the mosque, let him turn over his sandals, and if he sees anything in them let him wipe them, then let him pray in them if he wishes, or let him take them off. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub, from Muhammad, except Abbad ibn Kathir; Yahya ibn Ayyub is alone in it. Dawud al-Attar narrated it from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said; and Abd al-Razzaq narrated it from Mamar, from Ayyub, from a man, from Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8744","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ubaydillah ibn Zahr, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, from Abis al-Ghifari, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was on a roof and saw the people departing, and said: What is the matter with the people? It was said: They are departing because of the plague. He said: O plague, take me. His nephew said to him: Do you wish for death, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not wish for death, for it cuts off deeds and does not leave a man to be given a chance to make amends? He said: I fear I may live to see six things I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention: injustice in judgment, disregard for blood, the rule of fools, severing of kinship, a multitude of policemen, and the people putting forward a man who is neither the best nor the most knowledgeable among them to chant the Quran. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Umamah, from Abis, except with this chain; Yahya ibn Ayyub is alone in it."
"8745","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Umar ibn al-Saib, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Yaqub ibn al-Ashajj, from Said ibn Abi Said, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah will not take knowledge away from you after He has given it to you by stripping it away, but He will take the scholars with their knowledge, and ignorant people will remain and be asked, and they will give verdicts and go astray and lead astray. No one narrated this hadith from Said al-Maqburi except Yaqub ibn al-Ashajj, and none from Yaqub except Usamah ibn Zayd, and none from Usamah except Umar ibn al-Saib; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8746","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade muhaqalah, muzabanah, mukhabarah, and renting out land for payment, and said: Either you cultivate it, or you give it to your brothers to cultivate, or you leave it. Ishaq ibn Abi Farwah narrated nothing else from Abu al-Zubayr; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8747","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Ishaq ibn Abi Farwah, from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman and Abd al-Malik ibn Ibrahim ibn Qariz, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that a man from the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, all my wealth is charity. Then his parents became poor until they sat with the lowly rabble. Then they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, our son was among the wealthiest of the Ansar, then he gave his wealth in charity, and we became poor until we sat with the rabble. He said: Your sons charity is returned to you. Then they died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to their son: Return the charity, for charity is neither inherited nor given as a life-grant. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Hurayrah except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8748","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Abu Shurayh Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh al-Maafiri narrated to me, that he heard Umayrah ibn Abdullah al-Maafiri say: My father narrated to me, that he heard Amr ibn al-Hamiq say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be a tribulation in which the safest of people — or he said: the best of people — will be the western army. Ibn al-Hamiq said: That is why I came to you in Egypt. This hadith is not narrated from Amr ibn al-Hamiq except with this chain; Abu Shurayh is alone in it."
"8749","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Abu Shurayh narrated to me, from Abu al-Sabbah Muhammad ibn Shumayr, from Abu Ali al-Hamdani, from Abu Rayhanah, that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign, and one night we took shelter at a high place, and severe cold struck us there, until we saw men, one of them digging a pit, entering it and covering himself with his shield. When the Messenger of Allah saw that from them he said: Who will guard us tonight, that I may supplicate to Allah for him with a supplication that attains its bounty? A man stood and said: I, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Who are you? He said: I am so-and-so son of so-and-so al-Ansari. He said: Come near. He drew near to him and he took hold of part of his garment, then began the supplication. Abu Rayhanah said: When I heard what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for the Ansari, I stood and said: I am a man, O Messenger of Allah. He asked me as he had asked him, and said to me: Come near, as he had said to him, and he supplicated for me with a supplication less than what he supplicated for the Ansari. Then he said: The Fire is forbidden for an eye that stayed awake in the path of Allah, and the Fire is forbidden for an eye that shed tears from fear of Allah. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Rayhanah except with this chain; Abu Shurayh is alone in it."
"8750","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Abu Shurayh narrated to me, from Abu Hani, from Abu Ali, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever is pleased with Allah as Lord, and with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad as messenger, Paradise becomes obligatory for him. Abu Said said: So I praised Allah and exalted Him and bore witness, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And another thing, O Abu Said, by which Allah raises its people in Paradise one hundred degrees, between every two degrees like between heaven and earth. I said: What is that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Jihad in the path of Allah. Abu Shurayh narrated this hadith thus from Abu Hani, from Abu Ali; and al-Layth ibn Sad and Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated it from Abu Hani, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Said."
"8751","With the same chain: Abu Shurayh narrated to me, that he heard Musa ibn Wardan say: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Hour will not come until you fight a people with red faces and small eyes, as if their faces are hammered shields."
"8752","With the same chain: Abu Shurayh narrated to me, from Musa ibn Wardan or another, from Abu Hurayrah who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of the gardens, and the Dajjal was mentioned, and he brought his matter close, until some of us would turn, thinking he had already overwhelmed them. None narrated these two hadiths from Musa ibn Wardan except Abu Shurayh."
"8753","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Huwayrith, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, mighty and majestic, tests His servant with sickness until He expiates from him every sin. This hadith is not narrated from Jubayr ibn Mutim except with this chain; Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman is alone in it."
"8754","Muttalib narrated to us, saying: Abdullah narrated to us: Ibn Lahiah narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gentleness in earning a living is better than some trade. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir except Ibn Lahiah; Abdullah ibn Salih is alone in it, and it is not narrated from Jabir except with this chain."
"8755","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Harmalah ibn Imran narrated to me, that Abu al-Sumayt Said ibn Abi Said al-Mahri narrated to him, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that Muadh ibn Jabal said: O Messenger of Allah, advise me. He said: Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, give me more. He said: When you do wrong, do good. He said: O Messenger of Allah, give me more. He said: Be upright, and let your character be good. No one narrated this hadith from Said ibn al-Mahri except Harmalah ibn Imran."
"8756","With the same chain, Harmalah narrated to me: Kab ibn Alqamah narrated to me, that Ghurfah ibn al-Harith, who had companionship and fought with Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl in Yemen during the apostasy, was passed by a Christian from the people of Egypt called al-Mandaqun, and he invited him to Islam. The Christian mentioned the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and insulted him. That was reported to Amr ibn al-As, so he sent for them and said: We have given them a covenant. Ghurfah said: Allah forbid that covenants and pledges should mean they harm us regarding Allah and His Messenger; we only gave them covenant that they be left with their churches to say therein what they wish, that we not burden them with what they cannot bear, that we fight in their defense, and that we leave them to their laws, unless they come to us, in which case we judge between them by what Allah revealed. Amr ibn al-As said: You spoke the truth. This hadith is not narrated from Ghurfah ibn al-Harith except with this chain; Harmalah ibn Imran is alone in it."
"8757","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Rabiah ibn Sayf who said: We were with Shufayy al-Asbahi who said: Abdullah ibn Amr narrated to us, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will be twelve caliphs after me; among them is Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, who will not remain after me except a short while, and the master of the millstone of the Arabs, who will live praiseworthy and die a martyr. A man said: Who is he? He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to Uthman ibn Affan and said: O Uthman, if Allah clothes you with a shirt and the people want you to take it off, do not take it off; for by Allah, if you take it off you will not see Paradise until the camel enters the eye of the needle. This hadith is not narrated from Abdullah ibn Amr except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8758","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Nafi, that Abdullah ibn Umar informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go out on the day of Fitr and Adha with a spear, then plant it before him until he stood to pray. No one narrated this hadith from Said ibn Abi Hilal except Khalid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8759","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The jihad of the old, the weak, and the woman is Hajj and Umrah. No one narrated this hadith from Yazid except Said ibn Abi Hilal, and none from Said except Khalid ibn Yazid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8760","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, that Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki informed him that he saw Abdullah ibn Umar, when he prostrated, upon raising his head from the first prostration, sit on the tips of his toes, saying: It is from the Sunnah. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Zubayr except Ibn Ajlan, and none narrated it from Ibn Ajlan except Said ibn Abi Hilal, and none narrated it from Said except Khalid ibn Yazid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8761","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Ayyub ibn Musa, that Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informed him, that Thabit al-Bunani informed him, that Anas ibn Malik informed him, that he was at the knee of the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage, and when she rose with him he said: Labbayk for Umrah and Hajj. No one narrated this hadith from Said ibn Abi Hilal except Khalid ibn Yazid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8762","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me: Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Ibn al-Munkadir, that Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf informed him, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought dabb (lizard) meat while he was in the house of Maymunah, with Khalid ibn al-Walid present. Maymunah said: Inform the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what it is. When he was informed he left it. Khalid said to him: Is it forbidden, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No, but I find it distasteful. So Khalid took to gnawing its bones. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir except Said ibn Abi Hilal, and none narrated it from Said except Khalid ibn Yazid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8763","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Qatadah ibn Diamah, from Anas ibn Malik, that he narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha, and slept a sleep at Mina, then rode to the House and circumambulated it. No one narrated this hadith from Said ibn Abi Hilal except Khalid ibn Yazid; al-Layth is alone in it, and nothing is narrated from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Qatadah, from Anas, other than this hadith."
"8764","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed two prostrations on the day of Dhu al-Shimalayn after the salutation. None of those who narrated this hadith inserted Abd al-Karim between Ayyub and Ibn Sirin except Said ibn Abi Hilal; al-Layth is alone in it from Khalid ibn Yazid."
"8765","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Jafar ibn Abdullah al-Ansari, from al-Hakam ibn Abi al-Hakam al-Ansari, that he narrated to him, that Ibn Hurmuz narrated to him, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would kiss while fasting. None narrated this hadith from al-Araj except al-Hakam ibn Muslim."
"8766","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, that he asked her about the verse from Surah al-Nisa: And if you fear that you cannot do justice to the orphans. She said: It was revealed about the orphan girl who is with a man and is his partner in wealth, so he marries her for less than she deserves, then treats her badly."
"8767","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, about the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: They ask you for a ruling about women; say: Allah gives you a ruling about them, and what is recited to you in the Book: It was only revealed about a man who has an orphan girl under his authority; he does not marry her, nor leaves her, but keeps her until she dies and he inherits her. None narrated these two hadiths from Yazid ibn al-Had except Said ibn Abi Hilal."
"8768","With the same chain: He said: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed that I say: I shall fast the whole year and stand the whole night. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Are you the one who says: I shall fast the whole year and stand the whole night as long as I live? I said: I have said that, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: You cannot do that; so fast and break fast, and stand and sleep, and fast three days of the month, for a good deed is ten like it; that is like fasting the whole year. I said: I can do better than that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fast one day and break fast one day; that is the fast of Dawud (peace be upon him), and it is the most just of fasting. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I can do better than that. He said: There is nothing better than that."
"8769","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Ibn Shihab, that Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman informed him that he heard Muawiyah, while addressing the people in Madinah, say: O people of Madinah, where are your scholars? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: This is the day of Ashura, and its fasting has not been written upon you. Muawiyah said: I am fasting, so whoever wishes to fast let him fast, and whoever wishes to break fast let him break fast — meaning himself thereby."
"8770","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Ibn Shihab: Abu Abdullah al-Agharr narrated to us, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When it is Friday, there are angels at every door of the mosque writing the first and then the first; when the imam sits they fold the scrolls and come to listen to the remembrance. None narrated these hadiths from Said ibn Abi Hilal except Khalid; al-Layth is alone in them."
"8771","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Khalid narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Aban ibn Salih, from Abu al-Nadr, from Urwah, from Aishah, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that your people are close to disbelief, O Aishah, I would have ordered the Kabah to be demolished, and I would have joined its door to the ground, and made in it two doors. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Nadr except Aban, and none from Aban except Said ibn Abi Hilal; al-Layth is alone in it from Khalid."
"8772","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me: Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Yazid ibn Ruman, from Ibn Abbas who said: I wanted to ask Umar ibn al-Khattab about the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: And if you two back each other against him. I was in awe of him until we performed Hajj with him, and I said: If I do not ask him in this Hajj I will never ask him. When we completed our Hajj we caught up with him at Batn Marr, having stayed behind for some need. He said: Welcome, O son of the uncle of the Messenger of Allah, what is your need? I said: Something I wanted to ask you about, O Commander of the Faithful, but I was in awe of you. He said: Ask me what you wish, for we knew nothing until we learned. I said: Inform me about the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: And if you two back each other against him — who are they? He said: Do not ask anyone more knowledgeable about that than me. In Makkah our wives would not talk to us; they were but servants of the house, and when one had a need he would nudge her with his foot and fulfill his need from her. When we came to Madinah they learned from the women of the Ansar, and began talking to us and answering us back. I ordered some servant boys of mine for some need, and my wife said: Rather do such-and-such. I rose to her with a stick and struck her with it. She said: How strange of you, O son of al-Khattab, you want not to be talked to, while the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) talk to him. So I went out and entered upon Hafsah and said: My daughter, see that you do not talk to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about anything, nor ask him, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has neither dinars nor dirhams to give you; whatever need you have, even the oil for your head, ask me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prayed Fajr, would sit in his prayer place with the people around him until the sun rose, then enter upon his wives one by one, greeting them and supplicating for them; when it was the day of one of them he would stay with her. A skin of honey from Taif or Makkah was gifted to Hafsah bint Umar, and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her greeting, she would detain him until she fed him some of it or gave him some to drink. Aishah disapproved of his staying with her, so she said to a young Abyssinian slave girl with her called Khadra: When he enters upon Hafsah, enter and see what he does. The slave girl informed her what he did with the honey. Aishah sent to her companions and informed them, saying: When he enters upon you, say: We smell maghafir (a foul-smelling gum) from you. Then he entered upon Aishah, and she said: O Messenger of Allah, have you eaten anything today? I smell maghafir from you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked nothing more than that a smell be found from him. He said: It is honey; by Allah I shall never taste it again. When it was Hafsahs day, she said: O Messenger of Allah, I need to see my father; I have some maintenance with him, so permit me to go to him. He permitted her. Then he sent for Mariyah, his slave girl, and brought her into Hafsahs house and lay with her. Hafsah came and found the door locked, so she sat at the door. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out alarmed, his face dripping with sweat, while Hafsah wept. He said: What makes you weep? She said: You only permitted me for this; you brought your slave girl into my house then lay with her on my bed. You would not do this with any of them. By Allah, this is not lawful for you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: By Allah, you are not truthful; is she not my slave girl whom Allah made lawful for me? I bear witness to you that she is forbidden for me, seeking thereby your pleasure; see that you do not inform any of them of this, for it is a trust with you. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left, Hafsah knocked on the wall between her and Aishah and said: Shall I not give you glad tidings? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has forbidden his slave girl, and Allah has relieved us of her. Aishah said: By Allah, I had suspected he stayed at midday because of her. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: O Prophet, why do you forbid what Allah made lawful for you. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: And if you two back each other against him — they are Aishah and Hafsah. They claimed the two would conceal nothing from one another. I had a brother from the Ansar: when I attended and he was absent at one of his estates, I would inform him of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and when I was absent at one of my estates he would inform me. He came to me one day — we had been fearing Jabalah ibn al-Ayham al-Ghassani — and said: Do you know what happened? I said: What is it, is Jabalah ibn al-Ayham al-Ghassani mentioned? He said: No, but it is worse than that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Fajr and did not sit as he used to sit, nor enter upon his wives as he used to do; he has withdrawn to his upper chamber, and I left the people surging, not knowing his affair. So I came while the people in the mosque were surging, not knowing, and I said: O people, stay as you are. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in his upper chamber, for which a small ladder had been made so he climbed it. I said to a black boy of his who used to screen him: Ask permission for Umar ibn al-Khattab. He asked permission for me and I entered, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his upper chamber containing a mat and tanned skins hanging, his side having touched the mat so the mat left marks on his side, and under his head a leather pillow stuffed with fiber. When I saw him I wept. He said: What makes you weep? I said: O Messenger of Allah, Persia and Rome, one of them reclines in brocade and silk. He said: They have had their good things hastened in this world, and the Hereafter is for us. Then I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is your affair? I left the people surging among themselves. Is it news that came to you that you withdrew from them? He said: No, but there is something between me and my wives, so I swore not to enter upon them for a month. Then I went out to the people and said: O people, return, for there was something between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his wives and he wished to withdraw. Then I entered upon Hafsah and said: My daughter, do you talk to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), angering him and being jealous over him? She said: I shall never again talk to him about anything he dislikes. Then I entered upon Umm Salamah, who was my maternal aunt, and said to her as I said to Hafsah. She said: How strange of you, O Umar ibn al-Khattab, you speak about everything, even wanting to intervene between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his wives. What prevents us from being jealous over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when your wives are jealous over you? Then Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: O Prophet, say to your wives: If you desire the life of this world and its adornment, come, I shall provide for you and release you with a handsome release, until the end of the verse. No one narrated this hadith from Yazid ibn Ruman except Said ibn Abi Hilal, and none from Said except Khalid ibn Yazid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8773","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to me: Al-Layth narrated to me, Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from al-Qurazi, from Anas ibn Malik who said: Abu Talhah came to Umm Sulaym, who is the mother of Anas, Abu Talhah being his stepfather, and said: Do you have anything, O Umm Sulaym? For I passed by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was teaching the people of the Suffah Surah al-Nisa, having bound a stone on his belly from hunger. She said: I had some barley. She said: I ground it, then sent me to the markets, the markets being their walled gardens, and I brought them some firewood, and made from it a round loaf. Then he said: Do you have any condiment? She said: I had a skin with ghee in it, and I do not know if anything remained in it, so she brought it and squeezed it. He said: The squeezing of two is more effective than the squeezing of one, so they both squeezed together and produced about a dates worth. She said: She greased the loaf, then called me and said: My son, do you know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He said: I left him with the people of the Suffah teaching them, so invite him and do not invite anyone else with him; see that you do not disgrace me. So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when he saw me he said: Perhaps your father sent to us? I said: Yes. He said to the people: Go forth. They went forth that day, eighty men, and he held my hand. When I drew near the house I pulled my hand from his, then I came to him and informed him of the news, and he began seeking me in the house and throwing stones at me, saying: You disgraced me before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he went out to him and informed him of the news. He said: It will not harm you. He ordered them and they sat, then he entered, and we brought the loaf. He said: Is there any condiment? Umm Sulaym said: O Messenger of Allah, we had a skin, and Abu Talhah and I squeezed it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring it, for the squeezing of three is more effective than the squeezing of two. It was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) squeezed it with them both, and they produced about a dates worth, and they wiped the loaf with it; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped it with his hand, then supplicated for blessing in it, then said: Invite for me ten by ten. So I invited ten and they sat and ate until they burped with fullness. They kept entering ten by ten until they were full. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat, and we ate with him until there was leftover. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi except Said ibn Abi Hilal, and none from Said except Khalid ibn Yazid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8774","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abd al-Wahhab, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whomever Allah wills good for, He gives him understanding in religion; they are but portions, and Allah gives. There will never cease in this ummah a group upholding the command of Allah; those who oppose them will not harm them until the command of Allah comes while they are dominant over the people."
"8775","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Abd al-Wahhab, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman who said: I heard Ibn Abi Sufyan, with a lock of hair in his hand, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid the like of this, saying: The Children of Israel were only punished when their women adopted these."
"8776","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Abd al-Wahhab, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought, on the night he was taken to Iliya (Jerusalem), two cups: wine and milk. He looked at them, then took the milk. Jibril said to him: Praise be to Allah who guided you to the fitrah; had you taken the wine your ummah would have gone astray. None narrated these hadiths from Abd al-Wahhab except Yazid ibn al-Had."
"8777","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Do not make nabidh in gourds or varnished vessels."
"8778","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: By Allah, I seek Allahs forgiveness and repent to Him in a day more than seventy times."
"8779","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever catches a rakah of the prayer has caught the prayer."
"8780","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The likeness of one who comes early to prayer is like one who offers a camel, then the one after him like one who offers a cow, then the one after him like one who offers a ram, then the one after him like one who offers a chicken, then the one after him like one who offers an egg."
"8781","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whatever I forbid you, avoid it, and whatever I command you, do of it what you can; for those before you were only destroyed by their many questions and their differing with their prophets."
"8782","With the same chain, Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I saw Amr ibn Luhayy ibn Amir al-Khuzai dragging his intestines in the Fire; he was the first to set free the saibah (pagan sacrificial camels)."
"8783","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O Allah, I have taken a covenant with You which You will never break for me: whichever believer I insult or flog, make that a means of nearness for him to You on the Day of Resurrection."
"8784","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: May Allah fight the Jews and Christians; they took the graves of their prophets as mosques."
"8785","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, who said: When the muadhdhin fell silent after the first call of the Fajr prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stand and pray two light rakahs before the Fajr prayer, after the dawn had become clear, then lie down on his right side until the muadhdhin came to him with the iqamah."
"8786","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Fajr, and believing women would attend with him, wrapped in their cloaks, then return to their homes, unrecognized in the darkness."
"8787","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would supplicate in prayer: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave, and I seek refuge in You from the trial of the false Messiah, the Dajjal, and I seek refuge in You from the trial of life and death. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from sin and debt. Aishah said: Someone said to him: How often you seek refuge from debt, O Messenger of Allah? He said: When a man is in debt he speaks and lies, and promises and breaks his promise."
"8788","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Abdullah ibn Umar who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever misses the Asr prayer, it is as if he has been bereft of his family and wealth."
"8789","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Urwah, from Abdullah ibn Urwah, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever narrates a lie about me, let him take his seat in the Fire. No one narrated this hadith from Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Urwah except Yazid ibn al-Had; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8790","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibrahim ibn Sad, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: While I was sleeping I saw the people presented to me wearing shirts, some reaching the breasts and some less than that, and Umar ibn al-Khattab was presented to me wearing a shirt trailing. They said: How did you interpret it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Religion."
"8791","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibrahim ibn Sad, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab, from Muhammad ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab sought permission upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while with him were women of Quraysh talking to him and asking him much, their voices raised. When Umar sought permission they hastened to the veil. Umar entered while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed. Umar said: May Allah make your teeth laugh, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I wondered at those women with me; when they heard your voice they hastened to the veil. Umar said: You were more deserving that they fear. Then Umar said: O enemies of your own souls, do you fear me and not fear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: Yes, you are harsher and sterner than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in whose hand is my soul, Satan never met you walking a path but that he walked another path than yours."
"8792","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Ibrahim ibn Sad, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: While I was sleeping I saw myself at a well, and I drew from it what Allah willed, then the son of Abu Quhafah drew a bucket or two, and in his drawing was weakness, may Allah forgive him. Then it turned into a large bucket and Umar ibn al-Khattab took it, and I never saw a strong man among the people drawing his drawing, until the people struck camels at the trough. None narrated these hadiths from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Ibrahim ibn Sad, except al-Layth ibn Sad."
"8793","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray while I lay across before him like a funeral bier; when he intended to pray witr he would touch me with his foot, waking me, and I would pray witr."
"8794","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while between my chest and chin; I never dislike the severity of death for anyone ever after what I saw of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). None narrated these two hadiths from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, except al-Layth."
"8795","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The likeness of the mujahid in the path of Allah is like the devout one standing who does not tire of fasting or prayer until he returns. By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, no one is wounded in the path of Allah, and Allah knows best who is wounded in His path, but that he will come on the Day of Resurrection with the color the color of blood and the scent the scent of musk. By the One in whose hand is my soul, I would love to be killed in the path of Allah, then brought to life, then killed in the path of Allah, then brought to life, then killed. Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said it three times. No one narrated this hadith from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Abu al-Zinad, except al-Layth."
"8796","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Abu Said al-Khudri who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Iblis said to his Lord: By Your might and majesty, I will never cease misleading the children of Adam as long as souls remain in them. His Lord said to him: By My might and majesty, I will never cease forgiving them as long as they seek My forgiveness. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Said except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8797","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Hasten with your deeds before tribulations like pieces of the dark night; one of you will wake a Muslim in them and evening a disbeliever, and evening a Muslim and morning a disbeliever, selling his religion for a paltry price."
"8798","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The sun neither rises nor sets on a day greater than Friday; no creature but is frightened of Friday except the two heavy ones: jinn and men. Upon every door of the mosque on Friday stand angels writing the first and then the first, like one offering a camel, like one offering a cow, like one offering a sheep, like one offering a bird, and like one offering an egg; when the imam comes out the scrolls are folded."
"8799","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to me: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abdullah ibn Amr: How will you be, O Abdullah, when you remain among worthless dregs whose trusts and covenants are corrupted, differing and becoming like this? And he interlaced his fingers together. Abdullah said: How do you command me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Act upon what you know, leave what you disapprove, attend to your own self, and leave the common people. None narrated these hadiths from Amr ibn Abi Amr except Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri."
"8800","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Ammar ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, that Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab narrated to him, that Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to him, that Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman narrated to him, that Aishah narrated to her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If the slave girl commits adultery, flog her; if she commits adultery, flog her; if she commits adultery, flog her; then sell her even for a rope, and al-dafir is the rope. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Amrah, from Aishah, except Ammar ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Farwah, and none narrated it from Ammar except Yazid ibn Abi Habib; al-Layth ibn Sad is alone in it. The people narrated it from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydillah ibn Abdullah, from Abu Hurayrah and Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani; Ibn Uyaynah narrated it adding Shibl in its chain; and Ibn Abi Layla narrated it from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah."
"8801","Muttalib narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ramli narrated to us: Shuayb ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Anas who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray Maghrib while fasting until he broke his fast, even with a drink of water. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah except Said, and none from Said except Shuayb; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz is alone in it."
"8802","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Hasan ibn al-Khalil ibn Murrah narrated to me, from his father, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever marries has completed half of faith, so let him fear Allah in the remaining half."
"8803","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Hasan ibn al-Khalil ibn Murrah narrated to me, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made brotherhood between Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf and Sad ibn al-Rabi, so he returned with him to his family and said: Choose whichever wife you wish and I will divorce her for you, and my wealth is halves for you. Abd al-Rahman said: May Allah bless you in your family and wealth; show me the market. They showed him, and he did not return until he had gained something, then married a woman from the Ansar for the weight of a date-stone of gold. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met him with traces of yellow on him and said: What is this? He said: I married a woman from the Ansar for the weight of a date-stone of gold. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Give a feast, even with a sheep. None narrated these two hadiths from al-Hasan ibn al-Khalil except Abdullah ibn Salih."
"8804","Muttalib narrated to us: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us: Uthman ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muhajir, from Urwah ibn Ruwaym al-Lakhmi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of faith is to know that Allah is with you wherever you are. No one narrated this hadith from Urwah ibn Ruwaym except Muhammad ibn Muhajir; Uthman ibn Kathir is alone in it."
"8805","Muttalib narrated to us: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I and my ummah will be upon a hill on the Day of Resurrection, and Allah will clothe me in a green garment, then permit me, and I will praise Him with what He deserves; that is the praised station. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except al-Zubaydi."
"8806","Muttalib narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn al-Salt narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Humran, from Laqit ibn al-Harith, from Abu Umamah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The worst of people is the one who is harsh upon his family. They said: O Messenger of Allah, how is one harsh upon his family? He said: A man whose wife cowers when he enters his house, whose child flees, and whose servant runs away; when he leaves, his wife laughs and his household feels at ease. This hadith is not narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except with this chain; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz is alone in it."
"8807","Muttalib narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ramli narrated to us: Nasr ibn Ishaq al-Hamdani narrated to us, from al-Sari ibn Ismail, from al-Shabi, from al-Numan ibn Bashir who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, with what do you pray witr? He said: With Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and Say: O disbelievers, and Say: He is Allah, One. This hadith is not narrated from al-Numan ibn Bashir except with this chain; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz is alone in it."
"8808","Muttalib narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn al-Salt narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Bashir, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fire does not heal anyone. This hadith is not narrated from Salamah except with this chain; Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz is alone in it."
"8809","Muttalib ibn Shuayb narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abdullah, from Isa ibn Hilal al-Sadafi, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: A servant remains a believer for ages upon ages, then dies while Allah is angry with him; and a servant remains a disbeliever for ages upon ages, then dies while Allah is pleased with him. Whoever dies as a slanderer, backbiter, nicknaming people, his mark on the Day of Resurrection will be that Allah brands him on the nose from both sides. This hadith is not narrated from Abdullah ibn Amr except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8810","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Anas ibn Malik who said: A man from Banu Tamim came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man of much wealth, with a family and settled folk; tell me how I should spend and what I should do? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You pay zakah from your wealth, for it is a purification purifying you; you join your kin, and you recognize the right of the beggar, the neighbor, and the poor. He said: O Messenger of Allah, reduce for me. He said: Give the near relative his right, and the poor, and the traveler, and do not squander wastefully. He said: Is it enough for me, O Messenger of Allah, that when I pay zakah to your messenger I am absolved of it before Allah and His Messenger? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, when you pay zakah to the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), you are absolved of it, and you have its reward, while its sin is upon whoever alters it. This hadith is not narrated from Anas except with this chain; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8811","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The angels converse in the anan — and al-anan is the clouds — about a matter that will occur on earth, and the devils hear a word from them and cast it into the ears of the soothsayers, who add to it a hundred lies. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Aswad except Said ibn Abi Hilal, and none from Said except Khalid; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8812","With the same chain: Al-Layth narrated to me: Jafar ibn Rabiah narrated to me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abdullah ibn Umar, that Abu Bakr married Asma bint Umays after Jafar ibn Abi Talib. He came to enter upon Asma and found a group sitting in his house, so he returned to the Prophet of Allah and informed him. He said: O Abu Bakr, what is that? He said: I saw no harm. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah declare her innocent of that. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and said: No man shall enter upon a woman whose husband is absent except with another. No one narrated this hadith from Jubayr ibn Nufayr except Bakr ibn Sawadah, and none from Bakr except Jafar; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8813","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Rabiah ibn Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, from Ibn Qariz, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a woman prays her five, fasts her month, guards her chastity, and obeys her husband, let her enter through whichever gates of Paradise she wishes. This hadith is not narrated from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf except with this chain; Ibn Lahiah is alone in it."
"8814","Muttalib narrated to us: Abdullah narrated to us: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Abu Hazim, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir: The Jews used to say: When a woman is approached from behind, her child comes cross-eyed. Then this verse was revealed: Your women are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Hazim except Ibn al-Had; al-Layth is alone in it."
"8815","Miqdam ibn Dawud ibn Isa al-Ruayni al-Misri narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let no man touch another man skin to skin, and let no woman touch another woman skin to skin. No one narrated this hadith from al-Shaybani except Abu Muawiyah; Asad ibn Musa is alone in it."
"8816","Miqdam narrated to us: My uncle Said ibn Isa narrated to us: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Abu Bakr and Umar are the masters of the elderly of Paradise among the first and the last; and do not inform them, O Ali. No one narrated this hadith from Jafar ibn Muhammad except Sufyan ibn Uyaynah; Said ibn Isa is alone in it."
"8817","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Said ibn Isa narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, his wives sent Uthman ibn Affan to Abu Bakr asking him for their inheritance from what Allah had granted him. Aishah said: I would hold them back from that, saying: Do you not fear Allah? Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: We are not inherited from; what we leave is charity; the family of Muhammad only eats from the wealth of Allah."
"8818","Miqdam narrated to us: My uncle narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: When someone died among the people they would bring him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pray over him. He would ask: Is there any debt upon him? If they said: Yes, he said: Has he left means to settle his debt? If they said: Yes, he prayed over him; otherwise he said: Pray over your companion. When Allah granted him conquests he said: I am closer to the believers than their own selves; whoever dies owing a debt, it is upon us, and whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs."
"8819","Miqdam narrated to us: My uncle narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, whichever servant I insult, make it a nearness for him to You on the Day of Resurrection."
"8820","Miqdam narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri: Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated to us, from his father Kab ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never went out on a journey or dispatched an expedition except on Thursday."
"8821","Miqdam narrated to us: My uncle Said narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are more deserving of doubt than our father Ibrahim, when he said: My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead. He said: Do you not believe? He said: Yes, but that my heart may be at rest. And may Allah have mercy on my brother Lut; he would have resorted to a strong support. Had I remained in prison as long as Yusuf remained, I would have answered the caller. None narrated these hadiths from Amr ibn al-Harith except Bakr ibn Mudar, and none from Bakr except Abd al-Rahman; al-Miqdam is alone in it."
"8822","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abd al-Malik ibn Salamah al-Umawi narrated to us: Nafi ibn Abi Nuaym narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever comes to Friday prayer, let him bathe. No one narrated this hadith from Nafi except Abd al-Malik ibn Salamah."
"8823","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abd al-Malik narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said and Rabiah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Sahlah bint Suhayl to nurse Salim, so that what was in the heart of Abu Hudhayfah would go away. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya and Rabiah, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, except Sulayman ibn Bilal."
"8824","Al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sleep is the brother of death, and the people of Paradise do not sleep. No one narrated this hadith from Sufyan except Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah."
"8825","Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Sufyan and Misar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What an excellent condiment is vinegar. No one narrated this hadith from Misar except Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah, and Imran ibn Uyaynah."
"8826","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: My uncle Said ibn Isa narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ashras narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from his brother Ubaydillah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a mans voice, so he leapt up vigorously and went out to him. I followed him, and there he was leaning on the withers of his horse, and it was Dihyah al-Kalbi, turbaned with his turban let down between his shoulders. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, I said: You leapt up vigorously and went out, and it was Dihyah al-Kalbi? He said: And you saw him? I said: Yes. He said: That was Jibril, peace be upon him; he ordered me to go out to Banu Qurayzah. No one narrated this hadith from Ubaydillah ibn Umar except his brother Abdullah, and none narrated it from Abdullah except Abd al-Rahman ibn Ashras, and Rawh ibn Ubadah."
"8827","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Ayyub ibn Utbah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought forgiveness for the first row three times, for the second row twice, and for the third once. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, except Ayyub."
"8828","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Asad narrated to us: Ayyub narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The unmarried woman is consulted about herself, and the virgin is not married except with her permission. They said: And how is her permission? He said: That she remain silent."
"8829","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad narrated to us: Ayyub narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan with faith and seeking reward, his past sins are forgiven; and whoever stands the Night of Decree with faith and seeking reward, his past sins are forgiven. None narrated these two hadiths from Ayyub except Asad ibn Musa."
"8830","Al-Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Asad narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ayyash, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prostrated in When the sky is split asunder. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ayyash except Ibn Abi Dhib."
"8831","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Al-Dahhak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Recite the Quran; do not eat by it, do not seek much by it, do not go to excess in it, and do not turn away from it. Learn the Quran, for it intercedes for its companion on the Day of Resurrection. Learn the two radiant ones: Surah al-Baqarah and Al Imran, for they will come on the Day of Resurrection as if two clouds, or two shades, or two flocks of birds in ranks, interceding for their companion on the Day of Resurrection. Learn al-Baqarah, for taking it is blessing and leaving it is regret, and the sorcerers cannot handle it. No one narrated this hadith from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, except al-Dahhak; Asad ibn Musa is alone in it. Hisham, Aban, and Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated it from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Zayd ibn Sallam, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Umamah, and from Abu Rashid al-Hubrani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl."
"8832","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu Ayyub al-Ifriqi, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: People will come leading you in the prayers; if they complete them, it is for them and for you, and if they fall short, it is upon them and for you. No one narrated this hadith from Safwan ibn Sulaym except Abu Ayyub; Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman is alone in it."
"8833","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us: Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the privy he would say: In the name of Allah; O Allah, I seek refuge in You from filth, the impure, the wicked malignant one, the accursed Satan. No one narrated this hadith from al-Hasan and Qatadah except Ismail ibn Muslim; Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman is alone in it."
"8834","Miqdam narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sulami, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out when the sun declined, stood at Arafah, addressed the people, called Allah to witness over them and called them to witness over themselves, saying: You will be asked about me, so what will you say? They said: We bear witness that you conveyed. He said: O Allah, bear witness. He said much, and the muadhdhin called for Zuhr, then he stood and prayed Zuhr after the sermon, then the iqamah was made for Asr and he prayed Asr, then he rode and stood; when the sun set he hastened to Muzdalifah, prayed Maghrib and Isha with one adhan and two iqamahs, then lay down until dawn broke, prayed Fajr and stood. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sulami except Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman."
"8835","With the same chain: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Sawwar, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu al-Malih ibn Usamah, from his father Usamah who said: I saw myself with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the time of Hudaybiyah, and we were rained upon with rain that did not wet the soles of our sandals, when the caller of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called: Pray in your dwellings. No one narrated this hadith from Ashath except Abd al-Rahim; Ashath did not mention Abu Qilabah in his hadith. Al-Thawri narrated it from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Mulayh, from his father."
"8836","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Amir al-Aslami narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One who sent me with the truth, Allah will not punish on the Day of Resurrection one who had mercy on the orphan, was gentle with him in speech, had mercy on his orphanhood and weakness, and did not vaunt over his neighbor with the bounty Allah gave him. And he said: O nation of Muhammad, by the One who sent me with the truth, Allah will not accept on the Day of Resurrection charity from a man who has needy kin deserving his charity while he diverts it to others. By the One in whose hand is my soul, Allah will not look at him on the Day of Resurrection. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Abdullah ibn Amir; Khalid ibn Nizar is alone in it."
"8837","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar narrated to us: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Asma bint Yazid, who said: When Ibrahim, son of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), died, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq said: You are the most deserving to know Allahs right. He said: The eye sheds tears, the heart grieves, and we say nothing that angers the Lord; we are grieved by you, O Ibrahim. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Khuthaym except Muslim ibn Khalid."
"8838","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar narrated to us: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Abu Thumamah al-Hannat who said: Kab ibn Ujrah met me while I was at al-Balat, having interlaced my fingers, and said: Where are you heading? I said: I intend the mosque. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When one of you performs ablution, then goes out intending the mosque, he is in prayer as long as he does not interlace his fingers. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Thumamah except Dawud ibn Qays."
"8839","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid narrated to us: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Usamah ibn Zayd who said: Bilal and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered al-Aswaf. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to relieve himself, then they came out. Usamah said: I asked Bilal what he did? Bilal said: He went to relieve himself, then performed ablution, washing his face and hands, wiping his head, wiping over his leather socks, then prayed. No one narrated this hadith from Zayd ibn Aslam except Dawud ibn Qays, and al-Darawardi."
"8840","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid narrated to us: Isa ibn al-Muttalib narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that he said: I shall fast as long as I live, and stand the night as long as I live. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Are you the one who said: I shall fast the whole year as long as I live, and stand the night as long as I live? Abdullah ibn Amr said to him: I have said that, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fast and break fast, pray and sleep, and fast three days of the month; every good deed is ten like it. He said: I find myself stronger than that. He said: Fast one day and break fast two days. He said: I find myself stronger than better than that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fast one day and break fast one day. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I find myself stronger than that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is nothing better than that. No one narrated this hadith from Isa ibn al-Muttalib except Khalid ibn Nizar."
"8841","Miqdam narrated to us: My uncle Said ibn Isa and Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, saying: Mufaddal ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Abu Urwah al-Basri, from Ziyad ibn Abi Ammar, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seeking knowledge is obligatory upon every Muslim. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Urwah — and he is Mamar ibn Rashid — except Mufaddal ibn Fadalah."
"8842","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar narrated to us: Yazid ibn Abd al-Malik al-Nawfali narrated to us, from Abu Musa al-Hannat, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you touches his private part with his hand, ablution becomes obligatory upon him. None of those who narrated this hadith included between Yazid ibn Abd al-Malik and Said al-Maqburi: Abu Musa al-Hannat — and he is Isa ibn Abi Isa — except Khalid ibn Nizar."
"8843","Miqdam narrated to us: My uncle Said ibn Isa narrated to us: Mufaddal ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray upon the khumrah (small mat) and prostrate upon it. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Yunus; Mufaddal ibn Fadalah is alone in it."
"8844","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar narrated to us: Hammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That I witness Fajr, then sit remembering Allah until the sun rises, is dearer to me than that I mount racehorses in the path of Allah. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Hazim except Hammad ibn Abi Humayd, and he is Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd; the people of Madinah say: Hammad ibn Abi Humayd."
"8845","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Salih al-Tammar narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Attab ibn Asid, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the zakah of vineyards: They are estimated as date-palms are estimated, then their zakah is paid as raisins as the zakah of date-palms is paid as dates. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Muhammad ibn Salih al-Tammar."
"8846","Miqdam narrated to us: Ibn Nizar and Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam told us, they said: Muslim ibn Khalid al-Zanji told us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurairah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited this verse: {And if you turn away, He will replace you with a people other than you, then they will not be like you}. They said: O Messenger of Allah, who are these who, if we turn away, He will replace us with a people other than us then they will not be like us? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck the thigh of Salman al-Farisi, then said: This one and his people; if religion were suspended from the Pleiades, men from Persia would attain it. No one narrated this hadith from Muslim ibn Khalid except Khalid ibn Nizar."
"8847","Miqdam narrated to us: my uncle Said ibn Isa told us: Mufaddal ibn Fadalah told us, from Abu Isa al-Khurasani, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan al-Basri, from Abu Hurairah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to recite in the first rakah of witr: {Glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most High}, and in the second: {Say: O disbelievers}, and in the third: {Say: He is Allah, the One}, and the two Muawwidhatayn."
"8848","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Hafs ibn Umar ibn Abi al-Attaf told us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurairah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever forgets a prayer, its time is when he remembers it. No one narrated this hadith from Abu al-Zinad except Hafs ibn Umar."
"8849","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid told us: Ishaq ibn Hazim al-Zayyat told us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from al-Rubayyi bint Muawwidh ibn Afra, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in to her, then called for ablution water, so a vessel holding a mudd and a third, or a mudd and a half, by the mudd of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was brought. He performed ablution: he washed his face, washed his hands, wiped his head, wiped his temples, wiped the front of his head and the back of his head until reaching the middle in each wiping, and washed his feet. No one narrated this hadith from Ishaq ibn Hazim except Khalid ibn Nizar."
"8850","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid told us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam told us, from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked, and it was said to him: One of us wakes in the morning and has not prayed witr; sleep overcame him? He said: Then let him pray witr when morning comes. No one narrated this hadith connected (mawsul) from Zayd ibn Aslam except his son Abd al-Rahman, and a group narrated it disconnected (maqtu) from Ata ibn Yasar."
"8851","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid told us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, from his brother who said: I heard Abu Hurairah saying: There was no one among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with more hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than me, except Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, for he used to write and I did not write. No one narrated this hadith from Amr ibn Dinar except Sufyan ibn Uyaynah."
"8852","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid told us: Sufyan told us: al-Amash and Ibn al-Munkadir narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let me not find one of you when a matter from my affair comes to him, from what I forbade or what I commanded, while he is reclining on his couch, saying: What we found in the Book of Allah we followed. No one narrated it from Sufyan, from al-Amash and Ibn al-Munkadir, except Khalid; and people narrated it from Sufyan, from Abi al-Nadr, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father."
"8853","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Dhakwan told us, from Dawud ibn Bakr, from Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be after me leaders who pray the prayer outside its time; when they do that, pray the prayer at its time, and make your prayer with them voluntary. No one narrated this hadith from Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad except Dawud ibn Bakr; Umar ibn Hafs ibn Dhakwan was alone in narrating it."
"8854","Miqdam narrated to us: my uncle Said ibn Isa told us: Mufaddal ibn Fadalah told us: Muhammad ibn Ajlan informed me, from Abi al-Zinad, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurairah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honor his neighbor; and whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honor his guest, his reward being a day and a night, and hospitality is three days, and what is beyond that is charity; and it is not lawful for him to stay with him until he burdens him; and whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him say good or remain silent. No one narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Ajlan except Mufaddal ibn Fadalah."
"8855","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah told us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attacked Khaybar early in the morning when they had opened the fortress; they came out with their spades, then when they saw him they returned to the fortress and said: Muhammad and the army, Muhammad and the army. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Khaybar is ruined; when we descend in the courtyard of a people, {then evil is the morning of those who were warned}. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Sufyan ibn Uyaynah."
"8856","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Mubarak ibn Fadalah told us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, they differed, and said: We seek the good choice from our Lord; we send to the lahd-digger and to the shaqq-digger, and whichever of them comes first we leave him to it. So they sent to both, and the maker of the lahd came first, so they made a lahd for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). No one narrated this hadith from Humayd except Mubarak ibn Fadalah."
"8857","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid told us, from Maslamah ibn Nafi: my brother Duwayd ibn Nafi narrated to me: Abdullah ibn Muslim ibn Shihab, the brother of al-Zuhri, told us, from Anas ibn Malik who said: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, there is an offense in my belly, so carry out the prescribed punishment upon me. He said: We do not kill what is in your belly for your sin; go until you deliver what is in your belly. So she went, then when she delivered she came and said: I have delivered. He said: Go and nurse him until you wean him. Then when she weaned him she came and said: I have weaned him, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Go and take care of him. So she went, then she came with her sister walking, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was amazed at her patience. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her stoning. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: Go, and when she is placed in her pit, stand before her so that you are before her eyes, and point to her. And he ordered a man, saying: Go to a large stone, come to her from behind her, throw it at her and crush her head. No one narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Muslim except Duwayd ibn Nafi, nor from Duwayd except his brother Maslamah ibn Nafi; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid was alone in narrating it."
"8858","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Said ibn Zayd told us: al-Zubayr ibn Khirrit told us: Abu Labid said: Horses were sent out in the time of al-Hajjaj, while al-Hakam ibn Ayyub was governor over Basra. When the horses came, we said: If only we would go to Anas ibn Malik and ask him: O Abu Hamzah, did you race in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So we came to him while he was in his palace at al-Zawiyah, and asked him: O Abu Hamzah, did you race in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) race? He said: Yes, by Allah, he raced on a horse of his called Sabhah, and it outran the people, so he was cheerful at that and it pleased him. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Labid except al-Zubayr ibn Khirrit; Said ibn Zayd was alone in narrating it."
"8859","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Hilal ibn Abi Dawud al-Habati told us: my brother Harun ibn Abi Dawud narrated to me, he said: I came to Anas ibn Malik and said: O Abu Hamzah, the place is far, and we like to visit you. So he raised his head and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whenever a man visits a sick person, he wades in mercy; then when he sits with the sick person, mercy envelops him. No one narrated this hadith from Harun except his brother Hilal."
"8860","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Sallam told us: Yazid al-Dabbi told us: Anas ibn Malik told us, he said: I never prayed behind anyone a prayer lighter nor briefer yet complete than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). No one narrated this hadith from Yazid al-Dabbi except Sallam ibn Miskin."
"8861","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Attaf ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi told us: Zayd ibn Aslam told us, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz led us in Zuhr prayer, then we went to Anas ibn Malik to visit him sick. When I entered upon him he said: Have you prayed? We said: Yes. He said: O slave-girl, bring me ablution water. I never prayed behind an imam after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whose prayer more resembled the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than this imam of yours. And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz used to complete the bowing and prostration, and lighten the standing and sitting. No one narrated this hadith from Zayd ibn Aslam except Attaf ibn Khalid."
"8862","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Marwan ibn Muawiyah told us: Isa ibn Abi Isa al-Basri told us, from Musa, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The chief of your condiments is salt. This hadith is not narrated from Anas except with this chain; Marwan ibn Muawiyah was alone in narrating it."
"8863","Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us, Ashras ibn al-Rabi Abu Shayban al-Hudhali told us: Abu Zilal al-Qasmali told us that he entered upon Anas ibn Malik, who said to him: O Abu Zilal, when was your sight afflicted? He said: I do not remember it. He said: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith which the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to us, from Jibril (peace be upon him), from his Lord, the Exalted? He said: Allah said: O Jibril, what is the reward of My servant when I take his two precious ones except looking at My Face and neighborhood in My Abode. And I saw the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) weeping around him, wishing their eyesight would go. No one narrated this hadith from Ashras except Asad ibn Musa."
"8864","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah told us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, who heard Abu Hurairah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah says: The son of Adam harms Me; he curses time, and I am time; in My Hand is the affair, I turn the night and the day. No one narrated this hadith from Amr ibn Dinar except Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, and none narrated it from Sufyan except Asad ibn Musa and Ibrahim al-Saiqi."
"8865","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Ibn Lahiah told us: Abu al-Aswad told us, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abi Said al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to walk in one sandal or one khuff. No one narrated this hadith from Urwah except Abu al-Aswad; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it."
"8866","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Umarah ibn Zad han told us, from Thabit, from Anas who said: The king of Dhu Yazan gifted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a suit, which he had taken for thirty-three camels or thirty-three she-camels. He wore it for a short part of the day, then cast it off. No one narrated this hadith from Thabit except Umarah ibn Zad han."
"8867","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Said ibn Bashir told us, from Qatadah, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man does not believe until I am more beloved to him than his child, his parent, and all people."
"8868","And with the same chain: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The son of Adam grows old, while two things grow young with him: greed for wealth and greed for life."
"8869","And with the same chain: he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, a man does not believe until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself. No one narrated these hadiths from Said ibn Bashir except Asad."
"8870","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Umar ibn Qays told us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bathed from janabah, he performed his ablution for prayer: he poured over his hands three times, then washed his private part, then washed his left hand three times, then rinsed his mouth and nose three times, then washed his face three times, then washed his arms three times, then poured water over his head and body. No one narrated this hadith from Ata except Umar ibn Qays; Khalid ibn Nizar was alone in narrating it."
"8871","Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Ali ibn Mabad told us: Abdullah ibn Wahb told us, from Said ibn Abi Ayyub, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Buzruj who said: I heard Abu Hurairah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I fear nothing for my ummah except weakness of certainty. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Hurairah except Abd al-Rahman ibn Buzruj; Said ibn Abi Ayyub was alone in narrating it."
"8872","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Shayban Abu Muawiyah told us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik who said: A man invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to barley bread and rancid melted fat. He said: And I heard him once saying: By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, no sa of dates nor sa of wheat ever came in the morning to the family of Muhammad, while he had nine wives that day; and he pawned an armor of his with a Jew in Medina, taking food for it, and found nothing with which to redeem it. No one narrated this hadith from Shayban except Asad ibn Musa, and Adam."
"8873","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Afiyah ibn Yazid told us, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, that he entered upon her while she was weeping, and said: What makes you weep? She said: Whatever I wish to weep for, I weep; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died and did not eat his fill of wheat bread twice in a day, then the world collapsed upon her. And we were four months with no food except water and dates; and he died while his armor was pawned, until Abu Bakr redeemed it. No one narrated this hadith from Afiyah except Asad ibn Musa."
"8874","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Adi ibn al-Fadl told us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Hissan ibn Kahin who said: Aishah informed me, she said: A leg of sheep was gifted to us one night from the house of Abu Bakr. She said: By Allah, I would hold it for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he cut it, or: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held it while I cut it. I said: O Mother of the Believers, by lamp light was that? She said: Had we possessed lamp oil we would have eaten it; a month would indeed come upon the family of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which they baked no bread nor cooked. No one narrated this hadith from Yunus ibn Ubayd except Adi ibn al-Mufaddal; Asad ibn Musa was alone in narrating it. And Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated this hadith from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abi Burdah, from Aishah."
"8875","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Ali ibn Thabit told us, from al-Wazi ibn Nafi al-Uqayli, from Salim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, from his father who said: I entered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a garden of the gardens of Medina, and he began eating green unripe dates, and said: Eat, O Ibn Umar. I said: I do not desire it, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you not desire it? It is the first food the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has eaten for four days. No one narrated this hadith from Salim except al-Wazi ibn Nafi; Ali ibn Thabit was alone in narrating it."
"8876","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: al-Masudi told us, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs ibn Umar ibn Sad, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send us on expeditions with no provision except salf of dates, dividing it handful by handful, until it came to date by date. I said: O father, what could a date avail you? He said: Do not say that, O my son; as soon as we lost it, we felt its loss. No one narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah except Abu Bakr ibn Hafs; al-Masudi was alone in narrating it, and it is not narrated from Amir ibn Rabiah except with this chain."
"8877","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Abu Bakr al-Dahiri told us: Thawr ibn Yazid told us, from Khalid ibn Muhajir, from Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Son of Adam, with you is what suffices you, yet you seek what makes you transgress? Son of Adam, neither content with little, nor sated by much? Son of Adam, when you wake sound in your body, safe in your household, with the sustenance of your day, then upon the world be oblivion. This hadith is not narrated from Umar except with this chain; Asad ibn Musa was alone in narrating it."
"8878","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Fudayl ibn Iyad told us, from al-Amash, from Thumamah ibn Uqbah al-Muhallimi, from Zayd ibn Arqam who said: A Jew came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Aba al-Qasim, you claim that the people of Paradise eat and drink therein? He said: Yes, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, a man is given the strength of a hundred in eating, drinking, desire, and intercourse. The Jew said: One who eats and drinks has a need, while Paradise is pure? He said: The need of one of them is sweat flowing from his skin like the scent of musk, then his belly becomes slim. No one narrated this hadith from Fudayl except Asad ibn Musa."
"8879","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Said ibn Zarbi told us: Thabit ibn al-Bunani narrated to me, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me: Jibril (peace be upon him) narrated to me, he said: A man enters upon the houri and she receives him with embracing and handshaking. Thabit said: Anas said, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: With which fingertip does he touch her; if part of her fingertip appeared, its light would overcome the light of the sun and the moon, and if a lock of her hair appeared, it would fill what is between the east and the west with the fragrance of its scent. While he is reclining with her on his couch, a light from above him shines upon him, and he thinks that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has looked upon His creation; then a houri calls him: O friend of Allah, have we no turn with you? He says: Who are you, O you? She says: I am of those about whom Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: {And with Us is more}. So he turns to her, and with her is of beauty and perfection what was not with the first. While he is reclining with her on his couch, a light from above him shines upon him, and another houri calls him: O friend of Allah, have we no turn with you? He says: Who are you, O you? She says: I am of those about whom Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {No soul knows what is hidden for them of coolness of eyes as reward for what they used to do}. So he keeps moving from wife to wife. No one narrated this hadith from Thabit except Said ibn Zarbi; Asad ibn Musa was alone in narrating it."
"8880","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Ibn Thawban told us, from Ata ibn Qurrah, from Abdullah ibn Damrah, from Abu Hurairah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If the adornment of the lowest of the people of Paradise were matched against the adornment of all the people of this world, what Allah adorns him with in the Hereafter would be better than the adornment of all the people of this world."
"8881","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Ibn Thawban told us, from Ata ibn Qurrah, from Abdullah ibn Damrah, from Abu Hurairah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is pleased that Allah give him wine to drink in the Hereafter, let him leave it in this world; and whoever is pleased that Allah clothe him with silk in the Hereafter, let him leave it in this world. No one narrated these two hadiths from Ibn Thawban except Asad ibn Musa."
"8882","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Ibn Lahiah told us: Yazid ibn Abi Habib told us, from Dawud ibn Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: If what a fingernail carries of what is in Paradise appeared, what is between the horizons of the heavens and the earth would be adorned by it; and if a man of the people of Paradise looked out and his bracelet appeared, its light would efface the light of the sun just as the sun effaces the light of the stars. No one narrated this hadith from Dawud ibn Amir except Yazid ibn Abi Habib; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it, and it is not narrated from Sad except with this chain."
"8883","Miqdam narrated to us: Ali ibn Mabad told us: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn al-Hasan ibn Dinar al-Dabbi told us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abi al-Ahwas, from Abdullah ibn Masud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The disbeliever will be bridled with his sweat from the severity of that Day, meaning the Day of Resurrection, until he says: O Lord, relieve me though it be to the Fire. No one narrated this hadith from al-Thawri except Abd al-Ghaffar; Ali ibn Mabad was alone in narrating it."
"8884","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Ayyub ibn Khut told us: Qatadah told us, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has the world as his concern and grief, for it he looks up and for it he strives and seeks, Allah makes his poverty before his eyes, scatters his livelihood, and nothing comes to him of it except what was written for him; and whoever has the Hereafter as his concern and grief, for it he looks up and for it he strives and seeks, Allah makes his richness in his heart, gathers his livelihood for him, and the world comes to him humbled. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Asad, and none narrated it from Qatadah except Ayyub ibn Khut and Hammam; and none narrated it from Hammam except Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar; Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Azdi was alone in narrating it."
"8885","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Said ibn Bashir told us: Qatadah told us, from Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be balanced in prostration, and let none of you spread his arms like the dog. No one narrated it from Said except Asad."
"8886","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Abu Hilal al-Rasibi told us: Qatadah told us, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: My Lord promised me to admit one hundred thousand of my ummah into Paradise. Abu Bakr said: O Prophet of Allah, increase us. So he gestured with his hand like this, and scooped with it. Then he said: O Prophet of Allah, increase us. So he gestured with his hand like this. He said: O Prophet of Allah, increase us. So he gestured with his hand like this. Umar said: Your hand, O Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr said: What is that to us, O Ibn al-Khattab? Umar said: Allah is able to admit people into Paradise with a single handful. The Prophet said: Umar spoke the truth. No one narrated this hadith from Qatadah, from Anas, except Abu Hilal. And Mamar narrated it from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Anas; and Muadh ibn Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated it from his father, from Qatadah, from Abu Bakr ibn Anas, from Abu Bakr ibn Umayr, from his father."
"8887","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Ayyub ibn Khut told us: Qatadah told us, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has two tongues, Allah will make for him on the Day of Resurrection two tongues of fire. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Asad, and none narrated it from Qatadah except Ayyub and Ismail ibn Muslim."
"8888","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Said ibn Bashir told us: Qatadah told us, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Straighten your rows when you bow and prostrate, for I see you from behind my back. No one narrated this hadith from Said ibn Bashir except Asad ibn Musa."
"8889","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Mubarak ibn Fadalah told us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two men will come to me at the Hawd; when I see them raised to me, they will be snatched away from me. Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated no hadith from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb other than this."
"8890","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Abu al-Rabi al-Samman told us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to perfume himself with saffron. I said: What is perfuming with saffron? He said: Anointing with khaluq."
"8891","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Abu al-Rabi al-Samman told us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Fajr with the two shortest surahs of the Quran; when he finished his prayer he turned to us with his face and said: I only hastened so that the mother may finish to her child; and he had heard the voice of the child."
"8892","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Abu al-Rabi told us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque and found a rope stretched between two pillars. He said: What is this? They said: This belongs to so-and-so woman; she prays, and when she tires she holds onto it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) untied it and said: Let one of you pray in his energy; when he tires, let him sleep. No one narrated these hadiths from Abi al-Rabi except Asad ibn Musa."
"8893","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Umarah ibn Zad han told us, from Thabit, from Anas, that Bilal used to call the iqamah for prayer, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would enter, and a man would meet him and speak to him of a need until some of the people nodded off with their heads. No one narrated this hadith from Umarah ibn Zad han except Asad ibn Musa."
"8894","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Mubarak ibn Fadalah told us: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik who said: Wine was forbidden while they had no nabidh that day except unripe and ripe dates; and I was serving Abu Talhah and Suhayl ibn Bayda and Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, when the caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called: Verily wine has been forbidden, twice; so we poured it out, and it was only unripe and ripe dates. No one narrated this hadith from Mubarak ibn Fadalah except Asad ibn Musa."
"8895","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: al-Dahhak ibn Nibras al-Basri told us, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was a boy playing with children; he greeted us, then called me for a need; were it not that it was his secret I would have told you of it, but I do not tell the secret of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to anyone. No one narrated this hadith from al-Dahhak ibn Nibras except Asad ibn Musa."
"8896","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Abu al-Rabi al-Samman told us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to dye himself with saffron perfume. I said: What is saffron-dyeing? He said: Anointing with khaluq perfume."
"8897","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Abu al-Rabi al-Samman told us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Fajr with the two shortest surahs of the Quran; when he finished his prayer he turned to us with his face and said: I only hurried so that the mother may be free for her child; and he had heard the voice of the child."
"8898","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Abu al-Rabi told us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque and found a rope stretched between two pillars. He said: What is this? They said: This belongs to so-and-so woman; she prays, and when she tires she holds onto it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) untied it and said: Let one of you pray in his energy; when he tires, let him sleep. No one narrated these hadiths from Abi al-Rabi except Asad ibn Musa."
"8899","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Umarah ibn Zad han told us, from Thabit, from Anas, that Bilal would call the iqamah for prayer, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would enter, and a man would meet him and speak to him of a need until some of the people nodded off with their heads. No one narrated this hadith from Umarah ibn Zad han except Asad ibn Musa."
"8900","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Mubarak ibn Fadalah told us: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik who said: Wine was forbidden while they had no nabidh that day except unripe and ripe dates; and I was serving Abu Talhah and Suhayl ibn Bayda and Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, when the caller of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called: Verily wine has been forbidden, twice; so we poured it out, and it was only unripe and ripe dates. No one narrated this hadith from Mubarak ibn Fadalah except Asad ibn Musa."
"8901","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: al-Dahhak ibn Nibras al-Basri told us, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was a boy playing with children; he greeted us, then called me for a need; were it not that it was his secret I would have told you of it, but I do not tell the secret of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to anyone. No one narrated this hadith from al-Dahhak ibn Nibras except Asad ibn Musa."
"8902","Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam told us: Abdullah ibn Wahb told us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Bakr ibn Sawadah narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited the saying of Ibrahim: {So whoever follows me is of me, and whoever disobeys me, then You are Forgiving, Merciful}, and the saying of Isa: {If You punish them, they are Your servants; and if You forgive them, You are the Mighty, the Wise}. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hand and said: O Allah, my ummah, my ummah, and wept. Allah said to Jibril: Go to Muhammad, and your Lord knows best, and ask him what makes him weep? So Jibril came to him and asked him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said to Jibril: Go to Muhammad and say to him: We shall please you concerning your ummah and shall not grieve you. This hadith is not narrated from Abdullah ibn Amr except with this chain; Amr ibn al-Harith was alone in narrating it."
"8903","Miqdam narrated to us: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud Abu Salih al-Harrani told us: Ibn Lahiah told us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Jafar, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Salim ibn Khallad, from Rifah ibn Rafi al-Ansari who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not recite in Fajr prayer less than twenty verses, nor recite in Isha prayer less than ten verses. This hadith is not narrated from Rifah ibn Rafi except with this chain; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it."
"8904","Miqdam narrated to us: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj told us: Ibn Wahb told us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, that Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to him from Awn ibn Abdullah, from Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam, from his father who said: While we were traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he heard the people saying: Which deed is best, O Messenger of Allah? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Faith in Allah and His Messenger, jihad in the path of Allah, and an accepted hajj. Then he heard a call in the valley saying: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And I bear witness. Then he said: None bears witness to them except he is freed from shirk. This hadith is not narrated from Abdullah ibn Salam except with this chain; Amr ibn al-Harith was alone in narrating it."
"8905","Miqdam narrated to us: Asbagh told us: Ibn Wahb told us: Abu Sakhr narrated to me that Yahya ibn al-Nadr narrated to him, from Abu Qatadah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying on the minbar: Verily people are the outer garment, and the Ansar are the inner garment; if people took a valley and the Ansar took a mountain pass, I would follow the pass of the Ansar; and were it not for the Hijrah I would be a man of the Ansar. Whoever takes charge of any of their affairs, let him do good to their doer of good and overlook their doer of evil; and whoever frightens them has frightened this which is between these two, and he pointed to his chest, meaning his heart. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Qatadah except Yahya ibn al-Nadr; Abu Sakhr was alone in narrating it."
"8906","Miqdam narrated to us: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj told us: Ibn Wahb told us: Abu Sakhr informed me, from Sharik ibn Abdullah, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, that a son of his died at Usfan. He said: O Kurayb, have the people gathered for him? I went out and the people had gathered for him. He said: Did forty gather for him? I said: Yes. He said: Bring him out, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No Muslim man dies and forty men who associate nothing with Allah stand over his funeral except Allah intercedes them for him. No one narrated this hadith from Sharik except Abu Sakhr; Ibn Wahb was alone in narrating it."
"8907","Miqdam narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq told us: Hafs ibn Ghiyath told us, from Yahya ibn Abi Unaysah, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurairah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Respond to the sneezer three times; if he returns, it is a cold. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Yahya ibn Abi Unaysah."
"8908","Miqdam narrated to us: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj told us: Ibn Wahb told us: Abu Sakhr informed me, from Abd al-Karim, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Aishah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever kills a gecko, Allah effaces seven sins from him. No one narrated this hadith from Ata except Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq; Abu Sakhr was alone in narrating it."
"8909","Miqdam narrated to us: Said ibn Ufayr told us: Sahl Abu Hariz, freedman of al-Mughirah, told us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turbaned Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, letting down four fingers for him, and said: When I ascended to heaven, I saw most of the angels wearing turbans. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Sahl Abu Hariz; Said ibn Ufayr was alone in narrating it."
"8910","Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Qaysi told us: Abdullah ibn Wahb told us: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to him that she said: O Messenger of Allah, has there come upon you a day harder than the day of Uhud? He said: I met from your people, and the hardest I met from them was the day of al-Aqabah, when I offered myself to Ibn Abd Yalil ibn Abd Kulal, and he did not answer me to what I wanted. So I went on, grieved, on my face, and did not wake except while at Qarn al-Thaalib. I raised my head, and there was a cloud shading me; I looked and saw Jibril in it. He called me and said: Allah has heard the saying of your people and what they answered you, and He has sent to you the angel of the mountains that you may command him as you wish concerning them. The angel of the mountains called me, greeted me, then said: O Muhammad, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has heard the saying of your people; I am the angel of the mountains, and your Lord has sent me to you that you command me with your order as you wish; if you wish that I crush them with the two Akhshabs, I will do it. I said: Rather I hope that Allah brings forth from their loins those who worship Allah and associate nothing with Him. No one narrated this hadith from al-Zuhri except Yunus; Ibn Wahb was alone in narrating it."
"8911","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Umar ibn Qays told us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Qasim, from Muhammad, from Aishah, who said: Umm Sulaym, mother of Anas ibn Malik, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what if one of us finds in sleep what a man finds, until she finds wetness? He said: When one of you finds that, let her bathe. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Abi Mulaykah except Umar ibn Qays."
"8912","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Abdullah ibn Umar told us, from Sad ibn Said, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The nearness of time is that the year be like the month, the month like the week, the week like the day, the day like the hour, and the hour like the burning of fire; and one of you will sleep while his term is before his eyes. No one narrated this hadith from Sad ibn Said except Abdullah ibn Umar."
"8913","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: al-Walid ibn Muslim told us, from Masruq ibn Abi al-Hudhayl: al-Zuhri narrated to me: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah informed me, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he had boycotted his wives, and found him on a bed, resting on a pillow of leather stuffed with fiber. Umar said: I looked at folded hides whose smell had risen, and wept, and said: O Messenger of Allah, you are the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and His chosen one, while Kisra and Qaysar are in gold and silk? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat up straight and said: Are you in doubt, O Ibn al-Khattab? Those are a people whose good things were hastened for them in the life of this world. No one narrated this hadith from Masruq except al-Walid."
"8914","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: al-Walid ibn Muslim told us, from al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from Umar ibn al-Khattab who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No child is retaliated upon for a parent. No one narrated this hadith connected from Amr ibn Shuayb except al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah."
"8915","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Hammad ibn Salamah told us, from Habib ibn al-Shahid, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, nephew of Maymunah, from Maymunah bint al-Harith, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me at Sarif, while we were both in halal state, after he returned from Mecca. No one narrated this hadith well-mastered from Habib ibn al-Shahid except Hammad ibn Salamah, and none mentioned in the text of this hadith: after he returned from Mecca, except Hammad ibn Salamah."
"8916","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us: Ibn Lahiah told us: Abu al-Zubayr told us, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade trimming the mustache. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Zubayr except Ibn Lahiah; Abu al-Aswad was alone in narrating it."
"8917","Miqdam narrated to us: Habib, secretary of Malik, told us: Shibl ibn Abbad told us, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurairah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you touches his penis with his hand, let him perform ablution. No one narrated this hadith from Shibl except Habib, secretary of Malik."
"8918","Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah told us: Kamil Abu al-Ala told us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurairah who said: When Bilal wanted to call the iqamah for prayer, he would say: Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; the prayer, may Allah have mercy on you. No one narrated this hadith from Kamil except Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah."
"8919","Miqdam narrated to us: Abd al-Malik ibn Maslamah told us: Dawud ibn Ata told us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a sheet dyed with wars; he would wear it in his house, go round his wives in it, and pray in it. No one narrated this hadith from Hisham ibn Urwah except Dawud ibn Ata; Abd al-Malik ibn Maslamah was alone in narrating it."
"8920","Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Yahya ibn Zakariyya told us: Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Nadr who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: I never saw anyone whose prayer more resembled the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than this boy, meaning Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Nadr except Ibn Lahiah."
"8921","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Abu al-Rabi al-Samman told us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir who said: A clan of the Ansar called Banu Salimah, the kin of Muadh ibn Jabal, came, and he said: O Banu Salimah, who is your chief? They said: Jadd ibn Qays, though we consider him miserly. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And what disease is more diseased than miserliness? No one narrated this hadith from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir, except Abu al-Rabi."
"8922","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Ibn Lahiah told us: Jafar ibn Rabiah told us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah who said: On the day of the Conquest, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poured out wine, broke its jars, and forbade selling it and selling idols. No one narrated this hadith from Jafar ibn Rabiah except Ibn Lahiah."
"8923","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Hammad ibn Salamah told us, from Qays ibn Sad, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived when four of Dhu al-Hijjah had passed; when he circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, he said: Make it Umrah, except one who has the sacrificial animal with him. When it was the day of al-Tarwiyah, they circumambulated the House but did not go between al-Safa and al-Marwah. No one narrated this hadith from Qays ibn Sad except Hammad ibn Salamah."
"8924","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Sharik told us, from al-Ashath, from al-Hasan, from Jabir; it was said to him: Did he mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. He said: We do not inherit the People of the Book nor do they inherit us, except that a man inherits his slave or his slave-girl; and we marry their women, but they do not marry our women. No one narrated this hadith from Ashath ibn Sawwar except Sharik."
"8925","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah told us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The prophets were shown to me, and I most resemble Ibrahim (peace be upon him). No one narrated this hadith from Ayyub except Ibn Abi Zaidah."
"8926","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Ibn Lahiah told us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood praying, so I stood on his left; he moved me to his right. Then Jabbar ibn Sakhr came and stood on his left; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stepped forward, and we stood behind him. No one narrated this hadith from Khalid ibn Yazid except Ibn Lahiah."
"8927","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Hammad ibn Salamah told us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Jurayj, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stoned the Jamrah on the Day of Sacrifice in the forenoon, and stoned the Jamarat on the days of al-Tashriq after the sun had passed its zenith. Hammad ibn Salamah narrated no supported (musnad) hadith from Ibn Jurayj, from Abi al-Zubayr, other than this."
"8928","Miqdam narrated to us: Abd al-Malik ibn Maslamah al-Umawi told us: Ibrahim ibn Abi Bakr ibn al-Munkadir told us: my uncle Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Jibril, from Allah, the Exalted, who said: This religion I have chosen for Myself, and nothing suits it except generosity and good character; so honor it with them as long as you accompany it. This hadith is not narrated from Jabir except with this chain; Abd al-Malik ibn Maslamah was alone in narrating it."
"8929","Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us: Abdullah ibn Salim al-Himsi told us: Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani told us, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, that he saw a plowshare and something of farming tools pass by him, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: This does not enter the house of a people except Allah brings humiliation into it. This hadith is not narrated from Abu Umamah except with this chain; Abdullah ibn Yusuf was alone in narrating it."
"8930","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Said ibn al-Saib al-Taifi told us: my father narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Amir al-Suwai, that while they were circumambulating the Taghiyah they heard a speaker saying: {And if he had fabricated some sayings against Us, We would have seized him by the right, then cut from him the aorta}. We were startled at that and said: What is this speech we do not know? We looked, and there was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) going along."
"8931","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Said ibn al-Saib told us, from Abi al-Khurayf Ubayd ibn Sad al-Suwai, from Yazid ibn Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came with a group, until he stood at al-Qarn below al-Murayta, raising his hands, facing the Qiblah, supplicating. These two hadiths are not narrated from Yazid ibn Amir except with this chain; Said ibn al-Saib was alone in narrating them."
"8932","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Said ibn Yazid told us, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Nafi, from Said ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Silk is permitted for the females of my ummah, and forbidden for its males. No one narrated this hadith from Said ibn Yazid except Asad ibn Musa."
"8933","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Hammad ibn Salamah told us, from Hisham al-Dastuwai, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Musa, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Good and evil will be set up for people on the Day of Resurrection; as for good, it promises its people Paradise and gives them glad tidings; and as for evil, it says to its people: To me, to me, but they can do nothing but cling to it. No one narrated this hadith from Hammad ibn Salamah except Asad ibn Musa."
"8934","Miqdam narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Adi told us: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi told us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, from Abu Dharr who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever meets Allah without having equated anything with Him in this world, then though he has sins like the mountains, Allah will forgive him. No one narrated this hadith from Umarah except al-Darawardi, and it is not narrated from Abu Dharr except with this chain."
"8935","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Warqa ibn Umar told us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Zad han and Maysarah, from Ali, that he drank standing; it was said to him: Do you drink standing? He said: That I do; I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing it: if I drink standing, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drinking standing; and if I drink sitting, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drinking sitting. No one narrated this hadith from Warqa except Asad ibn Musa."
"8936","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Mubarak ibn Fadalah told us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah al-Salmani, who narrated to him that he witnessed Ali when he fought the people of al-Nahrawan, while he was standing on his mule, saying: Look among them for the defective-armed, or the crushed-armed, or the small-armed? They looked and did not find him. Ali said: Turn them over. They turned them over and brought out a man from a dark ditch, tall, with on his upper arm the like of a woman's breast, upon it black hairs. I heard Ali saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; were it not that you would rely, I would inform you of what Allah promised those who fought them upon the tongue of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When I heard him mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I drew near to him until I took the bridle of his mule while he was standing, and said: O Commander of the Believers, did you hear this from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Kabah. No one narrated this hadith from Mubarak ibn Fadalah except Asad ibn Musa, and none narrated it from Yunus ibn Ubayd except Mubarak ibn Fadalah and Abdullah ibn Isa al-Khazzaz."
"8937","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Ali ibn Thabit al-Jazari told us, from al-Walid ibn Amr ibn Saj, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, from his father who said: I ate tharid with fatty meat, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was belching. He said: Restrain your belching, O Abu Juhayfah; for the most sated of people in this world will be the longest hungry on the Day of Resurrection. Abu Juhayfah never ate his fill until he left this world; when he ate lunch he would not eat dinner, and when he ate dinner he would not eat lunch. No one narrated this hadith from al-Walid ibn Amr ibn Saj except Ali ibn Thabit al-Jazari."
"8938","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Said ibn Salim al-Qaddah told us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Shurahbil ibn Sad, that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Boycotting is not lawful beyond three days; if they meet and one of them greets the other and the other returns the greeting, they share in the reward; and if the other refuses to return the greeting, this one is free of sin and the other bears it. And I feared that if they die while boycotting one another, they will not meet in Paradise. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj except Said ibn Salim; Asad ibn Musa was alone in narrating it."
"8939","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Hatim ibn Ismail told us, from Hamzah ibn Abi Muhammad, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah said: I created a creation whose tongues are sweeter than honey, while their hearts are more bitter than aloes; until I swore I shall send upon them a trial leaving the forbearing among them perplexed; by Me they are deceived, and against Me they dare. No one narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Dinar except Hamzah; Hatim ibn Ismail was alone in narrating it."
"8940","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Said ibn Salim told us, from Ibn Jurayj: Abu Khalid narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Abi Said: Hafsah bint Umar narrated to me, she said: One day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had put his garment aside from his thigh; Abu Bakr came asking permission, and he gave him permission while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in his state. Then Umar came and asked permission, and he gave him permission while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in his state. Then Uthman came, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his garment and covered himself with it. They talked, then left. I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr and Umar and some of your Companions came while you were in your state, but when Uthman came you covered yourself with your garment? He said: Shall I not be shy of one whom the angels are shy of? No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj except Said ibn Salim al-Qaddah."
"8941","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Sharik told us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amir al-Shabi, from Urwah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, from his father, that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he relieved himself, then came, and said: Is there any purifying water? I brought him water; he washed his hands and face, then went to wash his arms, but the jubbah was tight for him, and it was a jubbah of the jubbahs of Rome; so he thrust his arms out from under the jubbah, and washed his arms. I bent toward the khuffs, and he said: Leave the khuffs, for I put the feet into the khuffs while they were pure. So he wiped over the khuffs. No one narrated this hadith from Abu Ishaq except Sharik, and none narrated it from Sharik except Asad ibn Musa and Yahya ibn Adam."
"8942","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Abu al-Rabi al-Samman told us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah loves the believing craftsman. No one narrated this hadith from Salim except Asim ibn Ubaydullah; Abu al-Rabi al-Samman was alone in narrating it, and it is not narrated from Ibn Umar except with this chain."
"8943","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Hammad ibn Salamah told us, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah, from Abu Hurairah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An angel at one of the gates of heaven says: Whoever lends today will be recompensed tomorrow; and an angel at another gate says: O Allah, give the spender of wealth a replacement, and give the withholder of wealth ruin. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah except Ishaq; Hammad ibn Salamah was alone in narrating it."
"8944","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Said ibn Salim told us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Amr ibn Abdullah al-Awdi, from Abu al-Darda who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever mentions a man with what is not in him, to disgrace him with what is not in him, Allah will confine him in the Fire of Hell until he brings the fulfillment of what he said about him. No one narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj except Said ibn Salim."
"8945","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid told us, from al-Dahhak ibn Humrah, from Hittan ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi, from Abu al-Darda who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Zakat is the bridge of Islam. This hadith is not narrated from Abu al-Darda except with this chain; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid was alone in narrating it."
"8946","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us: Abd al-Wahid al-Bazzar told us: Nahshal ibn Said told us, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim who said: I met with Tawus al-Yamani, Amr ibn Dinar al-Makki, Makhul al-Shami, and al-Hasan al-Basri in Masjid al-Khayf. We discussed qadar until our voices rose and our clamor increased. Tawus stood and said: Be silent; I inform you what I heard Abu al-Darda reporting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has obligated duties upon you, so do not waste them; He set limits for you, so do not transgress them; He forbade you things, so do not violate them; and He was silent about things, not from forgetfulness, so do not burden yourselves with them, as mercy from your Lord, so accept it. All matters are in the Hand of Allah; from Allah is their source, and to Him is their return; servants have in them neither delegation nor will. The people all stood, pleased with what Tawus said. This hadith is not narrated from Abu al-Darda except with this chain; Asad ibn Musa was alone in narrating it."
"8947","Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Abdullah ibn Wahb told us: Khalid ibn Humayd informed me, from Salim ibn Shurayh al-Ansari, from Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Ansari, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah intends humiliation for a servant, He makes him spend his wealth on buildings. This hadith is not narrated from Ibn Bishr al-Ansari except with this chain; Ibn Wahb was alone in narrating it."
"8948","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Rawh ibn Musafir told us, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Waraqah al-Ansari who said: I said: O Muhammad, how does he come to you, meaning Jibril (peace be upon him)? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He comes to me from heaven, his two wings pearl, and the insides of his feet green. No one narrated this hadith from al-Amash except Rawh ibn Musafir."
"8949","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Ismail ibn Ayyash told us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Maryam, from Zayd ibn Artah, from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: No servant says: There is no god but Allah, and Allah is Greatest, except Allah frees a quarter of him from the Fire; if he says it twice, Allah frees half of him from the Fire; if he says it three times, Allah frees three-quarters of him from the Fire; if he says it four times, Allah frees him from the Fire. This hadith is not narrated from Abu al-Darda except with this chain; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam was alone in narrating it."
"8950","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa told us: Yusuf ibn Ziyad told us, from Abd al-Munim ibn Idris, from his father Idris, from his grandfather Wahb ibn Munabbih, from Abu Hurairah, that a man of the Jews came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Aba al-Qasim, has Allah veiled Himself from His creation with anything other than the heavens and the earth? He said: Yes, between Him and the angels around the Throne are seventy veils of fire, seventy veils of light, seventy veils of darkness, seventy veils of brocade raffles, seventy veils of sundus raffles, seventy veils of white pearl, seventy veils of red pearl, seventy veils of yellow pearl, seventy veils of green pearl, seventy veils of radiance derived from fire and light, seventy veils of snow, seventy veils of water, seventy veils of cloud, seventy veils of hail, and seventy veils of the majesty of Allah which cannot be described. He said: Tell me of the angel of Allah nearest to Him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have I told you the truth, O Jew? He said: Yes. He said: The angel nearest to Him is Israfil, then Jibril, then Mikail, then the Angel of Death (peace be upon them all). This hadith is not narrated from Abu Hurairah except with this chain; Asad was alone in narrating it."
"8951","Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Salih told us: Ibn Lahiah narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had, from Musa ibn Sarjis, from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever eats with his left hand, a devil eats with him; and whoever drinks with his left hand, a devil drinks with him. No one narrated this hadith from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim except Musa ibn Sarjis, and none narrated it from Musa except Ibn al-Had; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it."
"8952","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar told us: Ibn Lahiah told us, from Ayyash ibn Abbas, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurairah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The Hour will not come until the people of the mudy overcome their mudys, the people of the qafiz their qafizes, the people of the irdabb their irdabbs, the people of the dinar their dinars, the people of the dirham their dirhams, and people return to their lands. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyash ibn Abbas except Ibn Lahiah; Abu al-Aswad was alone in narrating it."
"8953","Miqdam narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bukayr told us: Ibn Lahiah told us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Salih ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurairah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No owner of gold or silver who does not pay its due except it is made plates of fire, then heated upon it in the Fire of Hell, and his forehead and back are branded with it on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among people, and he sees his path either to Paradise or to the Fire; and no owner of camels who does not pay its due, and of its due is milking it on the day of watering, except they are brought on the Day of Resurrection, none of their young missing, and he is laid out for them on a flat plain, they trample him with their hooves and bite him with their mouths; whenever the last of them passes him, the first returns upon him, on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among people; and no owner of cows or sheep who does not pay its due except they are brought on the Day of Resurrection, then he is laid out for them on a flat plain with no hornless nor broken-horned among them, they trample him with their hooves and gore him with their horns, on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years; whenever the last passes him, the first returns upon him, until judgment is passed among people, and he sees his path either to Paradise or to the Fire. No one narrated this hadith from Salih ibn Abi Salih except Bukayr ibn Abdullah; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it."
"8954","Miqdam narrated to us: Khalid ibn Nizar told us: Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah told us, from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi, from Jarir ibn Abdullah al-Bajali, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, give charity and draw near to Allah, three times; none brought anything, until it was seen in the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he said: O people, give charity and draw near to Allah. An Ansari man stood and brought a piece of bracelet; he took from him. When people saw that, they followed in charity, and the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brightened. Then he said: Whoever institutes a good practice in Islam has its reward and the like of the reward of whoever acts by it until the Day of Resurrection, nothing diminished from their rewards; and whoever institutes an evil practice has its burden and the burden of whoever acts by it until the Day of Resurrection, nothing diminished from their burdens. No one narrated this hadith from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi except Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah; Khalid ibn Nizar was alone in narrating it."
"8955","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Tinnisi told us, Umar ibn al-Mughirah told us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Causing harm in the will is among the major sins. None raised this hadith from Dawud ibn Abi Hind except Umar ibn al-Mughirah."
"8956","Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us: Abdullah ibn al-Mughirah told us: Hisham ibn Hassan told us, from Aishah bint Arar, from Muadhah al-Adawiyyah, from Aishah, who said: Command your husbands to wash the trace of stool and urine; for I am shy to command them that; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do it. No one narrated this hadith from Aishah bint Arar except Hisham ibn Hassan."
"8957","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered his women and children on the night of Muzdalifah, and I was among them, so they departed at dawn until we prayed Fajr at Mina. No one narrated this hadith from Khalid ibn Yazid except Ibn Lahiah."
"8958","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from al-Maqburi, from Awn ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Abu Hurairah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer at midday. No one narrated this hadith from Awn ibn Abdullah except al-Maqburi, nor from al-Maqburi except Yazid ibn Abi Habib; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it."
"8959","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar told us, he said: Ibn Lahiah told us, from Ayyash ibn Abbas, from Shuyaym ibn Baytan, from Busr ibn Abi Artah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Hands are not cut in military expeditions. This hadith is not narrated from Busr ibn Abi Artah except with this chain; Ayyash ibn Abbas was alone in narrating it."
"8960","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Ayyash ibn Abbas, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Amrah, from Aishah, that she heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The hand is not cut except for what reaches a quarter dinar. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyash ibn Abbas except Ibn Lahiah."
"8961","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A servant is with whom he loves. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Zubayr except Ibn Lahiah."
"8962","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwah, from Aishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died only of pleurisy. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Aswad except Ibn Lahiah."
"8963","Miqdam narrated to us: my uncle Said ibn Isa told us, Mufaddal ibn Fadalah told us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Marwan ibn Abd al-Rahman, nephew of Rafi ibn Khadij, who said: Rafi ibn Khadij came to us saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a matter that was easy for you; he forbade muhaqalah and muzabanah, and forbade renting land. Ibn al-Had said: And Ibrahim ibn Rafi ibn Khadij narrated to me from his father likewise. No one narrated this hadith from Marwan ibn Abd al-Rahman and Ibrahim ibn Rafi except Yazid ibn al-Had."
"8964","Miqdam narrated to us: my uncle Said ibn Isa told us, Mufaddal ibn Fadalah told us, Yahya ibn Ayyub told us, from Yunus and Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Abi Salamah, from Abu Hurairah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the reciter says amin, say amin; whoever's amin coincides with the amin of the angels, his past sins are forgiven. No one narrated this hadith joined from Yunus and Uqayl, from al-Zuhri, from Said and Abi Salamah, except Yahya ibn Ayyub; and none from Yahya except Mufaddal ibn Fadalah; Said ibn Isa was alone in narrating it."
"8965","Miqdam narrated to us: Said ibn Isa told us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ashras told us, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from his brother Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All the roads of Mecca are a place of sacrifice. No one narrated this hadith from Ubaydullah ibn Umar except his brother Abdullah, and none narrated it from Abdullah except Abd al-Rahman ibn Ashras, and none from Abd al-Rahman except Said ibn Isa. And from Abdullah ibn Nafi: Abdullah ibn Abi Salih."
"8966","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar told us, Bakr ibn Mudar told us, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Abi al-Aswad, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you enters the mosque, let him not sit until he prays two rakahs. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Aswad except Jafar ibn Rabiah, and none from Jafar except Bakr ibn Mudar; Abu al-Aswad was alone in narrating it."
"8967","Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Tinnisi told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray al-Atamah, then pray in the mosque before returning to his house seven rakahs, greeting in the four in every two and praying witr with three, testifying in the first two of the witr as his testimony in the taslim, and praying witr with the Muawwidhat; when he returned to his house he would bow two rakahs and sleep. When he woke from his sleep, he would say: Praise be to Allah who made me sleep in health and woke me in health. Then he would raise his head to the sky and reflect, then say: {Our Lord, You did not create this in vain; glory be to You, protect us from the punishment of the Fire}. He would recite until reaching: {You do not break the promise}. Then he would perform ablution, then stand and pray two rakahs, lengthening the recitation, bowing, and prostration in them, and increasing supplication in them, until I would sleep and wake. Then he would leave, lie down and doze, then toss, then speak with the like of what he spoke the first time. Then he would stand and bow two rakahs longer than the first two, being in them more humble and seeking forgiveness, until I would say: Will he leave? That would continue to the end of the night. Then he would leave and doze a little, and I would say: Did he doze or not? Until the muadhdhin came to him, and he would say the like of what he said the first time. Then he would sit, call for the siwak, clean his teeth, perform ablution, then bow two light rakahs, then go out to prayer. This was his prayer, thirteen rakahs. No one narrated this hadith from Ayyash ibn Abbas except Ibn Lahiah."
"8968","Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us, al-Haytham ibn Humayd told us, al-Numan ibn al-Mundhir told us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah who said: I asked Aishah: Was it permitted for women to pray on riding animals? She said: They were not permitted that, neither in hardship nor in ease. No one narrated this hadith from al-Numan ibn al-Mundhir except al-Haytham ibn Humayd and Yahya ibn Hamzah."
"8969","Miqdam narrated to us: Asad told us, Adi ibn al-Fadl told us, from Humayd, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Three things: whoever guards them is truly my friend, and whoever wastes them is truly my enemy: prayer, fasting, and janabah. No one narrated this hadith from Humayd except Adi ibn al-Fadl; Asad ibn Musa was alone in narrating it."
"8970","Miqdam narrated to us: Ali ibn Mabad al-Raqqi told us, Wahb ibn Rashid told us, Malik ibn Dinar told us, from Khilas ibn Amr, from Abu al-Darda, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah says: I am Allah, there is no god but Me, Owner of kings and King of kings; the hearts of kings are in My Hand. When servants obey Me, I turn the hearts of their kings toward them with kindness and mercy; and when servants disobey Me, I turn their hearts against them with anger and vengeance, so they make them taste evil punishment. Do not busy yourselves with supplicating against kings, but busy yourselves with remembrance and humble supplication to Me; I suffice you against your kings."
"8971","Miqdam narrated to us: Ali ibn Mabad al-Raqqi told us, Wahb ibn Rashid told us, from Malik ibn Dinar, from Khilas ibn Amr, from Abu Hurairah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: One who takes back his gift is like the dog eating until sated, then vomiting, then returning to its vomit. No one narrated these two hadiths from Malik ibn Dinar except Wahb ibn Rashid."
"8972","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abu al-Aswad Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that people were shown Laylat al-Qadr in the last seven of Ramadan; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked, and it was said: People saw it in the last seven; so seek it in the last seven. No one narrated this hadith from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim except Abu al-Aswad, and none narrated it from Abi al-Aswad except Ibn Lahiah; Abu al-Aswad was alone in narrating it."
"8973","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever frees a share in a slave, it is assessed upon him who freed, from his wealth; and if he does not have, it is assessed the just value, then he works to earn without hardship. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Aswad except Ibn Lahiah; Abu al-Aswad was alone in narrating it."
"8974","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Urwah and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about a man who sees something in his dream but sees no wetness, and sees wetness but sees nothing? He said: When one of you finds wetness and saw nothing, let him bathe; and when he saw something but saw no wetness, let him not bathe. No one narrated this hadith from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad except Ubaydullah ibn Umar and Abu al-Aswad; Ubaydullah ibn Umar's brother Abdullah ibn Umar was alone in narrating it from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, and Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it from Abi al-Aswad."
"8975","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, that Barirah was under a slave; when she was freed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: You own yourself; if you wish, stay with your husband, and if you wish, leave him, so long as he has not touched you. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Aswad except Ibn Lahiah."
"8976","Miqdam narrated to us: Ali ibn Mabad told us, Abdullah ibn Umar told us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray while she was in front of him, between the Prophet of Allah and the Qiblah. No one narrated this hadith from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah except Abdullah ibn Umar and Abu Abd al-Rahim Khalid ibn Yazid."
"8977","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raced trained horses; they were sent from al-Hafya, and their limit was Thaniyyat al-Wada; and he raced untrained horses, they were sent, and their limit was the mosque of Banu Zurayq. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Aswad except Ibn Lahiah; Abu al-Aswad was alone in narrating it. He said:"
"8978","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes full possession of it. No one narrated this hadith except Abu al-Aswad; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it."
"8979","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying on the camel wherever it turned with him. No one narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar except Abu al-Aswad; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it."
"8980","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, who said: We used to give Zakat al-Fitr in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two mudds of wheat, by the mudd with which they sustained themselves. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Aswad except Ibn Lahiah."
"8981","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abu Hurairah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade making nabidh in muzaffat, dubba, and naqir. No one narrated this hadith from Urwah except Abu al-Aswad; Ibn Lahiah was alone in narrating it."
"8982","Miqdam narrated to us: Abu al-Aswad told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Aswad, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Sad ibn Abi Waqqas a young goat and ordered him to sacrifice it. No one narrated this hadith from Abi al-Aswad except Ibn Lahiah."
"8983","Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Umar ibn al-Khattab at the time of the Conquest to come to the House while he was at al-Batha, to efface every image in it; and he did not enter it until every image in it was effaced."
"8984","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Abi al-Zubayr, who said: I heard Jabir saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote before he died to Kisra, Qaysar, al-Najashi, and every stubborn tyrant."
"8985","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Abu Ayyash who said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Islam began strange and will return strange as it began, so glad tidings to the strangers. They said: Who are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Those who set right when people are corrupt."
"8986","And with his chain: from Jabir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every soul is gathered upon its desire; whoever desired disbelief is with disbelief, and his deed benefits him nothing."
"8987","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us, Bakr ibn Mudar told us, Abu Zarah Amr ibn Jabir al-Hadrami told us, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever fasts Ramadan and six of Shawwal, it is for him the fast of a year. Or: There is written for him the fast of a year."
"8988","And with it: from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning the plague: One fleeing from it is like one fleeing on the day of battle, and whoever is patient in it has the reward of a martyr."
"8989","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, Bakr ibn Sawadah told us, from Duwayd ibn Nafi, from Abu Musa, that Jabir ibn Abdullah narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with them the fear prayer on the day of Muharib, one rakah and two prostrations with each group."
"8990","Al-Miqdam narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Yusuf told us, Ibn Lahiah told us, from Utbah ibn Abi Hakim, from Husayn ibn Harmalah, from Abu Musabbih, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Horses have good and blessing tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection, and their people are aided upon them; put necklaces on them and do not put bowstrings as necklaces on them."
"8991","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to me, Abdullah ibn Muslim ibn Hurmuz narrated to us, from Abdurrahman ibn Sabit, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: a man said, 'How are you this morning, O Messenger of Allah?' So he said, 'Well, better than a man who did not wake up fasting, nor visit a sick person, nor follow a funeral.'"
"8992","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'Do not enter upon women whose husbands are absent, for Satan flows through man like the flow of blood.' They said, 'And from you, O Messenger of Allah?' He said, 'And from me, but Allah aided me against him so he submitted.'"
"8993","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Yazid, from Jundub ibn Abdullah al-Adwani, from Sufyan ibn Awf al-Qari, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the sun rose, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'On the Day of Resurrection there will come to Allah a people whose light is like the sun.' They said, 'Are we they, O Messenger of Allah?' He said, 'No, but you have much good; rather they are the poor emigrants who are gathered from the ends of the earth.'"
"8994","And with his chain: from Abdullah ibn Amr who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'Blessedness (Tuba) is for the strangers.' We said, 'And who are the strangers?' He said, 'A few righteous people among many evil people; those who disobey them are more than those who obey them.'"
"8995","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'No Muslim plants a plant nor sows a crop from which a man, or a bird, or anything eats, except that it is a reward for him.'"
"8996","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Yazid, from Salamah ibn Aksum al-Sadafi, from al-Qasim ibn al-Barahi who said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As narrating that two litigants disputed before Amr ibn al-As and he judged between them, and the one judged against became angry and came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'When the judge judges and strives and is correct, he has ten rewards, and when he strives and errs, he has two rewards.'"
"8997","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, al-Awzai narrated to us, Rabiah ibn Yazid narrated to me, Abdullah ibn al-Daylami narrated to me: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, 'Sulayman ibn Dawud asked Allah for three things and He gave him two, and I hope He has given him the third: he asked Allah for judgment that coincides with His judgment and He gave it to him; he asked for a kingdom that befits no one after him and He gave it to him; and he asked that any man who leaves his house intending only prayer in this mosque - meaning the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis - should emerge from his sins like the day his mother bore him.'"
"8998","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'What I fear most for you is what Allah brings forth for you of the blessings of the earth.' It was said, 'What are the blessings of the earth?' He said, 'The splendor of the world.' Then a man said, 'Does good bring evil?' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent until we thought revelation would descend upon him, then he began wiping sweat from his forehead, then said, 'Where is the questioner: does good bring evil?' The man said, 'Here I am.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'Good brings only good' - three times - 'and this wealth is lush and sweet; everything the spring grows that kills with bloat or nearly, except the eater of greens: it eats until when its flanks swell it faces the sun, then chews the cud, drops dung, urinates, then returns and eats. This wealth is lush and sweet: whoever takes it rightfully and places it rightfully, what an excellent aid it is; and whoever takes it wrongfully is like one who eats and is never satiated.'"
"8999","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'Blessing is with your elders.'"
"9000","Al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Kamil Abu al-Ala narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Maymunah while he was in ihram. [Tabarani said:] No one narrated this hadith from Kamil except Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah and Khalid ibn Abdurrahman al-Makhzumi."
